WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.875
- Let's start the board
meeting if we might.

00:00:06.875 --> 00:00:08.049
And thank you everybody

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:10.680
for joining the public board
meeting of NHS England.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.660
Can I just make a request of colleagues

00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:16.290
that the first time each of you speaks

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.426
for the purpose of the webcast,

00:00:18.426 --> 00:00:20.543
would you remember to
introduce yourselves?

00:00:21.541 --> 00:00:23.981
So Richard Meddings, Chair, NHS England,

00:00:23.981 --> 00:00:26.190
apologies for absence.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.711
We have some apologies for absence.

00:00:28.711 --> 00:00:32.319
A number of the colleagues
who were with us this morning

00:00:32.319 --> 00:00:33.501
in the private board

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:35.387
unfortunately can't be
with us this afternoon,

00:00:35.387 --> 00:00:37.200
but we've got apologies
from Suresh Viswanathan,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.580
Simon Wessely, Mark Bailey,
Mark Walport, Chris Hopson,

00:00:42.773 --> 00:00:43.853
and Tanuj Kapilashrami.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:47.559
Any declarations of interest,

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:48.847
new declarations of interest

00:00:48.847 --> 00:00:50.497
that board members need to share?

00:00:52.731 --> 00:00:55.750
Okay, thank you, and can
I approve the minutes

00:00:56.677 --> 00:00:59.583
of the meeting held on the 3rd of October?

00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:00.783
Can we sign this?

00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:01.972
- Yes.

00:01:01.972 --> 00:01:02.805
- Thank you very much.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.720
Let me just share some activities

00:01:08.694 --> 00:01:10.067
that I've been undertaking

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:10.980
but also some other reflections

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.703
attaching to this board meeting.

00:01:17.648 --> 00:01:20.033
I think what I should
just say to start with,

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:23.475
if you'll forgive the egotistical nature

00:01:23.475 --> 00:01:25.785
of the comment is since we
last met you, as you all know,

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:30.785
I am now due to step
down at the end of March

00:01:31.047 --> 00:01:32.993
and I'd just like to make

00:01:32.993 --> 00:01:34.389
some personal comments around that.

00:01:34.389 --> 00:01:38.677
To say it has been an honour
actually to act as chair

00:01:38.677 --> 00:01:41.280
of this organisation is an understatement.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.452
And before starting this
role, I was aware certainly

00:01:45.452 --> 00:01:48.780
of the unique place that the
NHS has in the nation's heart.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.570
And I was also aware that it also faced

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.450
a unique set of challenges and I've seen

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.870
and learned so much in
the past couple of years.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.440
And perhaps if you'll forgive
me, being over simplistic,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.580
perhaps these are sort
of three key reflections.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.241
And the first actually, and it's the quote

00:02:08.241 --> 00:02:09.934
and you've heard me say
it a couple of times

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:11.135
in the last couple of weeks actually,

00:02:11.135 --> 00:02:11.970
and I apologise for the quote,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.150
but it's the quote from
the Gladiator first film,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.280
not this Gladiator second film,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.280
which is to the effect that the NHS deals

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.747
with whatever comes through those gates.

00:02:22.747 --> 00:02:24.633
And there is no off switch.

00:02:26.158 --> 00:02:28.509
We don't get to choose our markets.

00:02:28.509 --> 00:02:32.700
The NHS treats everyone to
the best of its ability.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.820
And actually that is no mean
feat given the record levels

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:40.080
of demand from a growing
and ageing population.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.580
And plus also the structure

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.120
where the NHS operates
against a self-created

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.543
organisational boundary with social care,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.810
which itself is also
significantly under invested

00:02:53.667 --> 00:02:56.163
and facing rising acute pressures.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.013
It's a real, real challenge.

00:03:01.149 --> 00:03:02.249
The second reflection,

00:03:03.289 --> 00:03:06.897
and as our former board
colleague, Lord Darzi made clear

00:03:06.897 --> 00:03:10.246
in his review, we are
dealing with growing demand

00:03:10.246 --> 00:03:13.893
and that's in spite of chronic
capital under investment,

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.323
which is constraining capacity.

00:03:16.179 --> 00:03:18.150
So we have 1/3 the
hospital beds of Germany

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.450
and half those of a number
of other European countries

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.333
scaled to population size.

00:03:25.998 --> 00:03:30.699
And so while the NHS is today
providing absolute record

00:03:30.699 --> 00:03:31.532
levels of healthcare,

00:03:31.532 --> 00:03:34.355
more than we have ever provided before,

00:03:34.355 --> 00:03:35.760
which is actually just a testament

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.165
to the sheer commitment of our workforce,

00:03:39.165 --> 00:03:41.913
our frontline workforce,
and our broader workforce.

00:03:42.831 --> 00:03:45.510
My third observation is
that unless we invest

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.970
but also continue to reform,

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.613
more alone will never be enough.

00:03:52.163 --> 00:03:54.896
And then that said, what
gives me optimism actually

00:03:54.896 --> 00:03:58.170
for the NHS is the sheer
scale of innovation

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.300
and commitment that I've witnessed

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.150
across the NHS in my time here.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.461
So sometimes a bit simplistically,

00:04:05.461 --> 00:04:08.589
I could say heuristic judgement ,

00:04:08.589 --> 00:04:09.789
but that's a bit pompous,

00:04:09.789 --> 00:04:12.060
but put somewhat
simplistically, twin tracks.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.210
The twin tracks are to
continue to innovate

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:19.980
and at the same time to
invest in fixing the basics.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.780
And if I think about the innovation,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.393
and I'm often asked about innovation,

00:04:26.213 --> 00:04:29.472
the role of innovation is remarkable

00:04:29.472 --> 00:04:30.681
from ambient documentation

00:04:30.681 --> 00:04:33.390
to save clinicians time, the NHS app,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:38.390
the FDP, EPR, the
rollout of new medicines,

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:42.183
new treatments, robotics,
nanorobotics, med tech,

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.990
cancer vaccinations this
year, liquid biopsies.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.470
You hear this array of innovation

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.030
and actually it is
something that acts for me

00:04:52.983 --> 00:04:54.383
as a foundation of optimism.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.590
So I just wanted to say
that by way of intro

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:59.031
because since the last
board we've announced

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:00.980
that I'm going to be stepping down,

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:02.430
as I say at the end of March.

00:05:05.917 --> 00:05:08.393
Couple things if I might by
way of housekeeping again,

00:05:08.393 --> 00:05:10.543
we talked about this morning, the advert

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:13.013
for my replacement has not yet gone out.

00:05:13.013 --> 00:05:15.542
We think it is going
to be imminent I think,

00:05:15.542 --> 00:05:16.741
which will be good

00:05:16.741 --> 00:05:17.790
and that should hopefully
give enough time,

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:22.653
should give enough time,
sorry for my successor

00:05:22.653 --> 00:05:24.933
to come into role in good time.

00:05:26.145 --> 00:05:26.978
I would share though that,

00:05:26.978 --> 00:05:30.270
and I know the Secretary of
State is keen to see this,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.134
but again we need to
get this process going.

00:05:33.134 --> 00:05:35.560
Not us, sorry, the public
sector appointments process

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.950
needs to get going 'cause
we need to find three

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.112
or four new non-executive directors.

00:05:42.112 --> 00:05:44.770
The board knows of course
that Sir David Behan

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:49.320
and and Dame Helen Stokes
Lampard left the board

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.510
a couple of months or so ago.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.237
And it's not only me who's stepping down

00:05:54.237 --> 00:05:55.426
at the end of March,

00:05:55.426 --> 00:05:57.300
but clearly both the deputy chairs.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.710
So Wol Kalada and Andrew Morris

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:03.424
are also stepping down, so we are in need

00:06:03.424 --> 00:06:06.157
of getting that NED recruitment underway

00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:07.365
so that by the end of March,

00:06:07.365 --> 00:06:10.260
the board has sufficient
continuously high quality

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.749
non-executives not just to
do the work of the board

00:06:13.749 --> 00:06:15.503
but to do the work of
the board committees.

00:06:16.865 --> 00:06:19.449
And if I just then move from
that to the board committees

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:21.969
that we won't go through
them in detail now,

00:06:21.969 --> 00:06:24.180
we did that this morning but actually

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.180
the board committees carry a huge burden

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.980
in terms of more detailed engagement

00:06:30.972 --> 00:06:33.783
on issues as a matter of good governance,

00:06:33.783 --> 00:06:35.733
good, appropriate corporate governance.

00:06:36.897 --> 00:06:38.342
So whether it's a Digital Data

00:06:38.342 --> 00:06:39.175
and Technology Committee,

00:06:39.175 --> 00:06:41.790
which actually was the
replacement committee essentially

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.670
for the NHS Digital board to
make sure we had continuity

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:48.549
of agenda or whether it's
the Workforce Training

00:06:48.549 --> 00:06:50.079
and Education Committee,

00:06:50.079 --> 00:06:52.982
which actually was the same
actually substitute committee

00:06:52.982 --> 00:06:55.509
for the Health Education England Board

00:06:55.509 --> 00:06:57.154
to make sure we had continuity.

00:06:57.154 --> 00:07:00.303
And then clearly we have
the normal board committees

00:07:00.303 --> 00:07:02.220
of Audit and Risk and Quality Committee

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.135
and Reople and Remuneration
and then Noms Committee.

00:07:06.135 --> 00:07:08.490
So there's a significant
amount of work that's required.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:12.603
And we went through that this
morning in terms of the work

00:07:12.603 --> 00:07:14.912
that those committees have
done since the last board.

00:07:14.912 --> 00:07:16.410
But I list them and that
wasn't necessarily all of them

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.032
'cause I think it reveals

00:07:18.032 --> 00:07:20.866
and shows actually why
it's really important

00:07:20.866 --> 00:07:25.866
we do bring into the board
the replacement directors

00:07:26.532 --> 00:07:28.432
for those of us who are stepping down.

00:07:33.134 --> 00:07:35.820
Let me talk a little
bit more about the work

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:36.963
of the board.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:41.313
As a board, we've discussed
the 10 year health plan,

00:07:42.366 --> 00:07:44.130
which we've done sort of
three formal occasions

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.770
so far including actually last night.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.605
So yesterday evening we
had a very good discussion

00:07:49.605 --> 00:07:50.438
with not just Sally Warren

00:07:50.438 --> 00:07:55.041
but a number of the core groups,

00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:56.340
be they the vision groups
or the enabling groups.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.480
So we had a number of co-chairs,

00:07:58.480 --> 00:07:59.992
some of whom actually in this room clearly

00:07:59.992 --> 00:08:02.640
because a number of our NHS
England executive colleagues

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.440
are co-chairs of some of those groups

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.330
but also some externals.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.850
And I think that was
really, really helpful.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.190
I know some of you here have attended

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.430
the public deliberations
and the staff deliberations.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:19.210
So I attended the London
Regional staff engagement

00:08:20.049 --> 00:08:21.180
on the 10 year plan and on Sunday,

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.088
this coming Sunday I'm going to attend

00:08:24.088 --> 00:08:28.787
the London Event, deliberative
event this weekend

00:08:28.787 --> 00:08:29.620
with Minister Gwynne.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.008
And actually I'm quite excited to hear

00:08:32.008 --> 00:08:33.200
what we'll be focusing on.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:34.903
I know Steve, you've
attended them, Sarah-Jane,

00:08:34.903 --> 00:08:35.970
you've attended them,
Amanda, you've attended them

00:08:36.901 --> 00:08:38.387
and others have also, Vin,

00:08:38.387 --> 00:08:39.972
Emily have all attended these events

00:08:39.972 --> 00:08:42.934
and I think they're going to
be really informative for us.

00:08:42.934 --> 00:08:45.720
So there's the 10 year health plan

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.030
that's been launched and is well underway.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.890
And of course since we last met the chance

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:52.748
to set out the government spending plans

00:08:52.748 --> 00:08:55.740
in the autumn budget,
I would just say that,

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.360
and I'm sure you join with me,

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.240
that we welcome the
prioritisation of the NHS

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.135
and particularly around
capital investment.

00:09:04.135 --> 00:09:06.716
It was very helpful and
very supportive of this,

00:09:06.716 --> 00:09:08.320
of the government to give us

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.248
the additional capital investment,

00:09:11.248 --> 00:09:12.614
there have clearly been reports

00:09:12.614 --> 00:09:13.862
of under investment every many years.

00:09:13.862 --> 00:09:17.370
And we do need to start
investing to catch up with that.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:20.670
But suffice it to say finances remain

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:24.860
more than very challenging
and we will need

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:27.070
to retain our focus on maximising return

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:30.870
for every penny of taxpayer investment.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.480
