and also went down to
Cornwall to visit the Cornwall

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.943
and Isles of Scilly ICB.

00:13:18.841 --> 00:13:20.250
And it was good not to see the ICB

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.501
but we had an afternoon engaging

00:13:22.501 --> 00:13:24.857
with a number of voluntary
sector organisations

00:13:24.857 --> 00:13:26.460
who provide huge community support

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:28.440
actually into the provision of health

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:30.813
to the citizens of Cornwall.

00:13:32.351 --> 00:13:36.100
So that's my opening set
of chairman's remarks

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:39.000
and I'm happy to take any comments

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.490
or questions if people have any

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:43.410
and if not I can move on

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:46.893
to Amanda, chief execs update.

00:13:49.415 --> 00:13:50.850
- Great, thank you very
much indeed, Richard.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:54.390
And look, you know, I
don't want to embarrass you

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:55.980
but I think on behalf

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.782
of the executive, I
certainly would just like

00:13:58.782 --> 00:14:00.361
to take this opportunity
to thank you Richard.

00:14:00.361 --> 00:14:01.565
- There are two more boards, Amanda.

00:14:01.565 --> 00:14:04.093
- I know that's, this is very brief.

00:14:04.093 --> 00:14:06.106
The next ones will be much more fulsome

00:14:06.106 --> 00:14:07.441
but I think it's just worth saying,

00:14:07.441 --> 00:14:09.690
I mean you really have
been a staunch advocate

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:12.180
for frontline staff

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.090
and also for everything
the NHS has delivered

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:17.310
in the face of such huge pressure

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:19.800
and increasing demand under your tenure.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:22.770
And you have been also just
passionate about the things

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:26.604
that need to be different to allow the NHS

00:14:26.604 --> 00:14:27.853
to be fit for the future.

00:14:27.853 --> 00:14:29.235
And I know you're not planning

00:14:29.235 --> 00:14:30.829
to take any break in that pace

00:14:30.829 --> 00:14:33.480
and the encouragement of us all

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.230
as we look towards the 10 year plan

00:14:36.085 --> 00:14:37.320
and other opportunities,

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:40.565
we'll really miss you and I'm very glad.

00:14:40.565 --> 00:14:42.503
- We still have those two boards.

00:14:42.503 --> 00:14:43.336
- That you're with us to the end of March.

00:14:43.336 --> 00:14:44.640
So we'll have plenty of opportunity

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:47.430
to reflect between now and then.

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:51.540
I would also just sort
of by way of other news

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:53.730
on our own structure and senior team,

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:55.980
just like to welcome Louise Shepherd

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:59.989
who has now officially started
as the regional director

00:14:59.989 --> 00:15:00.900
for the northwest.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:04.233
It's great to have her now
on board within the team.

00:15:05.515 --> 00:15:08.328
A few things I thought it
might be worth covering,

00:15:08.328 --> 00:15:10.290
really picks up from some of
what Richard has already said.

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:11.640
So obviously since we've last met,

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.190
we had the budget in October

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:16.301
and I think we would all appreciate

00:15:16.301 --> 00:15:19.230
and certainly I know
I've said this publicly,

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.813
the very tough choices
the government has made

00:15:22.813 --> 00:15:24.930
to prioritise funding for the NHS

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:27.780
and in particular we welcome the boost

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:29.940
to capital investment,

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:33.000
much needed as you said
a moment ago, Richard,

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.400
due to the rising backlog maintenance bill

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:37.350
that will also help us continue to invest

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:39.210
in things that improve productivity.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:43.620
So things like upgrading
scanners, other tech

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.480
which we know will be absolutely crucial

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:49.170
in not just now but the
longer term as well.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.253
We'll discuss this further
in the finance update,

00:15:52.253 --> 00:15:55.419
but we are still working through
some of the details of that

00:15:55.419 --> 00:15:58.110
with government and there
will be a first opportunity

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:01.050
to have discussions with ICB leaders

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:05.520
on the shape of 25 26 next week.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.193
What we do know though for certain

00:16:08.193 --> 00:16:11.041
is that the financial position
for next year will be tight

00:16:11.041 --> 00:16:12.120
and will require very frank

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:16.290
and honest conversations as
part of the planning process.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:19.650
There will undoubtedly
be tough decisions ahead,

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:21.870
but we need to ensure every penny

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.600
of taxpayers money is well spent

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.070
and we need to work with systems

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:29.125
to ensure their financial plans are met.

00:16:29.125 --> 00:16:32.100
On a similar note, and thanks
to the efforts of our staff,

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:35.332
it was good to see the
recent analysis from the IFS

00:16:35.332 --> 00:16:37.418
that the NHS has turned a corner

00:16:37.418 --> 00:16:39.240
and is, quote, heading
in the right direction

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:40.710
when it comes to productivity.

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:44.160
Again, we'll talk more
about that in a moment

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:45.720
and something Julian and I talked about,

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:47.070
as you said a moment ago, Richard,

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:48.840
at the public accounts committee.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:52.140
Yeah and I think too, I'll repeat to you

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:54.990
what we said then, which is, you know,

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:57.870
there is a lot more to do

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:01.350
and we do have ongoing
productivity challenges

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.930
in the NHS and further progress to make.

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:07.410
But today's paper does
set out the improvements

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:10.680
that we are making and
the impact that is having.

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:14.396
So I agree with the IFS, I have to say

00:17:14.396 --> 00:17:17.376
that things are certainly
going in the right direction

00:17:17.376 --> 00:17:19.525
and that is thanks to
the enormous hard work

00:17:19.525 --> 00:17:22.020
of colleagues across the
country in really focusing

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:25.608
on that challenge to make
sure that every penny

00:17:25.608 --> 00:17:27.423
of taxpayer's money is well spent.

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:30.841
Can I say a word if I may,

00:17:30.841 --> 00:17:32.070
just about the government missions

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:35.700
because the government has
today set out their missions.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:37.530
I'm really pleased to see that the NHS

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:40.170
is central to their plans.

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:42.573
Obviously we share the same commitment

00:17:42.573 --> 00:17:45.810
that the government does
to reduce waiting times

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.520
and I think I would just
take this opportunity

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:49.650
to add that there has
been some media commentary

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:51.150
over the past week

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:54.570
about that coming at the
expense of services elsewhere.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:57.096
But I would just say you know,

00:17:57.096 --> 00:18:01.260
the NHS can and has
delivered on multiple fronts.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:04.830
For example, last winter,
despite unprecedented periods

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:06.810
of industrial action, we
delivered improvements

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.810
in urgent and emergency care performance

00:18:10.131 --> 00:18:11.340
and reductions in waiting lists

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:13.050
and with December data showing

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:16.290
that the waiting list fell
despite significant demand

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:17.823
for urgent care.

00:18:18.679 --> 00:18:19.512
So that's not to say

00:18:19.512 --> 00:18:24.000
that we are not going to have
a hugely challenging winter,

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.980
but NHS staff, even when hindered

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:28.309
by those strikes last year,

00:18:28.309 --> 00:18:31.946
did deliver incredibly high levels of care

00:18:31.946 --> 00:18:34.260
and pulled out all the
stops to do what they could

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:37.235
to provide the best
possible care for patients.

00:18:37.235 --> 00:18:40.580
And I'm absolutely certain
from what I see every day

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:41.460
as I talk to people in the NHS

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:43.743
and go around as you do Richard on visits,

00:18:44.761 --> 00:18:48.180
that that commitment has
not and will not change.

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:50.580
The other mission where we can play a part

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.520
is securing the highest sustained growth.

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:56.340
The NHS does play a really key role

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:57.810
as a driver of economic growth.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:00.180
And so really excited
about the conversation

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.357
we're going to have about our health

00:19:02.357 --> 00:19:04.773
and growth accelerators a bit later.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:11.076
Winter, so we have talked
about the preparations

00:19:11.076 --> 00:19:12.390
for winter at previous meetings

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:13.470
and we have the opportunity

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:16.410
to do so again today in more detail.

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:18.780
But I think it is just worth noting

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:20.610
that we do go into this winter

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:23.430
with more pressure than ever before.

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.920
So we've published today our first

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:30.030
of the weekly winter sit
reps, it being the start

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:34.210
of December and you will have seen already

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:36.396
that there are some
concerning figures in it.

00:19:36.396 --> 00:19:37.260
So we are heading into winter

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:41.970
with a 350% increase in
flu cases in hospitals

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:44.760
and an 86% rise in norovirus

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:47.550
compared to the same month last year.

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:50.940
And that shows I think just already

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:54.150
how busy our frontline teams are.

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:57.820
And that is with an average of over 96

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:00.660
and a half thousand hospital
beds occupied each day,

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:04.683
more so again at this
point in any other year.

00:20:04.683 --> 00:20:06.199
So comparing this point in time

00:20:06.199 --> 00:20:08.299
with previous years at this point in time,

00:20:09.203 --> 00:20:10.659
so expected pressure still to come

00:20:10.659 --> 00:20:14.100
because obviously we are only
at the beginning of December

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:17.760
and therefore managing
that additional demand

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:18.990
and the potential

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:22.560
for what I think is
being termed a quad-demic

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.900
of potential seasonal viruses
is I think probably top

00:20:26.765 --> 00:20:28.901
certainly of mine and
I'm sure other colleagues

00:20:28.901 --> 00:20:30.120
worry lists as we sit here today.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.840
What that means is, well
it means a number of things

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:34.033
but one of the things that means is our

00:20:34.033 --> 00:20:37.080
vaccination program is
more important than ever.

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:39.150
So it's great news that frontline teams

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:42.780
have now delivered more
than 27 million vaccinations

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:45.720
for flu, COVID and RSV.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:47.550
I think we worked out
last week that that was,

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:50.590
Steve will correct me if
I'm wrong, 300 jabs a minute

00:20:51.896 --> 00:20:53.060
since we launched our winter

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:56.520
or our seasonal vaccination campaign.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.380
That work needs to continue.

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:00.720
So we are protecting our
most vulnerable groups

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:03.330
and that of course includes
our own colleagues.

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:07.380
So we really need to make sure
that we are supporting staff

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:11.343
to get vaccinated if they
haven't already done so.

00:21:11.343 --> 00:21:12.824
And we did write out I think yesterday

00:21:12.824 --> 00:21:14.556
just to encourage people

00:21:14.556 --> 00:21:16.975
to take the opportunity
to protect themselves.

00:21:16.975 --> 00:21:19.740
Despite all of the efforts,

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:22.473
and we'll talk more about
this that we have made,

00:21:23.799 --> 00:21:26.003
that colleagues have
made to prepare as well

00:21:26.003 --> 00:21:27.206
as possible for winter.

00:21:27.206 --> 00:21:29.457
I think it is always just worth
noting that there are things

00:21:29.457 --> 00:21:32.786
of course outside our
control, not least the fact

00:21:32.786 --> 00:21:34.080
that we know that discharging patients

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:36.750
is a huge issue right across the country.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.383
And today's data, again reconfirms that,

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:44.312
as Lord Darzi said.

00:21:44.312 --> 00:21:46.960
And we again I think
discussed this as a board

00:21:48.387 --> 00:21:51.134
many times before but as
he said in his report,

00:21:51.134 --> 00:21:53.010
of course underinvestment
in capital in particular

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:56.040
has left a legacy of crumbling estate

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:57.510
and maintenance backlog.

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:00.000
Which means we still
face an uphill struggle

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.340
to consistently provide the right care

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:04.503
in the right place, at the right time.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:08.069
But finally, and this is the sort of

00:22:08.069 --> 00:22:09.690
where we really get a chance to look

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.938
to the future, I did just want to pick up

00:22:12.938 --> 00:22:13.771
on what you said about the importance

00:22:13.771 --> 00:22:15.750
of the work on the 10 year health plan.

00:22:15.750 --> 00:22:20.310
So as you said, that engagement
process is well underway

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:23.624
if Lord Darzi has done the
bit, which was setting out

00:22:23.624 --> 00:22:26.697
a sort of clear diagnosis
of what over the last

00:22:26.697 --> 00:22:28.410
sort of 20 years has been the
story of the health service.

00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:31.091
This is our opportunity to help create

00:22:31.091 --> 00:22:31.924
what the future will look like.

00:22:31.924 --> 00:22:34.830
Work streams have been set up
in many cases we've got NHSE

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:38.340
colleagues sitting as co-chairs of groups,

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:42.343
but more importantly the
public deliberation events

00:22:42.343 --> 00:22:43.320
have really been running at pace

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.900
and the engagement work
through the website

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:49.110
and with staff as already gathered.

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:53.343
I know hugely rich views
from the public and others,

00:22:54.635 --> 00:22:57.372
colleagues will have their own
reflections from the events.

00:22:57.372 --> 00:23:01.110
Mine from Middlesbrough was
just, I was incredibly struck

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:05.700
by how keen people were to work with us

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:08.490
to identify some of the solutions

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:12.000
and really lean into some
of the difficult discussions

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.830
about what it would really take

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:16.607
to create an NHS fit for the future.

00:23:16.607 --> 00:23:18.901
And it certainly said to me
that it's been absolutely

00:23:18.901 --> 00:23:21.330
the right thing to try and
do the depth of engagement

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:24.420
that the 10 year plan team
are currently undertaking,

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:28.230
but also a real vote actually
certainly the group I was in

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:30.150
for the importance of
the neighbourhood model,

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:32.370
which we've been talking
about since Claire Fuller

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:35.943
did her stock take and
working on as a group

00:23:35.943 --> 00:23:36.990
and with other partners since then.

00:23:36.990 --> 00:23:40.365
So Richard, I thought I
would stop there on the note

00:23:40.365 --> 00:23:42.330
of optimism for the
future whilst recognising

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:44.137
some of the challenges

00:23:44.137 --> 00:23:46.427
that we're going to
discuss in more detail now.

00:23:46.427 --> 00:23:49.111
- So any comments or questions for Amanda?

00:23:49.111 --> 00:23:51.171
We've had a good conversation
again earlier this morning

00:23:51.171 --> 00:23:52.385
in the private board as well.

00:23:52.385 --> 00:23:53.904
So thank you.

00:23:53.904 --> 00:23:56.819
What I would just share
then, I think the agenda

00:23:56.819 --> 00:23:58.470
for today is a very good agenda.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:00.240
Clearly we're going to have
the performance updates,

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:03.060
operation performance
recovery support programme,

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:05.760
financial performance,
and then productivity,

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:08.593
again really important for us to continue

00:24:08.593 --> 00:24:10.423
to focus on productivity.

00:24:10.423 --> 00:24:11.952
So we've got May Li here

00:24:11.952 --> 00:24:14.053
who's going to be leading first on that.

00:24:14.053 --> 00:24:15.213
A very good topic,

00:24:16.113 --> 00:24:17.100
a really important topic for the economy.

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:20.999
And Adam, welcome, on
health work and prevention.

00:24:20.999 --> 00:24:22.680
So the interaction between
health and the economy

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:24.570
and people's ability to work

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.490
and contribute to the workforce,

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:28.410
a review on the quality strategy.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.570
Very good, and we also know,

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:33.914
and Robert's here, you
know the quality committee

00:24:33.914 --> 00:24:36.090
is also transforming its
approach and its agenda,

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:38.700
but the quality strategy
mentioned strongly

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:42.510
in Lord Dazi's report,
it's a good topic to have

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:45.292
this afternoon, Steve and Duncan,

00:24:45.292 --> 00:24:47.335
I know you're going to
walk us through that

00:24:47.335 --> 00:24:49.175
and then importantly,
actually it might sound

00:24:49.175 --> 00:24:51.238
a little bit administrative
but actually the delegation

00:24:51.238 --> 00:24:52.800
of specialised services to ICBs,

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.360
the continued delegation

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:57.540
is part of that devolution
that we are doing.

00:24:57.540 --> 00:25:00.270
But that controlled
and sensible devolution

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:02.370
and in this case looking
at particular services,

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:04.260
specialised commissioning services

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:07.290
for where we're going to be
giving more responsibility

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:08.688
directly to those ICBs.

00:25:08.688 --> 00:25:11.610
So I think it's a good agenda.

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:13.634
I only started with that partly

00:25:13.634 --> 00:25:15.240
'cause I forgot to do something
which was really important

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:18.997
in my introduction, which is
just to note that we are going

00:25:18.997 --> 00:25:22.833
to be losing Jacqui Rock, which
is a real regret actually.

00:25:24.031 --> 00:25:26.790
You have been a really good strong leader

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:28.680
for NHS England, Jacqui,

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:31.813
and thank you for all that you have done.

00:25:31.813 --> 00:25:34.620
You've reformed the commercial

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:38.971
and estates portfolio, you've
oversee the establishment

00:25:38.971 --> 00:25:41.116
of a central commercial function

00:25:41.116 --> 00:25:42.030
and we're seeing the benefit of that

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:44.430
actually in much better
procurement pricing

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:48.609
and throughout the system
actually, which is really good.

00:25:48.609 --> 00:25:49.909
And some specific things you've done,

00:25:49.909 --> 00:25:52.585
you led on the procurement of the FDP,

00:25:52.585 --> 00:25:54.724
you led on the engagement

00:25:54.724 --> 00:25:56.430
with the new hospitals programme
government around the racks

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:58.680
and making sure that those themselves

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:00.690
are properly brought in.

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:02.942
So actually thank you for all you've done

00:26:02.942 --> 00:26:04.440
in leading a very good function

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:05.413
and we will miss you, miss you.

00:26:05.413 --> 00:26:09.769
- Can I, may I add, I'm so sorry Jacqui,

00:26:09.769 --> 00:26:11.609
it's because I've actually
in complete denial

00:26:11.609 --> 00:26:14.221
of the fact that you are allowed to go.

00:26:14.221 --> 00:26:16.228
But I just to add to
everything you've said

00:26:16.228 --> 00:26:18.777
about what Jacqui's done is
in the commercial function,

00:26:18.777 --> 00:26:20.237
which is now recognised in a way

00:26:20.237 --> 00:26:21.300
it just wasn't at all before

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:22.680
and has given I think a real pride

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:25.050
and an importance to that role.

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:27.652
And all of those colleagues across the NHS

00:26:27.652 --> 00:26:29.040
involved in similar roles.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:31.480
I know it's meant a huge amount

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:33.349
but I just wanted to
add as just as a member

00:26:33.349 --> 00:26:36.073
of the executive team and
the board Jacqui, thank you

00:26:36.073 --> 00:26:37.285
for your contribution because of course,

00:26:37.285 --> 00:26:41.220
you offer so much to
the corporate strength

00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:43.711
of the leadership of the
NHS as well as the strength

00:26:43.711 --> 00:26:45.561
and we will really miss you for both.

00:26:47.005 --> 00:26:48.543
- [Jacqui] Thank you very much.

00:26:48.543 --> 00:26:51.033
- Okay Emily, operational
performance update.

00:26:52.123 --> 00:26:53.220
- Thank you very much.

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:56.783
So the paper presents
the NHS's performance

00:26:56.783 --> 00:26:59.899
in September and October
based on the published data.

00:26:59.899 --> 00:27:03.300
I thought I would start with UEC

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:05.822
and winter, which is the key performance

00:27:05.822 --> 00:27:06.750
challenge at this point in the year.

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:08.250
And then I will give an overview

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:09.960
of the latest performance statistics

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:13.650
for elective, cancer,
diagnostics and mental health.

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:15.870
And I wanted to start with
the same frame of reference

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:18.150
that I've used at the last few boards,

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:22.072
which actually Amanda's
also just said a version of

00:27:22.072 --> 00:27:25.710
which is, as always these
presentations are a story

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:29.460
of both extraordinary
levels of improvement

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:31.680
and creativity in how
we deliver every day.

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:34.140
At the same time as
recognising we are nowhere near

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:36.052
where we would want to be

00:27:36.052 --> 00:27:38.538
and I hope that the way
the data's presented

00:27:38.538 --> 00:27:40.110
can show both things at the same time,

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:42.420
'cause I think it's important we recognise

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:43.800
what we've been able to do

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:46.350
as well as what we still have to do.

00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:50.370
So if we start on UEC, there's no doubt

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:53.079
as people have seen this morning

00:27:53.079 --> 00:27:55.368
that UEC performance has
started to feel the impact

00:27:55.368 --> 00:27:57.360
of winter pressures despite
that continued effort

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:59.847
and dedication of staff on the front line.

00:27:59.847 --> 00:28:02.970
We saw that in the data
which is in the paper,

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:04.140
which is the October published data

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:08.130
and in particular in the
category two response times.

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:10.230
That backdrop demonstrates the importance

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:12.957
of driving the three improvement areas

00:28:12.957 --> 00:28:15.720
that are outlined in the UEC
plan, avoiding conveyances,

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:18.450
avoiding admissions and
speeding up discharge.

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:19.560
All of which will continue

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:21.210
to be crucial over the next few months

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:24.416
to make sure the system is
as resilient as possible.

00:28:24.416 --> 00:28:27.240
We'll shortly be writing to the system,

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:30.530
setting out the importance
of a whole system approach

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:32.580
to risk across the UEC pathway this winter

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:35.201
to provide the safe high quality care

00:28:35.201 --> 00:28:38.101
for our patients while also
maintaining elective activity.

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:41.040
We know as Amanda's just mentioned,

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:43.260
that flu admissions are
already increasing pressure

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:45.510
on emergency departments.

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:48.480
So the bed occupancy we have at the moment

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:50.880
with flu is what we had
on the 24th of December

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:52.470
this time last year.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:54.884
So I thought it would be worth spending

00:28:54.884 --> 00:28:57.038
a couple of moments on
the vaccination campaign

00:28:57.038 --> 00:28:59.610
without belabouring it, given
it's already been mentioned.

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:03.210
But just to emphasise the
work that's already gone in

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:05.970
through all of the creativity
in the vaccine programme

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:08.550
to make sure as many
eligible people as possible

00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:12.120
are vaccinated against flu
and COVID-19 ahead of winter,

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:15.673
as well as the new campaign on RSV

00:29:15.673 --> 00:29:19.650
and Amanda's already shared that number,

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:24.630
but underneath that number
is continued creative

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:27.360
and supportive engagement
with a variety of communities

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.520
to make sure that vaccinations
are being delivered

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:32.100
in ways that work for the
communities that we serve.

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:34.690
And it may be that the
board may want Steve

00:29:35.756 --> 00:29:37.590
to elaborate on some of the
brilliant examples there

00:29:38.954 --> 00:29:42.180
if we just issue a bit of a
plea to the NHS on that basis.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.730
Only 24% of healthcare workers

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:46.920
at the end of October had
received a flu vaccine

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:50.905
and only 14% had received
their COVID vaccine.

00:29:50.905 --> 00:29:52.700
Those numbers have obviously gone up since

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:55.001
but still are not what
we would want them to be

00:29:55.001 --> 00:29:56.332
and lower than last year.

00:29:56.332 --> 00:29:58.510
This week surveillance figures

00:29:59.467 --> 00:30:01.313
show that flu positivity's increased.

00:30:02.273 --> 00:30:05.158
We know that vaccination
offers the best protection

00:30:05.158 --> 00:30:07.840
to eligible frontline health
and social care workers

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:08.673
and to the people who
are clinically vulnerable

00:30:08.673 --> 00:30:09.930
that they're caring for.

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:12.150
So we're asking colleagues
across the health system

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:13.950
to engage with staff about the importance

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:15.690
and benefits of vaccination

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:18.987
and to ensure staff can
easily access their jabs

00:30:18.987 --> 00:30:20.884
in a way that works for them.

00:30:20.884 --> 00:30:25.272
So if I now turn to the
rest of the detailed data,

00:30:25.272 --> 00:30:28.110
our most recent published UEC data

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:31.945
for October shows deterioration
in four hour performance

00:30:31.945 --> 00:30:34.626
and category two response
times as pressure started

00:30:34.626 --> 00:30:35.490
to increase at the end of the summer,

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:38.540
which coincided with
a wave of COVID cases,

00:30:38.540 --> 00:30:40.740
which is now coming down or has come down.

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:42.390
Total attendances in October

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:46.920
increased to 2,356,189

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:49.173
and emergency admissions also increased.

00:30:50.421 --> 00:30:52.260
Overall demand continues
to grow year on year

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:57.163
with October, 2024 total
attendances 6.2% above October 23.

00:30:58.530 --> 00:31:01.470
Four hour performance
in that context was 73%,

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:04.200
which is down 1.2 points from September,

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:09.033
but 2.8% above percentage
points above October 23.

00:31:09.899 --> 00:31:11.940
Despite that growth in attendances,

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:13.290
so good example of where the system

00:31:13.290 --> 00:31:15.773
is doing brilliant things at the same time

00:31:15.773 --> 00:31:17.100
as we have a long way to go.

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:19.200
Category two performance
also deteriorated,

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:22.470
which was up to 42 minutes
and 15 seconds in October,

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:26.040
34 seconds worse in that
case than October 23,

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:29.553
partly reflecting differences
in the COVID peak.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:32.520
12 hour breaches increased in October

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:34.650
and are once again
significantly above the levels

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:36.310
observed this time last year

00:31:37.222 --> 00:31:39.767
at 49,592 versus 44,655.

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:46.945
As we all know, our ambition
this year is to ensure that 78%

00:31:46.945 --> 00:31:49.170
of patients are seen within
four hours in March 25

00:31:49.170 --> 00:31:52.800
as well as improving category
two ambulance response times

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:54.690
to an average of 30
minutes across the year.

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:56.701
We are working with systems

00:31:56.701 --> 00:31:57.534
to support their plans

00:31:57.534 --> 00:32:00.340
including a new improvement
offer to those trusts

00:32:01.510 --> 00:32:02.790
that we saw were particularly struggling

00:32:03.841 --> 00:32:05.509
to implement all the
recommendations in the UEC plan

00:32:05.509 --> 00:32:08.220
and we are focused on
supporting those systems

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:10.710
to achieve an improved position.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:12.690
Remembering that in 23, 24,

00:32:12.690 --> 00:32:15.540
we saw a significant improvement
in four hour performance

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:17.190
in the final quarter of the year.

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:20.040
Turning to elective,

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:22.800
overall the total waiting list decreased

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:24.900
by 70,000 between August 24

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:28.440
and September 24 to 7.57 million.

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:31.444
And there were reductions in
all four long wait metrics

00:32:31.444 --> 00:32:34.020
in September 24 compared to August 24.

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:37.973
Specifically 104 week
waits decreased by 11

00:32:37.973 --> 00:32:42.973
to 113, 78 week waits
decreased by 632 to 2703.

00:32:44.010 --> 00:32:46.423
65 week waits decreased by 22,624

00:32:49.081 --> 00:32:50.313
to 22,903.

00:32:52.032 --> 00:32:55.542
We recognise that in September
we missed the initial target

00:32:55.542 --> 00:32:57.720
for virtual elimination of 65 week waits.

00:32:57.720 --> 00:32:59.670
But we are asking the NHS to make sure

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:03.967
that fewer than 10,000 people
are waiting more than 65 weeks

00:33:03.967 --> 00:33:06.000
for elective care by the 22nd of December.

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:08.250
And we're continuing to work closely

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:10.159
with the most challenged
trusts on that metric

00:33:10.159 --> 00:33:11.843
to support delivery against that target.

00:33:14.819 --> 00:33:15.720
Similarly, 18 week waits

00:33:15.720 --> 00:33:19.757
have decreased by 47,000

00:33:19.757 --> 00:33:22.830
from 3,189,538

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:27.830
to 3,142,530 within that performance

00:33:27.930 --> 00:33:30.075
against the 18 week standard

00:33:30.075 --> 00:33:32.740
remains broadly stable at 58.5%.

00:33:33.817 --> 00:33:36.510
In the coming weeks we will
publish the elective reform plan

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:38.520
which will set out how we
plan to work with systems

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:41.546
and partners to achieve
the constitutional target

00:33:41.546 --> 00:33:43.320
that the Prime Minister
has spoken about today.

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:45.180
Most importantly, as part of that reform

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:47.720
it will outline how we will put patients

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:48.553
and their experience of care

00:33:48.553 --> 00:33:49.863
at the heart of that reform.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:55.277
On cancer, levels of urgent
suspected cancer referrals

00:33:55.277 --> 00:33:56.610
are down 0.6% on the last year

00:33:56.610 --> 00:34:00.000
with 12,665 patients seen per working day

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:03.550
in September 24, in
September, none of the three

00:34:04.403 --> 00:34:05.250
cancer waiting time standards were met,

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:06.600
but all three have improved

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:09.900
if we compare September
24 to September 23,

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:11.670
which means we remain confident

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:14.130
about the delivery for
the end of the year.

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:16.821
For example, performance
against the faster

00:34:16.821 --> 00:34:19.144
diagnostic standards
or a seasonal decrease

00:34:19.144 --> 00:34:20.370
of 0.7% on the previous month

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:22.683
but was up 5.1% year on year.

00:34:24.597 --> 00:34:27.010
On diagnostics, our published statistics

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:30.263
show the total waiting
list stood at 1.587 million

00:34:30.263 --> 00:34:31.890
at the end of September with
a percentage of patients

00:34:31.890 --> 00:34:33.791
waiting over six weeks

00:34:33.791 --> 00:34:36.120
for a diagnostic test standing at 22.7%,

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:40.530
improving from 26.3% in September 23.

00:34:40.530 --> 00:34:43.590
And given the additional
capacity we've put in the system,

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:45.480
we are delighted to see that activity

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:47.423
continues to be delivered

00:34:47.423 --> 00:34:49.320
with almost every month
this year setting a record

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:51.783
which includes September at 2.37,

00:34:52.665 --> 00:34:55.115
almost 2.37 million
diagnostic tests carried out.

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:58.810
On mental health, we have improved access

00:34:59.651 --> 00:35:00.930
to specialist community
perinatal mental health

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:03.227
and maternal mental health services

00:35:03.227 --> 00:35:04.680
in the 12 months of September 24

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:09.680
to 60,637 people, which
is almost 100% increase

00:35:11.460 --> 00:35:13.803
in the 12 months to August 24.

00:35:15.814 --> 00:35:18.286
The national team continued
to work with regions

00:35:18.286 --> 00:35:19.676
to provide support to systems on access

00:35:19.676 --> 00:35:21.570
and other perinatal ambitions by collating

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:24.210
and sharing best practise
from high performing systems

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:25.950
as well as working to strengthen links

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:28.353
across the perinatal
mental health pathway,

00:35:29.336 --> 00:35:31.679
which is a good example
which I haven't covered

00:35:31.679 --> 00:35:32.760
in the other areas of where we are working

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:34.920
with systems with our regions

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.380
to try and reduce
variation in performance,

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:39.120
particularly where one system

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:41.580
or one organisation has fixed a problem

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:43.413
that is being experienced elsewhere.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:46.710
On children and young
people accessing service

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:51.613
increased to 798,479 in the
12 months to September 24.

00:35:54.158 --> 00:35:56.294
That's slightly ahead
of where system plans

00:35:56.294 --> 00:35:58.469
predicted we would be at
this point in the year,

00:35:58.469 --> 00:36:01.019
perhaps reflecting the
nationally led improvement offers

00:36:01.019 --> 00:36:02.285
that are in place to support systems

00:36:02.285 --> 00:36:03.390
to improve access as well as quality

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:04.620
and effectiveness

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:06.690
of the children young
people's community services

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:08.700
as well as improving data quality

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:11.641
which has been a fairly
substantive barrier

00:36:11.641 --> 00:36:14.185
to some of our work in
mental health when actually,

00:36:14.185 --> 00:36:16.154
it has not always been transparent

00:36:16.154 --> 00:36:18.404
exactly where the
performance challenges are.

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:22.860
We also have improved in the
12 months of September 24

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:27.780
the 673,153 people completing a course

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:30.300
of treatment with NHS talking therapies

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:32.670
where we are meeting a
reliable improvement rate

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:34.763
and fractionally below

00:36:34.763 --> 00:36:37.263
or hopefully on goal to reliable recovery.

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:42.360
I'll just finish with talking
about physical health checks

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:45.597
for people with severe mental
illness which were delivered

00:36:45.597 --> 00:36:48.990
to 272,874 people

00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:50.823
in the 12 months to September 24,

00:36:51.716 --> 00:36:55.470
which accounts for 57% of
people on the GPSMI registers.

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:59.550
I mentioned that the dementia
diagnosis rate is at 65.5%

00:36:59.550 --> 00:37:02.880
in September 24 against a target of 70%.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:05.280
This is one where there
is tremendous variation

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:08.099
across the country and
I continue to encourage

00:37:08.099 --> 00:37:09.540
sharing of best practise

00:37:09.540 --> 00:37:12.060
between regions via the
Regional Delivery Forum

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:15.289
to see if we can solidify that

00:37:15.289 --> 00:37:17.014
so that we can meet the target

00:37:17.014 --> 00:37:19.380
and better support people
living with dementia.

00:37:19.380 --> 00:37:22.533
Just before you go to
questions Chair sorry,

00:37:23.758 --> 00:37:25.850
rather than me, I just wondered
if it was worth hearing

00:37:25.850 --> 00:37:27.600
from Sarah-Jane briefly on where we're at

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:30.870
with current challenges
in the system given winter

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:33.459
before we go to border questions.

00:37:33.459 --> 00:37:35.541
- Can do, that would be
really helpful actually.

00:37:35.541 --> 00:37:36.755
And then we will get a question, yeah.

00:37:36.755 --> 00:37:37.588
- Thank you Emily and thank you chair.

00:37:37.588 --> 00:37:41.813
Just yeah, as Emily described,
we're really starting

00:37:41.813 --> 00:37:44.310
to feel some of those
challenges now on the ground.

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:46.440
We've had this rising tide of RSV,

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:49.355
which has been principally
impacting children

00:37:49.355 --> 00:37:52.920
but making lots of parts of
our UEC pathway really busy

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:54.460
and we're starting to see now

00:37:55.297 --> 00:37:57.570
the quite steep rise in flu cases.

00:37:57.570 --> 00:37:59.940
Lots of which is compounded
by the norovirus,

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:02.370
not least because as Amanda mentioned,

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:03.990
where we've got estates constraints,

00:38:03.990 --> 00:38:08.850
it can be quite difficult
to manage cases safely

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:09.800
when we've only got

00:38:10.752 --> 00:38:12.602
sort of a smaller number of cubicles.

00:38:13.846 --> 00:38:16.290
So that's all leading to
the higher bed utilisation

00:38:16.290 --> 00:38:19.500
and our congested emergency departments

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:23.190
which in turn has impacted
on handover delays.

00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:25.608
So our absolute focus at the moment

00:38:25.608 --> 00:38:30.608
right from NHS England
through our region's systems

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:34.410
and trust is to really
focus on quality and safety

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:37.080
and to do everything can, in particular,

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:39.150
to avoid the very longest waits

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:42.210
that some of our patients
might experience.

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:44.520
So trying to avoid wherever possible

00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:46.849
the longest handover delays

00:38:46.849 --> 00:38:48.258
and particularly some of the waits

00:38:48.258 --> 00:38:49.544
for mental health patients

00:38:49.544 --> 00:38:50.970
that we sometimes see in
our emergency departments,

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:55.489
which we know are exceptionally
distressing for everybody

00:38:55.489 --> 00:38:57.210
and despite really
challenging circumstances,

00:38:57.210 --> 00:38:59.400
we are starting to make
some headway with that

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:01.710
through real system working and mental

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:05.031
and physical health services
and coming together.

00:39:05.031 --> 00:39:08.820
And we are also using
the full strength really

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:11.630
of our clinical offer in
NHS England, our ECIS,

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:13.992
so the emergency care
intensive support team

00:39:13.992 --> 00:39:15.767
and GIRFT colleagues.

00:39:15.767 --> 00:39:18.870
So we have experienced
clinicians that can help

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:20.670
some of our clinical teams on the ground

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:22.890
in the most challenging organisations,

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:25.020
with real hands-on support

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.960
to be able to provide best practise.

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.000
So the challenge has started,

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:32.587
but I think everybody across the NHS

00:39:32.587 --> 00:39:35.700
has stepped up and is ready for that.

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:38.070
It will be a difficult few weeks to come,

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:39.690
but I just want to say a huge thank you

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:42.693
to reiterate those of Amanda's
and Emily's to our teams.

00:39:43.714 --> 00:39:47.927
People are going to
extraordinary lengths, you know,

00:39:47.927 --> 00:39:49.307
we'll talk about productivity later

00:39:49.307 --> 00:39:51.366
and some of the figures, but I see people

00:39:51.366 --> 00:39:54.891
staying many hours
beyond shifts, you know,

00:39:54.891 --> 00:39:56.310
getting up at extraordinary
times in the morning

00:39:56.310 --> 00:40:00.485
to check that our patients are safe

00:40:00.485 --> 00:40:01.895
and I know that people will continue

00:40:01.895 --> 00:40:03.131
to do that throughout winter.

00:40:03.131 --> 00:40:05.135
So yeah, a challenging few weeks to come,

00:40:05.135 --> 00:40:05.968
but I think all of our systems

00:40:05.968 --> 00:40:08.730
and processes are stood up in preparation

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:11.790
and from NHS England, we
will do everything we can

00:40:11.790 --> 00:40:15.280
to support frontline
services to maintain quality

00:40:16.132 --> 00:40:18.063
and safety over the coming weeks.

00:40:19.161 --> 00:40:21.510
- Very good, so can we open
up to questions for Emily

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:23.763
or Sarah-Jane, any particular comments,

00:40:24.624 --> 00:40:26.074
questions, Andrew then Wol

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:30.330
- So Andrew Morris, deputy chair.

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:33.600
So we're two percentage points up

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:37.200
compared to where we were
last year on four hours,

00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:39.990
which is good and we're likely

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:42.240
to treat another million people

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:44.583
by the end of this financial year,

00:40:45.552 --> 00:40:47.531
which is quite astonishing,

00:40:47.531 --> 00:40:49.676
recognising we've opened
very little extra capacity

00:40:49.676 --> 00:40:51.753
compared to last winter.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:57.800
So that performance I think
is really, really positive.

00:40:58.140 --> 00:41:00.930
My kind of question is around 12 hours

00:41:00.930 --> 00:41:04.140
and what is it that we are doing this year

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:07.117
that we weren't doing last year

00:41:07.117 --> 00:41:08.610
in terms of some of the initiatives

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:11.973
that we're making or
taking within systems.

00:41:12.971 --> 00:41:15.120
'Cause I think that's the
challenge we've got really,

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:17.760
to try and improve people's experiences

00:41:17.760 --> 00:41:20.100
'cause no one likes being
in an emergency department

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:22.450
full stop, waiting for 12 hours

00:41:23.381 --> 00:41:25.260
or more for a bed is, you
know, a particular challenge

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:27.120
for individuals and families.

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:29.891
- So can I just suggest between and Emily

00:41:29.891 --> 00:41:31.410
and Sarah-Jane, let's take a few

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:33.660
of the questions and then answer them.

00:41:33.660 --> 00:41:35.183
- [Speaker] Pens out Chair.

00:41:35.183 --> 00:41:37.395
- Pens out, I can see Emily's writing.

00:41:37.395 --> 00:41:38.607
So Wol then Robert.

00:41:38.607 --> 00:41:39.900
- So just to build on Andrew's point,

00:41:39.900 --> 00:41:41.550
I'm thinking about the flow rate.

00:41:42.503 --> 00:41:45.240
I mean 96% occupancy is running very hot

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:47.130
and I'm sort of thinking about,

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:48.900
asking about what are we doing

00:41:48.900 --> 00:41:50.880
to improve the flow rate through hospitals

00:41:52.412 --> 00:41:54.843
so that as we get,
again, I'm hearing a hint

00:41:54.843 --> 00:41:56.220
that this winter it's a
bit earlier the peaks,

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:57.900
it looks like it's going to happen.

00:41:57.900 --> 00:42:00.663
We're managing, is that
quad-demic, did you say?

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:03.071
- [Amanda] Potential.

00:42:03.071 --> 00:42:03.904
- Potential quad-demic

00:42:03.904 --> 00:42:05.843
and yeah we're running very, very hot

00:42:05.843 --> 00:42:07.800
and what sort of plans do we
have in place to make sure

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:09.520
we get through in a good manner

00:42:10.401 --> 00:42:12.353
and that the patients get a
decent experience through that?

00:42:13.350 --> 00:42:14.940
- Robert?

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:17.167
- Yeah, comment on the primary care data

00:42:17.167 --> 00:42:19.530
and a question about mental health.

00:42:19.530 --> 00:42:22.020
So the primary care data,

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:24.980
the fact that just over 50%

00:42:24.980 --> 00:42:26.930
of all practise appointments took place

00:42:27.842 --> 00:42:30.210
on the same day as the
booking is impressive

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:32.970
and it's slightly out of kilter
with the public perception

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:37.606
of GP access and 90% within 14 days.

00:42:37.606 --> 00:42:38.856
So that's just a comment.

00:42:40.323 --> 00:42:42.168
On the mental health front,

00:42:42.168 --> 00:42:44.266
the talking therapies
is a great success story

00:42:44.266 --> 00:42:46.410
'cause it's really an
evidence based intervention

00:42:46.410 --> 00:42:50.190
and the numbers are
impressive of 700,000 treated.

00:42:50.190 --> 00:42:52.774
My question was, I don't have a sense

00:42:52.774 --> 00:42:54.660
as to what fraction of demand that is.

00:42:54.660 --> 00:42:57.543
So what's the denominator as it were?

00:42:59.100 --> 00:42:59.933
- Very good.

00:42:59.933 --> 00:43:01.821
And then Jane do you want to come in

00:43:01.821 --> 00:43:03.957
and then we'll take that first part?

00:43:03.957 --> 00:43:04.955
- It was just a little, sorry,

00:43:04.955 --> 00:43:06.090
Jane Ellison executive director.

00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:08.190
It was just a little pick upon what Amanda

00:43:09.394 --> 00:43:10.500
and Emily mentioned around vaccination

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:12.870
just to see if there was any more capacity

00:43:12.870 --> 00:43:16.524
to get more from further
vaccination of members

00:43:16.524 --> 00:43:18.934
of the public as we're, you know, not yet

00:43:18.934 --> 00:43:22.833
through Christmas and
whether we there was more

00:43:22.833 --> 00:43:24.203
to be got from a another push.

00:43:25.115 --> 00:43:26.312
- And then if I add mine

00:43:26.312 --> 00:43:28.727
just before we then go
to the question which is,

00:43:28.727 --> 00:43:30.412
if we think about discharge

00:43:30.412 --> 00:43:31.609
and discharge into care,

00:43:31.609 --> 00:43:34.863
have we got any perspectives
on capacity in care?

00:43:35.813 --> 00:43:38.866
The provision of some care packages

00:43:38.866 --> 00:43:41.370
by local authorities, how we seeing that

00:43:41.370 --> 00:43:44.854
as we now approach the sort of
more difficult winter months?

00:43:44.854 --> 00:43:46.209
That would be a question.

00:43:46.209 --> 00:43:48.854
So I dunno how you're going
to team team this and.

00:43:48.854 --> 00:43:52.183
- I was just going to start on 12 hours

00:43:52.183 --> 00:43:53.250
just with a frame, but then
maybe turn to Sarah-Jane

00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:55.140
to talk a bit in more detail about that

00:43:55.140 --> 00:43:57.468
and the flow rate, which
should also include

00:43:57.468 --> 00:43:58.661
the care packages

00:43:58.661 --> 00:43:59.865
and then I can come back to the others

00:43:59.865 --> 00:44:01.321
but just on 12 hours,

00:44:01.321 --> 00:44:02.865
which obviously is a really good question.

00:44:02.865 --> 00:44:06.750
It's worth thinking about
it in the two categories.

00:44:06.750 --> 00:44:09.750
So those who go on to be
admitted, which is about 2/3

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:12.990
and those who go straight home
having been in the department

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:16.083
more than 12 hours because
actually the experience

00:44:16.083 --> 00:44:17.282
is probably quite different

00:44:17.282 --> 00:44:18.420
from a patient perspective, Sarah-Jane,

00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:19.520
do you want to come in

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:21.702
with what you've been doing on that?

00:44:21.702 --> 00:44:24.052
- Yeah, so it's our number
one concern, Andrew.

00:44:24.983 --> 00:44:26.436
Like absolutely.

00:44:26.436 --> 00:44:27.930
So the kind of, as Emily set out,

00:44:27.930 --> 00:44:30.540
our actions are really
under the three headings.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:34.290
One, we need to do everything
we possibly can for patients

00:44:34.290 --> 00:44:38.103
that could be supported through
urgent community response,

00:44:39.205 --> 00:44:41.460
virtual wards, other sorts of alternatives

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:45.360
to admission at the point
that they first ring 999,

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:48.270
that is starting to become
more and more successful.

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:50.520
But we need to keep pushing
the boundaries of that

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:52.230
because lots of the patients

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:54.510
that are conveyed to
hospital for all good reason

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:57.630
at the time are not getting
a huge medical benefit

00:44:57.630 --> 00:44:58.473
from the stay.

00:44:59.484 --> 00:45:01.587
So that's just the most
effective thing that we can do

00:45:01.587 --> 00:45:03.930
and we need to keep
really focused on that.

00:45:03.930 --> 00:45:07.230
The second is when patients
do arrive at hospital

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:11.160
where they don't really
need the full admission,

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:15.570
the SDEC pathways are there as extensive

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:18.577
as they can be through
all of the specialties

00:45:18.577 --> 00:45:20.221
and resilient throughout the day.

00:45:20.221 --> 00:45:21.891
So sometimes it becomes more difficult

00:45:21.891 --> 00:45:23.673
as the day progresses to use SDEC.

00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:27.450
Again, that is showing to be a
really effective intervention

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:30.180
but we've got quite a
lot of variation there.

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:32.640
And then beyond that, it is
that flow into the hospital

00:45:32.640 --> 00:45:35.730
and the discharge and the
senior clinical decision making.

00:45:35.730 --> 00:45:37.898
The teams that are on the
ground that I mentioned,

00:45:37.898 --> 00:45:39.841
ECIS and GIRFT, the number one thing

00:45:39.841 --> 00:45:44.700
that they work on is
the medical job planning

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:47.280
to ensure that people
have got sufficient time

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:50.520
to be able to dedicate
to UEC care pathways,

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:55.500
be that the acute medical
units or the wards and so on.

00:45:55.500 --> 00:45:58.920
When we start to get
into the real challenges,

00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:01.470
Wol, it really is about what can we do

00:46:01.470 --> 00:46:06.330
in terms of escalation,
our core bed capacity

00:46:06.330 --> 00:46:11.330
is almost at the maximum now
that we are funded to deliver,

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:13.920
but we have got the ability to be able

00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:15.930
to escalate into certain
parts of the hospital

00:46:15.930 --> 00:46:17.340
for periods of time.

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:19.830
We are then absolutely
it's about the quality

00:46:19.830 --> 00:46:22.120
and Duncan's team have done some work

00:46:23.499 --> 00:46:24.702
on just really making sure

00:46:24.702 --> 00:46:26.040
that the basic fundamental standards

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:28.830
of care are there for all of our patients

00:46:28.830 --> 00:46:31.270
no matter where they might
be in an organisation

00:46:32.106 --> 00:46:33.340
if they are in an area

00:46:35.048 --> 00:46:36.461
that we wouldn't ordinarily use

00:46:36.461 --> 00:46:37.380
for urgent and emergency care.

00:46:37.380 --> 00:46:39.750
And then just finally on discharge.

00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:43.143
At the moment, the numbers are holding,

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:45.840
but I think we are all
collectively concerned

00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:48.180
and when I say we, it is ourselves

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:50.977
and our colleagues in DHSC

00:46:50.977 --> 00:46:52.710
and social care leaders, just as we start

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:56.910
to move into next year,
that we will start to see

00:46:56.910 --> 00:47:01.216
an increase in challenge
with social care capacity,

00:47:01.216 --> 00:47:02.473
both in terms of some
of the funding available

00:47:02.473 --> 00:47:05.100
through local authorities,

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:06.840
but also some of the care packages

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:10.203
for that social care
providers are able to deliver.

00:47:11.052 --> 00:47:13.080
So it's something that we are
working closely on together

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:14.590
in particularly in the areas

00:47:16.056 --> 00:47:19.035
where there's challenged
UEC performance to ensure

00:47:19.035 --> 00:47:20.520
that those two things are
looked at collectively

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:23.520
and we are doing everything
we can to prioritise again

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:25.443
that flow and safety for patients.

00:47:26.352 --> 00:47:28.500
So lot as I say, lot's happening,

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:32.064
but even despite all of those
things, we know that yeah,

00:47:32.064 --> 00:47:33.967
there'll be challenging weeks to come.

00:47:33.967 --> 00:47:35.013
Thank you.

00:47:37.108 --> 00:47:38.567
- [Emily] Steve, do you
want to say something

00:47:38.567 --> 00:47:39.420
about the vaccination rate?

00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:41.250
- Yeah, thanks Emily, thanks Jane.

00:47:41.250 --> 00:47:44.484
So I won't repeat all of
the points that been made

00:47:44.484 --> 00:47:47.070
about where we're at
in the current campaign

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:49.020
other than just to highlight that on flu

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:51.540
in particular for over 65's,

00:47:51.540 --> 00:47:53.940
we're slightly ahead of
where we were last year,

00:47:54.982 --> 00:47:57.022
so about half a million more vaccinations

00:47:57.022 --> 00:47:58.524
in that particular group,

00:47:58.524 --> 00:48:01.020
which is particularly
important than last year.

00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:05.460
We started the flu and COVID
campaigns a bit later this year

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:07.660
because it's clinically beneficial

00:48:09.396 --> 00:48:10.493
to vaccinate closer to winter.

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:13.162
So we started on the 3rd of October

00:48:13.162 --> 00:48:17.166
and just to your point
Jane, the campaigns for flu

00:48:17.166 --> 00:48:19.590
and COVID will run on the main offer

00:48:19.590 --> 00:48:21.300
until the 20th of December.

00:48:21.300 --> 00:48:23.400
So it's definitely not too late.

00:48:23.400 --> 00:48:27.150
They're around a million
appointments available every week.

00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:30.690
People can book them dead
easily either on the NHS app,

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:32.190
the national booking service,

00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:36.231
or they can ring 119 if
those methods don't work.

00:48:36.231 --> 00:48:39.542
And there are more sites
across general practise

00:48:39.542 --> 00:48:43.042
and community pharmacy
than we've ever had.

00:48:43.042 --> 00:48:45.390
So the offer is pretty
convenient this year

00:48:45.390 --> 00:48:46.500
and we'd really encourage people

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:49.440
who haven't yet had their jabs

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:51.570
to go and book an appointment.

00:48:51.570 --> 00:48:53.791
People can book to get a flu

00:48:53.791 --> 00:48:55.984
and COVID jab together if they want

00:48:55.984 --> 00:48:57.178
to get them at the same time.

00:48:57.178 --> 00:49:00.450
And then maybe just a touch on the point

00:49:00.450 --> 00:49:03.960
Emily and Amanda both made about RSV,

00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:06.420
that's an all year round offer.

00:49:06.420 --> 00:49:09.898
So whilst COVID and flu kind
of campaigns and have a start

00:49:09.898 --> 00:49:13.230
and an end point, RSV's offered year round

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:17.768
for the older adult cohort
and for pregnant women.

00:49:17.768 --> 00:49:21.690
So again, we'd really, maternity
units are doing a great job

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:24.930
of offering vaccination to pregnant women

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:28.980
and general practise are
calling in 75 year olds,

00:49:28.980 --> 00:49:33.015
75 to 80 year olds for their RSV jab.

00:49:33.015 --> 00:49:34.620
So if people haven't yet been invited,

00:49:34.620 --> 00:49:37.140
they could contact their general practise

00:49:37.140 --> 00:49:38.557
if they want to get a jab.

00:49:38.557 --> 00:49:39.748
- Thank you.

00:49:40.940 --> 00:49:43.813
Just quickly on the
other was only one thing,

00:49:43.813 --> 00:49:45.030
'cause I think your primary
care point was a recognition

00:49:45.030 --> 00:49:47.310
as opposed to a question.

00:49:47.310 --> 00:49:50.876
So on mental health, the statistic

00:49:50.876 --> 00:49:53.070
that we collect is not the people waiting,

00:49:53.070 --> 00:49:55.307
it's the people receiving
a course of treatment.

00:49:55.307 --> 00:49:57.450
So I don't have a clear denominator

00:49:57.450 --> 00:50:00.030
and it's a good example of
where we have transparency

00:50:00.030 --> 00:50:03.080
over some parts of the mental health data

00:50:03.080 --> 00:50:05.384
and some other parts I'll
pick that up with the team.

00:50:05.384 --> 00:50:07.920
The thing to develop on that point,

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:11.625
because as you say it's a proven
therapy, is the difference

00:50:11.625 --> 00:50:14.700
between the percentage
achieving reliable improvement

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:16.440
and reliable recovery.

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:19.210
And the team is very focused at the moment

00:50:19.210 --> 00:50:20.939
on obviously increasing those percentages

00:50:20.939 --> 00:50:22.453
so that we are not just tracking those

00:50:22.453 --> 00:50:23.668
completing a course of treatment,

00:50:23.668 --> 00:50:24.960
which is the number that I referred to,

00:50:24.960 --> 00:50:27.027
but the 67% achieving reliable improvement

00:50:27.027 --> 00:50:31.150
and 48% achieving
reliable recovery targets.

00:50:33.450 --> 00:50:36.645
So that's an illumination,

00:50:36.645 --> 00:50:38.430
but not actually an
answer to the question.

00:50:38.430 --> 00:50:43.430
- Okay, thank you, should
we move on to the update

00:50:44.314 --> 00:50:45.930
on the Recovery Support
Programme, Emily, which you

00:50:46.869 --> 00:50:48.642
and Jan are leading into force, I think.

00:50:48.642 --> 00:50:51.377
- Thank you, so I'll just
say a few words to, sorry?

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:53.913
- Explain it what it is.

00:50:53.913 --> 00:50:56.520
- To explain what it is.

00:50:56.520 --> 00:51:00.030
Well the RSP stands for
Recovery Support Programme

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:03.880
and the RSP approach has been in place

00:51:05.098 --> 00:51:07.113
since June of 2021.

00:51:08.625 --> 00:51:10.365
Providing focused, intensive support

00:51:10.365 --> 00:51:12.717
and oversight to the most challenged ICBs

00:51:12.717 --> 00:51:14.973
and providers in the country.

00:51:14.973 --> 00:51:15.806
We wanted to take the opportunity

00:51:15.806 --> 00:51:18.450
to update the board on the work of the RSP

00:51:18.450 --> 00:51:20.795
and to discuss how we'll continue

00:51:20.795 --> 00:51:22.770
to develop a mature approach
over the coming months

00:51:22.770 --> 00:51:24.933
as the operating model evolves.

00:51:26.289 --> 00:51:27.813
I'm delighted that Jan Thomas,

00:51:27.813 --> 00:51:29.322
who's our new national director,

00:51:29.322 --> 00:51:30.922
can join us for the session today.

00:51:30.922 --> 00:51:34.770
RSP replaced the previous
special measures programmes

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:37.620
that people probably be familiar
with the language around.

00:51:37.620 --> 00:51:39.927
At the moment there are 20 NHS providers

00:51:39.927 --> 00:51:42.960
and three ICBs enrolled in RSP

00:51:42.960 --> 00:51:44.580
and that list of organisations

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:47.798
is kept up to date on the NHSE website.

00:51:47.798 --> 00:51:50.040
So it's publicly available information.

00:51:50.040 --> 00:51:52.890
RSP can be applied to an
individual organisation

00:51:52.890 --> 00:51:56.867
or to a whole system,
which will comprise the ICB

00:51:56.867 --> 00:51:58.917
as well as the constituent NHS providers.

00:52:00.381 --> 00:52:01.740
When an organisation enters RSP,

00:52:01.740 --> 00:52:03.810
each organisation undergoes a diagnostic

00:52:03.810 --> 00:52:05.960
to identify the drivers
of underperformance

00:52:06.828 --> 00:52:08.734
and that then informs the development

00:52:08.734 --> 00:52:09.660
of an integrated improvement plan

00:52:09.660 --> 00:52:13.715
that will cover all aspects
of challenging performance,

00:52:13.715 --> 00:52:16.983
which includes finance,
quality, operations,

00:52:17.865 --> 00:52:19.590
and organisational culture.

00:52:19.590 --> 00:52:21.120
Crucially, the RSP is designed

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:23.580
to provide support just
for a time limited period,

00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:26.520
and one of the first things
the team do is to work

00:52:26.520 --> 00:52:29.880
with leaders to identify
what the exit criteria are

00:52:29.880 --> 00:52:32.523
and what is the estimated exit date.

00:52:33.510 --> 00:52:37.500
We support the organisations
to exit the programme

00:52:37.500 --> 00:52:39.847
and then continue to provide oversight

00:52:39.847 --> 00:52:41.697
to ensure improvements are sustained.

00:52:43.424 --> 00:52:45.162
As the paper before the board highlights,

00:52:45.162 --> 00:52:48.429
almost all the organisations
in RSP experience changes

00:52:48.429 --> 00:52:49.942
to board level leadership
through their time

00:52:49.942 --> 00:52:52.059
in the program, with changes
to chief executive officer,

00:52:52.059 --> 00:52:55.770
chief financial officer and
chair being the most common.

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:57.060
We've seen this play out

00:52:57.060 --> 00:52:59.325
indeed just over the last few months

00:52:59.325 --> 00:53:00.600
where changes in leadership
in several organisations

00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:02.580
have helped provide energy, focus

00:53:02.580 --> 00:53:04.503
and grip to make improvement happen.

00:53:06.332 --> 00:53:07.528
We know we've got more to do though

00:53:07.528 --> 00:53:09.390
to strengthen leadership in
the most challenged places

00:53:09.390 --> 00:53:12.849
and we are developing a more
systematic way of identifying

00:53:12.849 --> 00:53:13.682
and developing talented leaders

00:53:13.682 --> 00:53:15.466
and matching them to roles

00:53:15.466 --> 00:53:16.710
where they can make the biggest impact.

00:53:16.710 --> 00:53:19.866
When in post, that means
we need to give leaders

00:53:19.866 --> 00:53:21.056
the right flexibilities,

00:53:21.056 --> 00:53:23.758
incentives and support
to turn things around,

00:53:23.758 --> 00:53:26.130
given the challenging
nature of many of the roles

00:53:26.130 --> 00:53:27.153
that are involved.

00:53:28.497 --> 00:53:30.000
We do already do that in many
places across the country,

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:32.250
but there's an opportunity
to be more systematic,

00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:36.003
both in identification and
mobilisation of talented leaders.

00:53:36.990 --> 00:53:41.770
We are also looking to evolve
our overall approach to RSP,

00:53:41.770 --> 00:53:44.040
which Jan may want to say a
few words on most critically,

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:45.810
making sure that our diagnostic

00:53:45.810 --> 00:53:49.230
really gets at the underlying drivers

00:53:49.230 --> 00:53:50.920
of underperformance as opposed

00:53:51.857 --> 00:53:53.700
to the symptoms that
may have been the reason

00:53:53.700 --> 00:53:57.590
that the organisation went
into the RSP programme

00:53:57.590 --> 00:53:58.463
in the first place.

00:53:59.515 --> 00:54:01.440
We want to make sure
that organisations in RSP

00:54:01.440 --> 00:54:02.640
are able to remain focused

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:05.130
on their most foundational challenges.

00:54:05.130 --> 00:54:08.130
And it's an opportunity for this board too

00:54:08.130 --> 00:54:10.920
because the NHSE support
needs to be clear,

00:54:10.920 --> 00:54:14.010
needs to be agile and needs
to be well coordinated

00:54:14.010 --> 00:54:16.020
and expert in all of the areas

00:54:16.020 --> 00:54:18.580
that might need to be addressed in RSP

00:54:19.999 --> 00:54:21.270
as well as focused on
supporting organisations

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:24.420
to address the key drivers of
the performance challenges.

00:54:24.420 --> 00:54:26.820
So as we evolve that, we're
working closely with Steve

00:54:26.820 --> 00:54:28.590
and with Adam Doyle and others

00:54:28.590 --> 00:54:31.360
to ensure the improvements
we're making to RSP align

00:54:32.264 --> 00:54:35.166
with the new operating model
and the updated oversight

00:54:35.166 --> 00:54:36.365
and assessment framework

00:54:36.365 --> 00:54:37.575
and make the most of some

00:54:37.575 --> 00:54:40.020
of the improvement resources
Sarah-Jane's already mentioned

00:54:40.020 --> 00:54:43.170
and the whole spectrum from
improvement to assurance

00:54:43.170 --> 00:54:45.090
and performance management.

00:54:45.090 --> 00:54:47.320
So I'll just hand over to Jan to take us

00:54:48.213 --> 00:54:49.740
through some of the detail in the paper.

00:54:49.740 --> 00:54:50.790
- Thank you, Emily.

00:54:50.790 --> 00:54:52.701
Yes, I'm Jan Thomas.

00:54:52.701 --> 00:54:54.430
I'm the national director for RSP

00:54:55.281 --> 00:54:58.293
and I'm also the chief
executive of Cambridge

00:54:58.293 --> 00:55:00.240
and Peterborough, who's
been in RSP and out of RSP.

00:55:00.240 --> 00:55:02.920
So it's a bit of user experience here

00:55:03.756 --> 00:55:05.370
on this one applied as well.

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:08.610
So look, RSP and what we try to do

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:10.860
is really difficult and
it's really difficult

00:55:10.860 --> 00:55:14.550
because these are often large
challenges that are deep

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:18.880
and complex and not solved
by pulling one lever

00:55:18.880 --> 00:55:19.713
and something happens.

00:55:19.713 --> 00:55:23.950
They're often to do with
not just cultural issues

00:55:24.803 --> 00:55:26.019
or operational issues,

00:55:26.019 --> 00:55:28.871
but actually there are often
some deep structural issues

00:55:28.871 --> 00:55:30.184
there that we have to deal with.

00:55:30.184 --> 00:55:32.078
So these are issues that are going

00:55:32.078 --> 00:55:35.211
to not be fixed within
two or three months.

00:55:35.211 --> 00:55:37.260
It is about how do you get the changes

00:55:37.260 --> 00:55:39.330
and momentum around improvement?

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:40.440
And I think that's an important word.

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:41.730
How do we get the momentum

00:55:41.730 --> 00:55:44.219
and then how do we build the foundations

00:55:44.219 --> 00:55:45.052
for this to be sustainable?

00:55:45.052 --> 00:55:46.830
Because the success criteria should be,

00:55:46.830 --> 00:55:49.140
are they still improving in two years time

00:55:49.140 --> 00:55:51.713
as well as did they exit the programme?

00:55:51.713 --> 00:55:52.680
And I think having been
through the programme,

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:56.400
building those foundations is
the most important element.

00:55:56.400 --> 00:56:00.030
And those foundations as Emily highlighted

00:56:00.030 --> 00:56:03.960
are not single, they
are around the quality,

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:06.210
the performance, and the finance together

00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:09.600
and how those things interact
alongside medical leadership

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:13.230
to make sure we've got the
balance right on that quadrant

00:56:13.230 --> 00:56:15.003
and making sure that's sustainable.

00:56:16.283 --> 00:56:19.415
So what we do now, as Emily has outlined,

00:56:19.415 --> 00:56:21.270
is around how do we get the diagnostic,

00:56:21.270 --> 00:56:23.537
how do we then apply regulation?

00:56:23.537 --> 00:56:26.002
How do we then really drive

00:56:26.002 --> 00:56:30.480
the targeted approach to the support?

00:56:30.480 --> 00:56:33.060
But obviously, because I've
said these are complex issues,

00:56:33.060 --> 00:56:34.623
we also lean in with support.

00:56:35.492 --> 00:56:37.212
We've got excellent support into a number

00:56:37.212 --> 00:56:40.440
of our RSP from the tier one
support for UEC, for example,

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:43.787
where it compliments the
work that we are doing

00:56:43.787 --> 00:56:47.942
to drive the sustainability,
going forward,

00:56:47.942 --> 00:56:49.620
what we are concentrating
on is what I describe

00:56:49.620 --> 00:56:53.528
as the kind of quadrant
approach to diagnostics.

00:56:53.528 --> 00:56:55.590
And what I mean by that is
how do we really balance

00:56:55.590 --> 00:57:00.590
what is happening operationally,
clinically with quality

00:57:00.923 --> 00:57:03.600
and finance, and how do we
really use that quadrant

00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:07.800
to assess what are the right solutions

00:57:07.800 --> 00:57:09.543
for those organisations.

00:57:10.485 --> 00:57:12.150
From that, we can then
have clear accountability

00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:15.180
and then from that we can have
the clear improvement plan,

00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:17.760
which will be different for
different organisations.

00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:20.340
But actually we think that
looking at that improvement plan

00:57:20.340 --> 00:57:22.020
over a two year period

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:24.060
and measuring the improvements
that they're making,

00:57:24.060 --> 00:57:26.336
statistically along those measures

00:57:26.336 --> 00:57:29.070
is the way through on it.

00:57:29.070 --> 00:57:32.268
As Emily's also mentioned,
we are not doing this alone.

00:57:32.268 --> 00:57:35.760
There are some real
helpful interdependencies.

00:57:35.760 --> 00:57:37.170
The work on management

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:38.910
and leadership is really critical

00:57:38.910 --> 00:57:41.080
to make sure we are aligning people

00:57:42.042 --> 00:57:43.544
with the right skill sets to do this

00:57:43.544 --> 00:57:45.390
'cause this is a difficult thing to do

00:57:45.390 --> 00:57:48.450
and it is a skill set that
usually follows a roadmap

00:57:48.450 --> 00:57:51.634
of how you get that
change in transformation.

00:57:51.634 --> 00:57:52.981
And that's what we're really

00:57:52.981 --> 00:57:54.210
focusing on going forward.

00:57:54.210 --> 00:57:57.311
- Thank you, so Andrew,
do you want to comment,

00:57:57.311 --> 00:58:01.230
because you do quite a lot of
lead for NHS England board.

00:58:01.230 --> 00:58:03.783
- So I think it's a
really good paper, Jan,

00:58:04.617 --> 00:58:07.783
and it will change our approach to this.

00:58:07.783 --> 00:58:11.148
And at the end of the day,
NHS England should be there

00:58:11.148 --> 00:58:13.231
to support organisations to improve.

00:58:13.231 --> 00:58:14.340
That's what this is about.

00:58:14.340 --> 00:58:16.920
It's not necessarily the regulatory bit

00:58:16.920 --> 00:58:18.320
and historically in the NHS,

00:58:19.221 --> 00:58:23.130
we've emphasised too much
on that in my opinion,

00:58:23.130 --> 00:58:25.350
however we've got both to discharge.

00:58:25.350 --> 00:58:29.640
But I think the improvement
bit is really key.

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:33.930
And NHS England making
the right diagnostic call

00:58:33.930 --> 00:58:38.700
around what's wrong with this
organisation is a key bit,

00:58:38.700 --> 00:58:41.120
'cause often we haven't
quite got that right

00:58:41.120 --> 00:58:44.580
in my experience at
some of these meetings,

00:58:44.580 --> 00:58:49.580
I also think that we have not held culture

00:58:50.800 --> 00:58:51.960
in the highest regard

00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:54.910
around why organisations fail

00:58:55.955 --> 00:58:58.385
and the relationship between the board

00:58:58.385 --> 00:59:00.840
and the clinicians,
which we take for granted

00:59:00.840 --> 00:59:02.670
in some situations.

00:59:02.670 --> 00:59:04.470
But actually that's the key.

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:07.680
And when we place chief
execs into these situations,

00:59:07.680 --> 00:59:09.540
newly appointed chief execs,

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:11.880
we should give them time and space.

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:15.540
It takes three to five years
to turn an organisation around

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:18.042
and the chief exec is one individual.

00:59:18.042 --> 00:59:21.750
And over time that individual's
got to get the team

00:59:21.750 --> 00:59:24.540
around them to transform
that organisation.

00:59:24.540 --> 00:59:26.760
All of that takes a lot of time.

00:59:26.760 --> 00:59:29.820
But if we've got confidence
in the people we appoint,

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.860
we should give them the space.

00:59:31.860 --> 00:59:36.036
I think on culture, the
bit that I would suggest

00:59:36.036 --> 00:59:38.340
is that we're clear around what's wrong,

00:59:38.340 --> 00:59:42.310
but we ask the board
to give us the markers

00:59:43.335 --> 00:59:44.730
of change over a period of time

00:59:44.730 --> 00:59:48.480
so we can monitor the progress.

00:59:48.480 --> 00:59:49.800
'Cause it does take time

00:59:49.800 --> 00:59:53.264
and you know, if you take say East Kent

00:59:53.264 --> 00:59:54.210
as a good example,

00:59:54.210 --> 00:59:57.450
I mean we are seeing
some positive changes now

00:59:57.450 --> 01:00:01.593
and you know, the chief
exec there has had what,

01:00:01.593 --> 01:00:03.180
three years in post

01:00:03.180 --> 01:00:06.021
and you know, she's making some really,

01:00:06.021 --> 01:00:07.213
really good progress

01:00:07.213 --> 01:00:10.920
and we should back those
individuals all the way

01:00:10.920 --> 01:00:13.080
providing we've got confidence in them.

01:00:13.080 --> 01:00:14.820
So I welcome this approach

01:00:14.820 --> 01:00:16.897
and I think it's a superb bit of work

01:00:16.897 --> 01:00:21.129
but I think NHS England has
got to get its act together

01:00:21.129 --> 01:00:22.350
a bit better in understanding

01:00:22.350 --> 01:00:24.090
what the fundamental issues are

01:00:24.090 --> 01:00:27.090
around what's wrong with
this particular organisation

01:00:27.090 --> 01:00:31.503
and giving the board space
and you know, time to improve.

01:00:32.384 --> 01:00:33.784
- Yeah, I think the diagnostic,

01:00:33.784 --> 01:00:35.463
Wol wants to come in the
diagnostics important.

01:00:35.463 --> 01:00:38.280
The phase I always used to
use was causes not symptoms.

01:00:38.280 --> 01:00:39.653
And actually that's what we've really got

01:00:39.653 --> 01:00:42.477
to find here, but Wol,
you want to come in.

01:00:42.477 --> 01:00:43.880
- Thank, sorry I forgot
to introduce myself.

01:00:43.880 --> 01:00:47.037
So Wol Kolade deputy chair
following Andrew Morris

01:00:47.037 --> 01:00:48.405
when he's such a wise old man

01:00:48.405 --> 01:00:50.073
in terms of it's really difficult

01:00:50.073 --> 01:00:51.503
but I actually have got a
slight different question.

01:00:52.632 --> 01:00:54.720
So in the private sector,

01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:58.094
when you have a sort of
turnaround process in place

01:00:58.094 --> 01:00:59.921
and you highlighted the fact

01:00:59.921 --> 01:01:01.830
that often you see change
in terms of leadership team.

01:01:01.830 --> 01:01:04.921
So chair, chief exec, finance director,

01:01:04.921 --> 01:01:07.240
the key or rate limited factor

01:01:08.148 --> 01:01:09.577
tends to be finding the talent.

01:01:09.577 --> 01:01:11.043
- [Emily] Yes.

01:01:11.043 --> 01:01:13.009
- So what you tend

01:01:13.009 --> 01:01:15.173
to do when you are finding
talent is take more risk.

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:18.730
And that means, you know, I
don't want to put someone in

01:01:18.730 --> 01:01:21.457
who's got exactly everything
I need to make this change.

01:01:21.457 --> 01:01:22.654
You actually say I have someone

01:01:22.654 --> 01:01:25.579
who has the potential to make this change.

01:01:25.579 --> 01:01:26.490
Can we just talk a bit about whether,

01:01:26.490 --> 01:01:28.406
use that approach,

01:01:28.406 --> 01:01:29.741
whether that's completely not consistent

01:01:29.741 --> 01:01:32.190
with this environment, I'm
just interested to know.

01:01:32.190 --> 01:01:33.023
- Yeah.

01:01:34.350 --> 01:01:36.705
Are you gathering questions chair?

01:01:36.705 --> 01:01:38.213
- Okay, yeah, I should be
gathering questions, sorry.

01:01:40.473 --> 01:01:41.306
- Good coaching.

01:01:41.306 --> 01:01:42.300
- Good coaching, very necessary.

01:01:42.300 --> 01:01:45.240
So we're going to go
Duncan and Amanda, Duncan.

01:01:45.240 --> 01:01:46.770
- Yeah, two points and one first

01:01:46.770 --> 01:01:49.800
just to say I think the value
of the improvement directors

01:01:49.800 --> 01:01:53.453
and the work that they do,
I think is having worked

01:01:53.453 --> 01:01:55.530
with a number of them now, I
think we should pay tribute

01:01:55.530 --> 01:01:58.763
to the work that they do and
colleagues in the regions

01:01:58.763 --> 01:02:00.143
as well that work alongside them.

01:02:00.143 --> 01:02:02.730
I guess my question was around learning,

01:02:02.730 --> 01:02:05.839
taking the learning that
comes out of the RSP

01:02:05.839 --> 01:02:08.830
across the entire NHS,
particularly as we think

01:02:09.696 --> 01:02:10.902
about how do we get upstream

01:02:10.902 --> 01:02:12.383
of some of the issues and the findings.

01:02:12.383 --> 01:02:13.574
We've been trying to do that

01:02:13.574 --> 01:02:15.643
as part of the maternity
safety support programme

01:02:15.643 --> 01:02:18.317
and working alongside you and colleagues,

01:02:18.317 --> 01:02:20.445
but really kind of making
sure that we get up stream

01:02:20.445 --> 01:02:21.780
and actually we don't
get into the needs to

01:02:21.780 --> 01:02:25.020
for organisations to go
into RSP in the first place.

01:02:25.020 --> 01:02:26.610
We can take the wider learning.

01:02:26.610 --> 01:02:29.723
- Okay, Amanda, do you want
to do your our question?

01:02:29.723 --> 01:02:31.463
- I've got questions as
well, if that's all right.

01:02:31.463 --> 01:02:32.660
- Yeah, course it is.

01:02:32.660 --> 01:02:34.901
- So comment, just to say to Jan,

01:02:34.901 --> 01:02:36.190
thank you very much indeed for this

01:02:36.190 --> 01:02:37.392
I thought excellent paper

01:02:37.392 --> 01:02:38.340
and for indeed coming in,
offering your expertise

01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:40.320
to help lead the work,

01:02:40.320 --> 01:02:42.330
two things that I strongly agree with.

01:02:42.330 --> 01:02:45.390
One is this point you've
made about improvement

01:02:45.390 --> 01:02:47.551
and I just wanted to
pick up on Andrew's point

01:02:47.551 --> 01:02:49.463
'cause I think it's just a question for me

01:02:49.463 --> 01:02:51.197
about how we really measure that.

01:02:51.197 --> 01:02:54.003
Because in objective terms,
things can take a while

01:02:54.003 --> 01:02:55.303
before you start to see that,

01:02:55.303 --> 01:02:56.917
you know, coming through in the data.

01:02:56.917 --> 01:02:59.352
But actually there can
be a lot that's going on

01:02:59.352 --> 01:03:01.197
sort of under the headlines

01:03:01.197 --> 01:03:03.797
that I think we sometimes
struggle to really be able

01:03:04.727 --> 01:03:08.833
to pull that out and
really see what's going on.

01:03:10.123 --> 01:03:10.956
So how would we measure it

01:03:10.956 --> 01:03:14.162
and how would we account for,
if you like your point Andrew,

01:03:14.162 --> 01:03:16.125
about it takes time so that you know,

01:03:16.125 --> 01:03:17.610
things are moving in the right direction

01:03:17.610 --> 01:03:19.890
and you can therefore sort
of stick with it for longer

01:03:19.890 --> 01:03:21.720
and have the confidence to do so.

01:03:21.720 --> 01:03:23.997
Second point I again
strongly agree with is,

01:03:23.997 --> 01:03:25.897
and again I think Secretary of State's

01:03:26.883 --> 01:03:27.960
been clear about this, we
need to back the leaders

01:03:27.960 --> 01:03:28.930
who are going to go

01:03:29.793 --> 01:03:31.425
to some of our most challenged places

01:03:31.425 --> 01:03:33.099
and we need to give them
the support they need

01:03:33.099 --> 01:03:35.687
to be successful because
I completely agree,

01:03:35.687 --> 01:03:37.341
this is not about heroic individuals

01:03:37.341 --> 01:03:39.009
who can sort of make magic happen.

01:03:39.009 --> 01:03:40.197
This is about teams

01:03:40.197 --> 01:03:43.110
and it's also about the
right support around them

01:03:43.110 --> 01:03:46.468
and the right long term
sort of investment.

01:03:46.468 --> 01:03:47.301
I don't necessarily mean in money terms,

01:03:47.301 --> 01:03:49.980
but I mean in sort of time and energy

01:03:49.980 --> 01:03:53.700
and the wider partnership coming
around those organisations.

01:03:53.700 --> 01:03:57.526
So again, just be grateful
for your thoughts Jan,

01:03:57.526 --> 01:03:59.370
on how we get the balance
right between one hand

01:03:59.370 --> 01:04:01.320
of course appropriate accountability,

01:04:01.320 --> 01:04:04.325
but on the other, kind of
really backing the people

01:04:04.325 --> 01:04:05.158
who are doing the hardest jobs.

01:04:05.158 --> 01:04:08.490
And but my question was also
just about finance and quality

01:04:08.490 --> 01:04:12.120
because RSP folded two previous regimes

01:04:12.120 --> 01:04:14.493
that were the finance special measures

01:04:14.493 --> 01:04:15.326
and quality special measures.

01:04:15.326 --> 01:04:18.130
The logic being partly that by having them

01:04:18.992 --> 01:04:21.417
as two separate things,
those organisations

01:04:21.417 --> 01:04:23.100
that were almost inevitably
struggling with both

01:04:23.100 --> 01:04:24.300
could end up being given

01:04:24.300 --> 01:04:28.290
sort of two competing sets of directions.

01:04:28.290 --> 01:04:30.510
So I think that was the right idea,

01:04:30.510 --> 01:04:32.606
but my question is really,

01:04:32.606 --> 01:04:35.370
have we got that right yet

01:04:35.370 --> 01:04:38.460
as a way of kind of getting
the right focus on both?

01:04:38.460 --> 01:04:41.820
Because as we look to the future
that focus on productivity,

01:04:41.820 --> 01:04:45.300
on value for money is going
to be absolutely crucial.

01:04:45.300 --> 01:04:48.360
But so is the focus on quality and access

01:04:48.360 --> 01:04:51.483
and improving services for patients.

01:04:53.184 --> 01:04:55.209
- So do you want to have a go at those?

01:04:55.209 --> 01:04:57.082
Who wants to go for Emily?

01:04:57.082 --> 01:04:58.830
Jan, you say yours

01:04:58.830 --> 01:05:01.193
and then hand to Jan what you want.

01:05:01.193 --> 01:05:02.520
- Yeah, I mean we work as one

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:04.496
so we can both.

01:05:04.496 --> 01:05:05.958
- Can double team it.

01:05:05.958 --> 01:05:10.272
- I've probably already
expressed an opinion

01:05:10.272 --> 01:05:12.912
on the finance and quality piece,

01:05:12.912 --> 01:05:16.039
I might let Jan pick that one.

01:05:16.039 --> 01:05:17.960
I was just going to start
talking on the leadership one.

01:05:17.960 --> 01:05:19.260
Which several people have picked up,

01:05:19.260 --> 01:05:20.640
but just sorry, quickly Duncan,

01:05:20.640 --> 01:05:22.413
on your upstream point.

01:05:24.585 --> 01:05:25.773
Completely agree

01:05:25.773 --> 01:05:27.300
and that has been a key discussion

01:05:27.300 --> 01:05:30.870
with the regional directors
who all have their own ways

01:05:30.870 --> 01:05:34.800
of sort of identifying the
systems they're worried about.

01:05:34.800 --> 01:05:37.590
But we may not yet have
spotted for various reasons

01:05:37.590 --> 01:05:41.301
and one of my goals with all of the work

01:05:41.301 --> 01:05:44.765
that we're doing on data is
to get better at figuring out

01:05:44.765 --> 01:05:45.598
what are the metrics that tend

01:05:45.598 --> 01:05:47.670
to go off sooner rather than later,

01:05:47.670 --> 01:05:49.641
as you say, in trying to address it.

01:05:49.641 --> 01:05:52.777
And of course all the
work that Miranda Carter

01:05:52.777 --> 01:05:53.880
and and Steve's team has done

01:05:53.880 --> 01:05:56.190
to develop the new oversight framework,

01:05:56.190 --> 01:05:57.990
I think is hopefully building on that

01:05:57.990 --> 01:06:00.783
and they'll no doubt be
more improvements to come.

01:06:00.783 --> 01:06:03.600
On the leadership point,

01:06:03.600 --> 01:06:06.390
we spent some time talking to people

01:06:06.390 --> 01:06:08.700
who were the kind of people we tend to ask

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:11.910
to take on these challenges
earlier in the year,

01:06:11.910 --> 01:06:13.450
which is work that Anne Eden

01:06:14.361 --> 01:06:17.551
and Lesley Watts have sponsored,
which has been brilliant.

01:06:17.551 --> 01:06:20.490
And those leaders
mentioned several things.

01:06:20.490 --> 01:06:23.580
The first thing was about backing them,

01:06:23.580 --> 01:06:25.387
it was about support.

01:06:25.387 --> 01:06:27.046
It was the point Andrew made about,

01:06:27.046 --> 01:06:28.304
look it's going to take
three to five years

01:06:28.304 --> 01:06:30.810
so when the first thing doesn't
happen or I lift up a stone

01:06:30.810 --> 01:06:33.768
and it turns out there's yet
another thing underneath it

01:06:33.768 --> 01:06:35.841
that you didn't already know,
like you need to back me

01:06:35.841 --> 01:06:37.380
because it's going to take
me a while to do that.

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:40.560
And they did comment on
the sort of personal risk

01:06:40.560 --> 01:06:42.300
that people feel when they
take these kind of jobs.

01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:44.950
I don't think we have an answer to that

01:06:44.950 --> 01:06:46.320
because obviously it's quite a challenging

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:48.467
external environment and that front.

01:06:48.467 --> 01:06:50.813
But I think that is something
we need to think about.

01:06:52.100 --> 01:06:54.090
They talked about incentives

01:06:54.090 --> 01:06:56.776
and that's often a question
we get asked is like,

01:06:56.776 --> 01:06:58.470
can we sort of pay people
danger money effectively?

01:06:58.470 --> 01:07:00.090
I'm not saying people are
going to object to that,

01:07:00.090 --> 01:07:02.160
but that wasn't what
people were asking for.

01:07:02.160 --> 01:07:05.369
They were asking for support
more than anything else

01:07:05.369 --> 01:07:07.461
in practical support also
to change their teams

01:07:07.461 --> 01:07:09.540
because it can be quite hard sometimes,

01:07:09.540 --> 01:07:10.590
particularly when you're new

01:07:10.590 --> 01:07:13.800
to, you know, make the right decisions

01:07:13.800 --> 01:07:15.974
in a new team environment.

01:07:15.974 --> 01:07:18.660
So there were a few things I wanted,

01:07:18.660 --> 01:07:20.413
those were a few things I wanted

01:07:20.413 --> 01:07:21.632
to pull out of that diagnostic.

01:07:21.632 --> 01:07:23.100
Jan may want to mention others.

01:07:23.100 --> 01:07:25.529
The other point I wanted to pick up,

01:07:25.529 --> 01:07:27.618
Wol, I think it was partly
from you, is the work

01:07:27.618 --> 01:07:29.522
that we've been doing
with Nivina's team though

01:07:29.522 --> 01:07:32.130
on trying to identify
earlier in their careers

01:07:32.130 --> 01:07:35.710
people who have the right
sort of intrinsic capabilities

01:07:36.663 --> 01:07:39.510
for this, but don't yet
have the right experience.

01:07:39.510 --> 01:07:40.620
And then supporting them

01:07:40.620 --> 01:07:43.394
to make sure they do
have the right experience

01:07:43.394 --> 01:07:44.594
and the right career path.

01:07:44.594 --> 01:07:47.460
Particularly things like
brilliant clinical leaders

01:07:47.460 --> 01:07:49.740
who need broader general
management experience.

01:07:49.740 --> 01:07:52.850
'Cause one of the things we're
identifying from the work

01:07:52.850 --> 01:07:54.554
Jan's doing on the diagnostic
is the absolute centrality

01:07:54.554 --> 01:07:59.010
of the medical director it
being in the right space

01:07:59.010 --> 01:08:00.370
with the CEO, the CNO

01:08:01.285 --> 01:08:04.263
and the CFO to absolutely
drive that performance.

01:08:05.180 --> 01:08:07.455
So to pause there for a second,

01:08:07.455 --> 01:08:09.472
I haven't covered
everything, but Jan will want

01:08:09.472 --> 01:08:11.458
to elaborate from her perspective.

01:08:11.458 --> 01:08:13.270
- Yeah, I just want to, I'll start

01:08:14.234 --> 01:08:15.810
with the last question
and then work backwards.

01:08:15.810 --> 01:08:18.720
So I've yet to find somewhere

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:22.050
that really has finances
nailed without quality issues

01:08:22.050 --> 01:08:24.503
or quality issues without finance.

01:08:24.503 --> 01:08:25.440
They are intrinsically linked

01:08:25.440 --> 01:08:27.180
and I think we've got to get our heads

01:08:27.180 --> 01:08:29.490
around even though we are seeing one thing

01:08:29.490 --> 01:08:32.634
that we should probably
explore what else is happening.

01:08:32.634 --> 01:08:34.650
I think that is probably how
we should think about this.

01:08:34.650 --> 01:08:37.890
And that's why that quadrant approach

01:08:37.890 --> 01:08:38.740
is so important

01:08:39.747 --> 01:08:41.641
because it isn't just
this one area doing well.

01:08:41.641 --> 01:08:44.280
It's the interactions between
them and the balancing of risk

01:08:44.280 --> 01:08:47.940
and understanding that
conscious decisions individuals

01:08:47.940 --> 01:08:51.340
and boards are making in
order to balance that risk off

01:08:52.191 --> 01:08:54.087
between quality, finance and performance.

01:08:54.087 --> 01:08:56.312
And I, and I think rather than just look,

01:08:56.312 --> 01:08:57.817
we're determined not just to say

01:08:57.817 --> 01:08:58.650
here's the chief executive chair,

01:08:58.650 --> 01:09:00.990
we are saying actually it's a team effort

01:09:00.990 --> 01:09:03.353
and if you get that balance right

01:09:03.353 --> 01:09:05.564
and that could take longer,

01:09:05.564 --> 01:09:07.560
but actually, that will
drive the sustainability.

01:09:07.560 --> 01:09:09.060
So I think Amanda, to your point,

01:09:09.060 --> 01:09:11.730
I think you've got to keep it together

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:13.260
because it won't just be one thing,

01:09:13.260 --> 01:09:15.570
but let's not just make it quality

01:09:15.570 --> 01:09:17.160
and finance, let's make it,

01:09:17.160 --> 01:09:19.560
how do you get the clinical change

01:09:19.560 --> 01:09:22.860
along with the operations et cetera in.

01:09:22.860 --> 01:09:25.110
In terms of measuring,

01:09:25.110 --> 01:09:26.400
I think this is really interesting

01:09:26.400 --> 01:09:30.360
because I've looked at charts forever

01:09:30.360 --> 01:09:32.190
and we get more reporting
than ever before, don't we?

01:09:32.190 --> 01:09:33.720
About is this getting better, is it worse?

01:09:33.720 --> 01:09:36.870
And I can't emphasise enough,
we have to start looking

01:09:36.870 --> 01:09:41.370
and continually looking at
the statistical improvement

01:09:41.370 --> 01:09:45.002
over a longer period of
time rather than just saying

01:09:45.002 --> 01:09:46.935
what was the performance at this point?

01:09:46.935 --> 01:09:48.180
Because that is what causes momentum

01:09:48.180 --> 01:09:51.240
and that's what gives
confidence in organisations.

01:09:51.240 --> 01:09:53.160
And what I would say is we quite often go,

01:09:53.160 --> 01:09:55.350
this one thing is going well.

01:09:55.350 --> 01:09:58.030
The key to what we're talking about

01:09:58.970 --> 01:10:01.420
with momentum is can they
do more than one thing well

01:10:01.420 --> 01:10:02.940
at the same time and improve,

01:10:02.940 --> 01:10:05.760
because otherwise it's that
good old whack-a-mole isn't it?

01:10:05.760 --> 01:10:07.230
If we are not careful.

01:10:07.230 --> 01:10:09.543
So I think when we're
talking about momentum,

01:10:09.543 --> 01:10:10.376
it's not just how do you do one thing,

01:10:10.376 --> 01:10:15.030
it's how do you do more than
one thing together better.

01:10:15.030 --> 01:10:18.881
I think just on the point about leaders

01:10:18.881 --> 01:10:20.162
I think is really important,

01:10:20.162 --> 01:10:24.210
my personal opinion on this is,

01:10:24.210 --> 01:10:26.760
it usually takes a
different kind of person

01:10:26.760 --> 01:10:30.090
to maintain a good
organisation and make it grow

01:10:30.090 --> 01:10:32.910
than actually to go into
something quite difficult.

01:10:32.910 --> 01:10:35.280
Be able to prioritise really quickly,

01:10:35.280 --> 01:10:37.530
get to the nub of it, diagnose it.

01:10:37.530 --> 01:10:39.330
And I think we need to think about that

01:10:39.330 --> 01:10:40.981
when we're talking about this

01:10:40.981 --> 01:10:43.610
because we assume that it's
a person that can go in

01:10:43.610 --> 01:10:46.130
and do, but it might be that
actually we need a tag team

01:10:46.130 --> 01:10:48.090
of a longer term solution

01:10:48.090 --> 01:10:51.150
but actually somebody who
can go in and support people.

01:10:51.150 --> 01:10:53.430
'Cause it is difficult
and it is a skillset

01:10:53.430 --> 01:10:55.320
and it's tiring and we have

01:10:55.320 --> 01:10:56.730
to recognise that for people.

01:10:56.730 --> 01:10:58.620
But it's really inspiring as well

01:10:58.620 --> 01:11:01.125
because these people are usually people

01:11:01.125 --> 01:11:01.958
who can really listen

01:11:01.958 --> 01:11:04.020
and I think, you know, we
shouldn't underplay things

01:11:04.020 --> 01:11:06.300
like the staff surveys and
how we are really listening

01:11:06.300 --> 01:11:08.280
to those to get that momentum.

01:11:08.280 --> 01:11:11.220
But that might be a
different kind of person

01:11:11.220 --> 01:11:13.747
than we thought about in the past.

01:11:13.747 --> 01:11:17.066
- Thank you, I'm going to move
us on if that's all right.

01:11:17.066 --> 01:11:19.006
I think as Andrew said,

01:11:19.006 --> 01:11:22.263
and Andrew you chair most
of the board to boards

01:11:22.263 --> 01:11:26.104
on behalf of NHS England
in the RSP programme.

01:11:26.104 --> 01:11:28.020
I do some of them but a
fraction of what you do.

01:11:28.020 --> 01:11:30.300
I think they're a really
good intervention actually.

01:11:30.300 --> 01:11:33.990
They're supportive,
which is the right style

01:11:33.990 --> 01:11:35.190
and they are diagnostic

01:11:35.190 --> 01:11:36.630
and we can improve our diagnostics

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:39.120
to find, you know, the true causes.

01:11:39.120 --> 01:11:41.728
I think that's been a very
good conversation, thank you.

01:11:41.728 --> 01:11:42.900
And Jan, thank you for
leading in this space.

01:11:42.900 --> 01:11:44.954
Very good, thank you very much.

01:11:44.954 --> 01:11:48.242
Julian, can we go on
to a financial update?

01:11:48.242 --> 01:11:53.242
- Sure, so the paper presented,

01:11:53.550 --> 01:11:54.993
the things to bring out in it

01:11:54.993 --> 01:11:59.993
are, number one, we are currently working

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:05.010
to a total budget for NHS England

01:12:05.010 --> 01:12:09.722
of 186.6 billion pounds.

01:12:09.722 --> 01:12:12.180
That is updated following the budget

01:12:12.180 --> 01:12:16.680
for the cost of the pay deals

01:12:16.680 --> 01:12:20.190
that the government
finalised over the summer

01:12:20.190 --> 01:12:23.520
and the extra funding

01:12:23.520 --> 01:12:25.230
provided by the treasury

01:12:25.230 --> 01:12:28.440
or at least them having
fixed the amount of funding

01:12:28.440 --> 01:12:32.258
that is available for elective activity.

01:12:32.258 --> 01:12:37.258
It does not yet, you know, well actually,

01:12:38.679 --> 01:12:42.423
that is the budget as we
have it for this year.

01:12:43.456 --> 01:12:45.570
That's important just to understand

01:12:45.570 --> 01:12:48.010
when we then come onto
just looking ahead a bit

01:12:49.365 --> 01:12:50.198
to next year.

01:12:51.635 --> 01:12:55.323
Overall, at the end of month seven,

01:12:58.473 --> 01:13:00.550
we are 725 million off plan

01:13:02.137 --> 01:13:03.540
that is about 150 million worse

01:13:03.540 --> 01:13:08.540
than this time last month when
we were looking at month six,

01:13:11.880 --> 01:13:13.710
basically the kind of run rate

01:13:13.710 --> 01:13:16.320
of cost expenditure within
systems has improved

01:13:16.320 --> 01:13:19.110
but not by as much as they were planning.

01:13:19.110 --> 01:13:22.860
So although you know, on
the one hand an improvement,

01:13:22.860 --> 01:13:25.530
on the other hand, still by not as much

01:13:25.530 --> 01:13:28.653
as is needed.

01:13:30.353 --> 01:13:34.754
I will say we have now
though played in the funding

01:13:34.754 --> 01:13:36.111
for the pay deals.

01:13:36.111 --> 01:13:38.212
We are clear that, you
know, we are covering

01:13:38.212 --> 01:13:40.560
the cost of over performance on activity.

01:13:40.560 --> 01:13:42.804
We have funded the direct
cost of industrial action.

01:13:42.804 --> 01:13:47.138
So you know, we have sort
of met the conditions

01:13:47.138 --> 01:13:48.690
we set out at the beginning of the year

01:13:48.690 --> 01:13:52.161
when we agreed plans
with boards and systems

01:13:52.161 --> 01:13:53.861
and therefore, you know,
our expectation really

01:13:53.861 --> 01:13:58.844
is that then ICBs and providers

01:13:58.844 --> 01:14:00.794
delivered the financial plans

01:14:00.794 --> 01:14:04.363
that their boards agreed
that they submitted to us

01:14:04.363 --> 01:14:05.813
and that we agreed with them.

01:14:07.058 --> 01:14:08.642
Now despite the fact we
are slightly off plan,

01:14:08.642 --> 01:14:12.386
there are a lot of
positive things happening

01:14:12.386 --> 01:14:13.473
at the end of month seven,

01:14:13.473 --> 01:14:16.110
people have delivered four billion pounds

01:14:16.110 --> 01:14:19.230
of cost improvement plans against a goal

01:14:19.230 --> 01:14:24.230
to get to about 9.3 by the
end of the financial year.

01:14:25.208 --> 01:14:27.390
That is significantly more than last year

01:14:27.390 --> 01:14:30.890
even though it is about
two or 300 million less

01:14:30.890 --> 01:14:32.744
than they had been planning to do.

01:14:32.744 --> 01:14:34.255
So you can see the sort of stretch

01:14:34.255 --> 01:14:35.456
that people are putting in,

01:14:35.456 --> 01:14:39.690
but you know, we're not
quite holding to the goal.

01:14:39.690 --> 01:14:42.210
We can see agency spend is down,

01:14:42.210 --> 01:14:44.910
I mean we have spent
600 million pounds less

01:14:44.910 --> 01:14:49.910
on agency staffing for
the period to the end

01:14:49.912 --> 01:14:52.340
of month seven this year

01:14:52.340 --> 01:14:54.158
compared to the comparable period last

01:14:54.158 --> 01:14:56.590
and you know, currently we'll be on track

01:14:56.590 --> 01:15:01.590
to be somewhere between 2.2 and
2.3 billion pounds of spend.

01:15:01.659 --> 01:15:03.630
So that's like about one and
a half billion pounds lower

01:15:03.630 --> 01:15:07.800
than we were spending two years ago.

01:15:07.800 --> 01:15:10.995
I mean the thing now is we
really need to turn our attention

01:15:10.995 --> 01:15:13.596
to total temporary staffing spend

01:15:13.596 --> 01:15:17.709
given that, you know, still
substantive staff recruitment

01:15:17.709 --> 01:15:18.542
is going well.

01:15:21.330 --> 01:15:24.150
Productivity, we'll come to
it in a more detailed paper

01:15:24.150 --> 01:15:26.903
is up again year on year.

01:15:26.903 --> 01:15:28.890
So we can see continued recovery

01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:31.820
up to end of August including the impact

01:15:31.820 --> 01:15:34.531
of the strike in the early summer.

01:15:34.531 --> 01:15:37.154
It's about 1.8% higher than again

01:15:37.154 --> 01:15:40.083
for the same period last year.

01:15:40.980 --> 01:15:43.230
You know, we have flagged
that we are very concerned

01:15:43.230 --> 01:15:45.900
about the performance of some systems.

01:15:45.900 --> 01:15:49.200
We launched sort of a programme

01:15:49.200 --> 01:15:53.145
to properly investigate
whether people had plans,

01:15:53.145 --> 01:15:54.368
whether they're credible

01:15:54.368 --> 01:15:56.100
and if they didn't, to
do something about it.

01:15:56.100 --> 01:15:59.700
And you know, for most of the systems

01:15:59.700 --> 01:16:03.480
with which we have engaged,
we have seen an improvement

01:16:03.480 --> 01:16:06.121
about like sort of 190 million pounds

01:16:06.121 --> 01:16:07.579
in their expenditure run rate,

01:16:07.579 --> 01:16:08.640
which is better than the average

01:16:08.640 --> 01:16:10.620
in those systems where we haven't done

01:16:10.620 --> 01:16:13.686
the sort of intensive investigation.

01:16:13.686 --> 01:16:16.503
So I think that is like a
positive sign of the impact.

01:16:17.852 --> 01:16:22.640
But we do remain substantially
concerned about a few systems

01:16:22.640 --> 01:16:24.140
where I think the evidence is,

01:16:26.460 --> 01:16:28.020
that their plans aren't robust

01:16:28.020 --> 01:16:30.400
and that they're not turning it around

01:16:31.266 --> 01:16:33.780
and as per, in fact the
conversation we have just had,

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:36.962
you know, we've just had,
we will need to look at,

01:16:36.962 --> 01:16:39.600
you know, what the next steps are

01:16:39.600 --> 01:16:43.248
to I mean effectively achieve turnaround,

01:16:43.248 --> 01:16:44.452
not just this year

01:16:44.452 --> 01:16:47.373
but actually to put people
back on a sustainable path.

01:16:49.199 --> 01:16:51.183
I mean overall despite that position,

01:16:52.820 --> 01:16:53.940
as I look at total spending

01:16:53.940 --> 01:16:58.300
and spending on medicines
and training budgets

01:16:59.191 --> 01:17:00.863
and you know various other things,

01:17:01.976 --> 01:17:03.450
I'm still at this point confident

01:17:03.450 --> 01:17:06.750
we will live within the mandate.

01:17:06.750 --> 01:17:09.270
But look, it is going to be very tight

01:17:09.270 --> 01:17:13.206
and we are looking for systems

01:17:13.206 --> 01:17:15.647
to basically deliver the plans.

01:17:15.647 --> 01:17:18.210
They have said accepting
that I think there are some

01:17:18.210 --> 01:17:20.552
who won't and as I said, we are looking

01:17:20.552 --> 01:17:23.880
at like what the next
appropriate steps are to take.

01:17:23.880 --> 01:17:26.490
And as I think Andrew was saying earlier,

01:17:26.490 --> 01:17:28.080
I mean our focus has to be,

01:17:28.080 --> 01:17:31.760
does the board as a whole have a plan?

01:17:31.760 --> 01:17:34.871
Do they understand it and
are they implementing it?

01:17:34.871 --> 01:17:36.990
This is not just the job
of the finance director,

01:17:36.990 --> 01:17:38.313
this is the whole team.

01:17:41.454 --> 01:17:43.319
- Can I just, Jan, I
know you've got to run,

01:17:43.319 --> 01:17:45.094
thank you very much for what you're doing

01:17:45.094 --> 01:17:47.123
and thank you for coming
to the board, thank you.

01:17:48.237 --> 01:17:50.413
- I would just say on capital
briefly, we are sort of,

01:17:50.413 --> 01:17:52.764
you know, I mean people
are basically on track

01:17:52.764 --> 01:17:54.783
to spend pretty much
the whole of the budget

01:17:54.783 --> 01:17:58.534
we have given them and
we are working through

01:17:58.534 --> 01:17:59.640
to make sure we maximise the opportunity

01:17:59.640 --> 01:18:03.249
of all the capital available
to the Department of Health.

01:18:03.249 --> 01:18:07.378
Clearly it's whatever the date is,

01:18:07.378 --> 01:18:08.591
it's the 5th of December.

01:18:08.591 --> 01:18:10.582
So we're approaching that time of the year

01:18:10.582 --> 01:18:12.210
where people wait with bated breath

01:18:12.210 --> 01:18:15.600
for us to issue the kind of
planning guidance for next year.

01:18:15.600 --> 01:18:18.690
We are still in discussions

01:18:18.690 --> 01:18:21.030
with both the department

01:18:21.030 --> 01:18:23.650
and the government on the final budget.

01:18:23.650 --> 01:18:25.390
There are still some important details

01:18:25.390 --> 01:18:29.783
to be finalised including the
funding for employer NICs.

01:18:31.042 --> 01:18:31.875
So all of that is ongoing.

01:18:31.875 --> 01:18:33.300
We're in discussions

01:18:33.300 --> 01:18:36.031
about what we think it can achieve

01:18:36.031 --> 01:18:39.180
consistent with the
government's priorities.

01:18:39.180 --> 01:18:42.166
We are still aiming to
get planning guidance out

01:18:42.166 --> 01:18:47.166
before Christmas, you know,
days rather than hours

01:18:49.950 --> 01:18:52.440
before Christmas would be good.

01:18:52.440 --> 01:18:56.007
And we will be talking to
system leaders about it.

01:18:56.007 --> 01:18:58.092
I mean I think as Amanda
has said in her introductory

01:18:58.092 --> 01:19:00.060
remarks and indeed
elsewhere, look, next year

01:19:00.060 --> 01:19:04.290
will financially be
tougher than this year.

01:19:04.290 --> 01:19:06.934
You know, once you've
accounted for inflation,

01:19:06.934 --> 01:19:11.110
you know spending growth
will be about 2% next year

01:19:12.087 --> 01:19:13.200
and that, you know, has to cover

01:19:13.200 --> 01:19:17.820
and the government is
clear any paid settlement,

01:19:17.820 --> 01:19:20.254
all activity that we are doing

01:19:20.254 --> 01:19:21.449
and any other pressures

01:19:21.449 --> 01:19:24.003
that arise, it will be tougher.

01:19:24.854 --> 01:19:26.970
And it is absolutely why people need

01:19:26.970 --> 01:19:30.930
to, you know, get their spend rate

01:19:30.930 --> 01:19:34.051
on a path which is sustainable.

01:19:34.051 --> 01:19:35.940
So even if they've got
problems through this year,

01:19:35.940 --> 01:19:37.110
they really do need to focus on

01:19:37.110 --> 01:19:39.010
where they get to by the end of March.

01:19:40.502 --> 01:19:42.750
- Thank you, mean that message

01:19:42.750 --> 01:19:44.070
is a really important one

01:19:44.070 --> 01:19:47.463
out clearly into the systems
about this coming year,

01:19:49.147 --> 01:19:50.625
well this year is a tough year,

01:19:50.625 --> 01:19:53.825
but actually next year
if anything is tougher

01:19:53.825 --> 01:19:56.040
and if I can just share

01:19:56.040 --> 01:19:59.800
and test with you if I
might, what's on my mind

01:20:01.294 --> 01:20:02.940
And it partly actually
having had conversations

01:20:02.940 --> 01:20:05.415
in the CFO advisory group.

01:20:05.415 --> 01:20:08.165
So other colleagues around
the table who sat with that.

01:20:09.520 --> 01:20:12.120
So I always carry certain sort of metrics.

01:20:12.120 --> 01:20:13.560
One which is quite interesting

01:20:13.560 --> 01:20:17.613
is that every 1% increase
in pay is about 1.3 billion.

01:20:19.066 --> 01:20:22.025
I'm also struck by that metric you gave

01:20:22.025 --> 01:20:23.190
about how hard the NHS is working.

01:20:23.190 --> 01:20:26.610
So nine billion of
essentially cost efficiencies

01:20:26.610 --> 01:20:30.227
targeted for this year, 24, 25,

01:20:30.227 --> 01:20:31.350
which is up on the seven billion

01:20:32.232 --> 01:20:34.023
achieved actually in 23 and 24.

01:20:35.782 --> 01:20:37.871
And actually the overall
productivity we're running at

01:20:37.871 --> 01:20:41.250
are pretty good levels actually
currently at about 1.8%

01:20:41.250 --> 01:20:44.823
we think is the likely
productivity benefit we get.

01:20:45.760 --> 01:20:47.010
So they're quite
important sort of dynamics

01:20:47.010 --> 01:20:49.593
as you come into this.

01:20:50.724 --> 01:20:52.408
But what what I think really is important

01:20:52.408 --> 01:20:53.722
and I just want to make sure

01:20:53.722 --> 01:20:57.166
that my another non-exec
colleagues understanding

01:20:57.166 --> 01:20:58.355
is right here.

01:20:58.355 --> 01:20:59.790
If you go back and focus on the 22 billion

01:20:59.790 --> 01:21:02.880
or so, the additional
and very well, you know,

01:21:02.880 --> 01:21:06.857
really welcome commitment from government

01:21:06.857 --> 01:21:08.070
and from the Secretary of State,

01:21:08.070 --> 01:21:11.190
which was a really
important clear commitment

01:21:11.190 --> 01:21:13.260
to help us deal with actually continuing

01:21:13.260 --> 01:21:15.090
exponential growth in demand.

01:21:15.090 --> 01:21:17.460
Which is what we're seeing.

01:21:17.460 --> 01:21:19.623
If I look at how that plays out,

01:21:19.623 --> 01:21:24.190
to an extent it's front loaded into 24, 25

01:21:25.230 --> 01:21:28.300
and within that, the numbers I hold

01:21:29.185 --> 01:21:34.020
is that just the pay awards
already agreed to given

01:21:34.020 --> 01:21:34.998
eats up about eight

01:21:34.998 --> 01:21:39.705
and a half billion,
non-pay awards is about one

01:21:39.705 --> 01:21:41.441
and a quarter billion I think.

01:21:41.441 --> 01:21:42.274
And then there is this pension valuation

01:21:42.274 --> 01:21:43.770
you talked to us about,

01:21:43.770 --> 01:21:46.260
which is an obvious necessary
pensions revaluation,

01:21:46.260 --> 01:21:49.307
it's necessary and wholly
appropriate accounting,

01:21:49.307 --> 01:21:50.490
but it's another two billion.

01:21:50.490 --> 01:21:52.560
And then other areas not captured

01:21:52.560 --> 01:21:57.560
just in those three pockets
of money would be the cost

01:21:57.900 --> 01:22:00.517
of elective recovery running at 118%.

01:22:00.517 --> 01:22:04.064
We were budgeted about 107%

01:22:04.064 --> 01:22:05.522
I think at the start of the year.

01:22:05.522 --> 01:22:07.170
So the machine in terms of
value weighted electives

01:22:07.170 --> 01:22:10.977
is running stronger and
that does cost more money.

01:22:10.977 --> 01:22:13.726
So that is a significant extra drag.

01:22:13.726 --> 01:22:16.620
Then clearly the annual
growth we get just in demand

01:22:16.620 --> 01:22:19.200
actually again that will be some billions

01:22:19.200 --> 01:22:21.422
in terms of additional costs.

01:22:21.422 --> 01:22:22.980
So you see those and you can see

01:22:22.980 --> 01:22:25.530
that there's a quite large amount

01:22:25.530 --> 01:22:28.620
of the 22 billion
essentially already absorbed

01:22:28.620 --> 01:22:30.310
in current activity levels

01:22:31.209 --> 01:22:33.371
and medicine increases
and in pay increases

01:22:33.371 --> 01:22:37.200
and inflation in the
first of the two years,

01:22:37.200 --> 01:22:39.093
which the 22 billion arrives.

01:22:40.030 --> 01:22:41.850
So we then look at next
year where we can see

01:22:41.850 --> 01:22:44.100
for the department of which we are clearly

01:22:44.100 --> 01:22:47.463
a major part of the cost,
an additional 10 billion,

01:22:48.334 --> 01:22:51.090
which probably gives you some indication

01:22:51.090 --> 01:22:52.917
of how we're trying to manage,

01:22:52.917 --> 01:22:55.465
you know, what's the proportion of the 22

01:22:55.465 --> 01:22:56.678
that's front loaded,

01:22:56.678 --> 01:22:58.980
as long as we can manage those pressures.

01:22:58.980 --> 01:23:01.379
And we don't have a finalised budget yet.

01:23:01.379 --> 01:23:04.094
And that I think is where
you and Amanda and Emily

01:23:04.094 --> 01:23:06.114
and other execs around the table

01:23:06.114 --> 01:23:07.860
are engaged in a really interesting

01:23:07.860 --> 01:23:12.243
and challenging set of
choices around that money.

01:23:13.138 --> 01:23:15.087
But if you think about
that, then 10 billion,

01:23:15.087 --> 01:23:20.087
that has got to deal with
pay award, it's got to deal.

01:23:23.175 --> 01:23:24.371
And again, it goes back to that

01:23:24.371 --> 01:23:26.398
for every 1%, there's 1.3 billion,

01:23:26.398 --> 01:23:29.583
it's got to deal with non-pay inflation,

01:23:30.973 --> 01:23:32.892
it's got to deal with demand growth.

01:23:32.892 --> 01:23:34.650
So what sort of demand
growth would we get?

01:23:34.650 --> 01:23:37.170
And what it tells you actually is that

01:23:37.170 --> 01:23:39.570
it's a really important
commitment from the government

01:23:39.570 --> 01:23:42.330
that they've given us,
but actually decisions

01:23:42.330 --> 01:23:44.926
already made and in run
rates already in play.

01:23:44.926 --> 01:23:48.870
It strikes me that's
why going into next year

01:23:48.870 --> 01:23:52.887
we've got a worse position,
worse is the wrong word,

01:23:52.887 --> 01:23:54.120
we've got a much tighter position

01:23:54.120 --> 01:23:56.370
than we had even this year.

01:23:56.370 --> 01:23:59.638
Now that's my picture.

01:23:59.638 --> 01:24:01.692
I dunno, Jeremy or Wol or others

01:24:01.692 --> 01:24:04.380
if you have a different
view on those components

01:24:04.380 --> 01:24:05.883
and others want to test really,

01:24:06.735 --> 01:24:11.253
what's the mistake in
that high level analysis?

01:24:13.507 --> 01:24:15.486
- If we start with sort of the comment,

01:24:15.486 --> 01:24:17.316
I mean Amanda said right at the beginning

01:24:17.316 --> 01:24:18.450
about the money is important,

01:24:18.450 --> 01:24:20.673
Julian, you just sort of laid out

01:24:20.673 --> 01:24:22.983
and the chair has
further expanded on this.

01:24:23.845 --> 01:24:26.079
I mean it's super critical
we give the right messages

01:24:26.079 --> 01:24:27.275
to the system.

01:24:27.275 --> 01:24:28.479
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:24:28.479 --> 01:24:29.686
- And in trying to understand.

01:24:29.686 --> 01:24:30.904
- [Richard] And to our partners.

01:24:30.904 --> 01:24:32.986
- And to our partners and
trying to understand, you know,

01:24:32.986 --> 01:24:35.012
the government's made a
very powerful commitment

01:24:35.012 --> 01:24:36.946
to NHS to 22 billion.

01:24:36.946 --> 01:24:39.360
But really making sure we
understand how that plays out.

01:24:39.360 --> 01:24:42.668
And my sense is, again,
I'm here to be corrected

01:24:42.668 --> 01:24:46.650
that actually despite that it's
still going to be very tough

01:24:46.650 --> 01:24:49.818
and I think just really
getting to some detail

01:24:49.818 --> 01:24:51.503
as to why that's the case
would be very helpful.

01:24:53.911 --> 01:24:55.124
- Yeah.

01:24:55.124 --> 01:24:55.957
- So you are a live finance.

01:24:55.957 --> 01:24:56.790
- I'm live finance director and I know

01:24:56.790 --> 01:24:58.530
and I'm in the retail
sector, Jeremy Townsend

01:24:58.530 --> 01:25:00.423
and I'm chair of the audit committee.

01:25:01.979 --> 01:25:03.833
In the retail sector where,

01:25:03.833 --> 01:25:04.666
so there's national insurance increases

01:25:04.666 --> 01:25:06.880
potentially hitting primary care

01:25:08.010 --> 01:25:09.672
and there's inflation's
probably going to be higher

01:25:09.672 --> 01:25:11.628
than we expected through the system.

01:25:11.628 --> 01:25:13.144
So yeah.

01:25:13.144 --> 01:25:15.677
So I think that the
detail, the other question,

01:25:15.677 --> 01:25:18.083
and it may be early days in the process,

01:25:18.083 --> 01:25:19.908
'cause the other thing that strikes me,

01:25:19.908 --> 01:25:22.753
which is a slightly
different piece on this is,

01:25:22.753 --> 01:25:24.365
is not only understanding the numbers

01:25:24.365 --> 01:25:25.770
for the current year in the future,

01:25:25.770 --> 01:25:26.964
but as we're heading into a 10 year plan.

01:25:26.964 --> 01:25:27.797
- [Chair] Yeah.

01:25:27.797 --> 01:25:29.490
- And we're looking how
demand isn't going to go away

01:25:29.490 --> 01:25:31.848
in the future, it's going to increase.

01:25:31.848 --> 01:25:35.762
Are we going to be facing
this very tight situation

01:25:35.762 --> 01:25:37.434
of year and we've talked
about productivity

01:25:37.434 --> 01:25:38.580
and we're going to talk
about productivity later on.

01:25:38.580 --> 01:25:41.370
Is there going to be
capacity in the numbers

01:25:41.370 --> 01:25:43.650
next year to make the investments

01:25:43.650 --> 01:25:45.540
we might want to make
in driving productivity

01:25:45.540 --> 01:25:47.340
so that we can try and
get ahead of the game.

01:25:47.340 --> 01:25:50.850
So I think there's the challenge around

01:25:50.850 --> 01:25:53.280
how does the money that
we've got access to

01:25:53.280 --> 01:25:54.960
fund the inflationary increases,

01:25:54.960 --> 01:25:57.450
but where is our thinking

01:25:57.450 --> 01:26:00.162
or is it to be progressed
in terms of how we try

01:26:00.162 --> 01:26:00.995
and deliver productivity and systems

01:26:00.995 --> 01:26:02.580
to try and get ahead of the game

01:26:02.580 --> 01:26:05.100
for not even next year but future years.

01:26:05.100 --> 01:26:08.670
- So what's wrong with that analysis?

01:26:11.232 --> 01:26:12.065
- Analysis is a big word for
what we just ran through,

01:26:12.065 --> 01:26:13.679
but what's wrong with the?

01:26:13.679 --> 01:26:17.587
- Analysis is broadly right

01:26:17.587 --> 01:26:18.420
I mean, you know, you've just
sort of outlined the cost

01:26:18.420 --> 01:26:21.333
you've got to deal with,
pay, non-pay inflation,

01:26:22.237 --> 01:26:24.963
medicines, prices, you know, this year.

01:26:26.222 --> 01:26:27.432
And it reflects the degree

01:26:27.432 --> 01:26:30.855
of prioritisation we've
already done this year

01:26:30.855 --> 01:26:33.270
and done with the department

01:26:33.270 --> 01:26:37.634
and ministers to make sure
that can actually afford

01:26:37.634 --> 01:26:39.762
to do the things which
are their priorities.

01:26:39.762 --> 01:26:41.760
And in particular I would say, you know

01:26:41.760 --> 01:26:44.460
how we keep going on
reducing the waiting list,

01:26:44.460 --> 01:26:47.613
which we are year on year,
the waiting list is down.

01:26:48.490 --> 01:26:49.323
- [Chair] Yeah.

01:26:49.323 --> 01:26:52.260
- You know, but there's
been a lot of prioritisation

01:26:52.260 --> 01:26:53.520
and it's why the focus

01:26:53.520 --> 01:26:58.520
on actually cost control
productivity is so, so important.

01:26:58.961 --> 01:27:00.120
And as we look ahead to next year,

01:27:00.120 --> 01:27:01.860
as I've said, I mean just you know,

01:27:01.860 --> 01:27:04.009
for the reasons you again have just said

01:27:04.009 --> 01:27:07.710
and outlined, there isn't
really going to be new money

01:27:07.710 --> 01:27:09.000
for new activity.

01:27:09.000 --> 01:27:11.820
We're going to have to do
it through productivity.

01:27:11.820 --> 01:27:12.653
- [Chair] Yeah.

01:27:13.706 --> 01:27:14.899
- And even after that,

01:27:14.899 --> 01:27:16.620
I suspect we will face some tough choices.

01:27:16.620 --> 01:27:19.140
You know, we are going
through that exercise

01:27:19.140 --> 01:27:21.907
with the Secretary of State at the moment.

01:27:21.907 --> 01:27:22.800
You know, he is really forcing us

01:27:22.800 --> 01:27:25.302
and the department to
examine everything we do

01:27:25.302 --> 01:27:28.800
and make sure the money is being spent

01:27:28.800 --> 01:27:32.370
on the things that matter.

01:27:32.370 --> 01:27:34.071
- [Chair] The biggest priority.

01:27:34.071 --> 01:27:35.292
- And their priorities

01:27:35.292 --> 01:27:37.512
and we're going to have to
make within NHS England,

01:27:37.512 --> 01:27:39.306
you know, we will have
to make further choices

01:27:39.306 --> 01:27:42.611
about where our resources
and our people are deployed

01:27:42.611 --> 01:27:45.210
and you know, we are going to
have to move things around,

01:27:45.210 --> 01:27:47.894
you know, we need to create the headroom

01:27:47.894 --> 01:27:51.960
in fact to really launch and you know,

01:27:51.960 --> 01:27:55.413
and scale up a national improvement.

01:27:56.916 --> 01:28:00.326
- Programme because only through exactly,

01:28:00.326 --> 01:28:01.525
again, some of the stuff

01:28:01.525 --> 01:28:04.620
we were talking about
under the RSP agenda item

01:28:04.620 --> 01:28:08.931
that we by just through
continuous improvement,

01:28:08.931 --> 01:28:11.190
better use of the resources
that already exist,

01:28:11.190 --> 01:28:16.190
allied to the reforms that,
you know, for example Vin,

01:28:18.012 --> 01:28:19.380
but others are driving around this table

01:28:19.380 --> 01:28:20.730
or the work Navina is doing

01:28:20.730 --> 01:28:25.730
on improving staffing retentions
and working conditions.

01:28:25.890 --> 01:28:29.370
It's only through those
things will we manage

01:28:29.370 --> 01:28:34.370
to, you know, sort of hold
and improve patient access

01:28:35.363 --> 01:28:37.020
and care because next year

01:28:37.020 --> 01:28:41.087
there won't be resources for new activity.

01:28:41.087 --> 01:28:42.929
We are going to really have to, you know,

01:28:42.929 --> 01:28:44.820
it will be through productivity and reform

01:28:44.820 --> 01:28:48.420
and if boards have not got a grip on this,

01:28:48.420 --> 01:28:50.462
they are going to find themselves,

01:28:50.462 --> 01:28:53.943
you know, in some difficult.

01:28:55.199 --> 01:28:56.391
- So against,

01:28:56.391 --> 01:28:57.738
I mean Andrew going to bring
in for the last comment

01:28:57.738 --> 01:28:59.831
but against that picture of
the, sorry I didn't see Mary,

01:28:59.831 --> 01:29:01.031
so Mary first and Andrew,

01:29:01.031 --> 01:29:02.231
but I mean the picture I carry

01:29:02.231 --> 01:29:05.230
is effectively the 22 billion

01:29:06.140 --> 01:29:07.640
is already more than committed

01:29:08.943 --> 01:29:10.950
or absorbed in inflationary run rate

01:29:10.950 --> 01:29:12.840
and other activities.

01:29:12.840 --> 01:29:16.336
And that's why when we
look into this year 25, 26,

01:29:16.336 --> 01:29:18.526
we've got some really
difficult prioritisation calls

01:29:18.526 --> 01:29:19.710
and challenges and
productivity challenges,

01:29:19.710 --> 01:29:20.730
which we must meet.

01:29:20.730 --> 01:29:22.470
But we've got really difficult messages.

01:29:22.470 --> 01:29:24.900
So Mary and then Andrew
and then I'll move us on.

01:29:24.900 --> 01:29:27.960
- Thank you, I wanted to say thank you

01:29:27.960 --> 01:29:31.110
for that really thorough explanation

01:29:31.110 --> 01:29:33.750
but for me I'm celebrating the fact

01:29:33.750 --> 01:29:36.960
that we are reducing
agency costs significantly,

01:29:36.960 --> 01:29:39.690
which will improve continuity of care,

01:29:39.690 --> 01:29:42.570
which in turn should become, you know,

01:29:42.570 --> 01:29:45.690
productivity improvement but also

01:29:45.690 --> 01:29:48.600
because we are being absolutely clear

01:29:48.600 --> 01:29:53.010
that there is not significant
extra money next year.

01:29:53.010 --> 01:29:54.840
I think the whole board would agree

01:29:54.840 --> 01:29:58.710
that we are still pleased
that the pay increase

01:29:58.710 --> 01:30:02.880
to those clinical staff was welcomed by us

01:30:02.880 --> 01:30:05.730
and it has reduced the strike days

01:30:05.730 --> 01:30:07.860
and that we wouldn't want
to give the impression

01:30:07.860 --> 01:30:09.498
that we didn't welcome that.

01:30:09.498 --> 01:30:10.753
- No, well said, well said, Andrew.

01:30:10.753 --> 01:30:14.100
- So I think it would be
useful just to understand

01:30:14.100 --> 01:30:16.923
what this means for a
typical trust, you know,

01:30:17.829 --> 01:30:19.823
a typical DGH or a mental health trust

01:30:19.823 --> 01:30:23.056
and what boards may, you know,

01:30:23.056 --> 01:30:24.150
the challenge boards will be facing.

01:30:24.150 --> 01:30:28.680
And alongside that,
what's our offer to help,

01:30:28.680 --> 01:30:29.970
you know, bridge the gap?

01:30:29.970 --> 01:30:32.776
'Cause there will be
potentially a gap here

01:30:32.776 --> 01:30:34.890
around improved productivity

01:30:34.890 --> 01:30:37.920
because I think we've got
an obligation as NHS England

01:30:37.920 --> 01:30:41.430
to you know, promulgate best practise

01:30:41.430 --> 01:30:43.990
and I think there's a lot that we can do

01:30:45.726 --> 01:30:47.110
and we should be on it

01:30:47.948 --> 01:30:49.948
and supporting organisations to improve.

01:30:52.697 --> 01:30:53.897
- I agree, so Amanda do you want

01:30:53.897 --> 01:30:55.796
to have the last comment on that?

01:30:55.796 --> 01:30:56.940
- Only to that just a
really helpful discussion.

01:30:56.940 --> 01:30:57.773
Thank you.

01:30:58.806 --> 01:31:00.534
But I just wanted to say very quickly,

01:31:00.534 --> 01:31:01.710
I think it's really important
that we just remember

01:31:01.710 --> 01:31:03.427
that we are in no way different

01:31:03.427 --> 01:31:05.052
to the rest of the public sector.

01:31:05.052 --> 01:31:06.620
- Yes.

01:31:06.620 --> 01:31:10.237
- And actually every part

01:31:10.237 --> 01:31:12.138
of the public sector is having

01:31:12.138 --> 01:31:14.453
to have the same conversations
about how do they make sure

01:31:14.453 --> 01:31:16.579
that taxpayers money is best spent.

01:31:16.579 --> 01:31:17.907
- Well yeah.

01:31:17.907 --> 01:31:19.170
- So I really agree with Mary strongly

01:31:19.170 --> 01:31:21.240
that I think we should take confidence

01:31:21.240 --> 01:31:23.790
from what we've done
over the last two years.

01:31:23.790 --> 01:31:25.580
And when I say we, of
course what I really mean

01:31:25.580 --> 01:31:27.330
is all of those people working in the NHS

01:31:27.330 --> 01:31:29.738
because actually what
our colleagues have done

01:31:29.738 --> 01:31:31.567
is they have delivered, you know,

01:31:31.567 --> 01:31:34.233
1.8% increase in productivity this year.

01:31:35.418 --> 01:31:37.219
And that's on the back of,

01:31:37.219 --> 01:31:39.990
you know, similar improvement last year.

01:31:39.990 --> 01:31:42.630
And we have improved performance

01:31:42.630 --> 01:31:45.720
and we have taken some really
important steps on reform.

01:31:45.720 --> 01:31:48.750
So as we look to the future,
I do think that the challenge

01:31:48.750 --> 01:31:51.332
for us is to remain appropriately
ambitious for our patients

01:31:51.332 --> 01:31:54.900
and appropriately ambitious to keep going

01:31:54.900 --> 01:31:58.770
with some of those critical
steps on the reform journey.

01:31:58.770 --> 01:32:01.831
And that's what, as Julian says, you know,

01:32:01.831 --> 01:32:03.720
we're just working through
that now nationally,

01:32:03.720 --> 01:32:06.984
but it is the importance of
then the planning process

01:32:06.984 --> 01:32:08.884
with our local systems as we make sure

01:32:10.108 --> 01:32:14.910
that we have absolutely
landed that in a way

01:32:14.910 --> 01:32:18.750
that is really owned by local systems,

01:32:18.750 --> 01:32:21.960
local organisations with the
appropriate level of ambition

01:32:21.960 --> 01:32:25.290
for our patients, for our taxpayers,

01:32:25.290 --> 01:32:29.323
but also just recognising that
that is going to be continued

01:32:29.323 --> 01:32:34.260
real focus on driving
productivity, on driving out waste,

01:32:34.260 --> 01:32:36.390
on making sure that all of our systems

01:32:36.390 --> 01:32:37.660
and processes are working

01:32:38.513 --> 01:32:39.510
as effectively as they possibly can.

01:32:39.510 --> 01:32:40.583
- Thank you, okay.

01:32:41.441 --> 01:32:44.790
I now need to move us on
to try and get us back on

01:32:44.790 --> 01:32:47.130
and Wol yes, thank you
for, I know you've got,

01:32:47.130 --> 01:32:49.590
you've got to run but thank you Wol.

01:32:49.590 --> 01:32:54.405
So let's move on to productivity,
which is quite good.

01:32:54.405 --> 01:32:57.783
It's a good segue into
productivity, is May joining?

01:32:58.639 --> 01:32:59.855
- No, no.

01:32:59.855 --> 01:33:02.709
May is otherwise being productive.

01:33:02.709 --> 01:33:04.459
- [Chair] So, okay.

01:33:06.780 --> 01:33:08.043
You'll fly solo then.

01:33:08.935 --> 01:33:10.035
- Yeah, I'll fly solo.

01:33:11.067 --> 01:33:12.960
So look, thanks for the time.

01:33:12.960 --> 01:33:17.040
We brought a paper to
this board back in May

01:33:17.040 --> 01:33:20.739
where we outlined you know,
understood the position

01:33:20.739 --> 01:33:22.467
to be in the NHS

01:33:22.467 --> 01:33:25.350
and I will just briefly remind folk

01:33:25.350 --> 01:33:30.350
that productivity fell in the
year 2021 as we went through,

01:33:32.255 --> 01:33:35.503
you know, that first really
sort of emergency phase of COVID

01:33:35.503 --> 01:33:37.530
where we had to stop a lot
of work in order to deal

01:33:37.530 --> 01:33:42.530
with the pressure that
the pandemic brought

01:33:42.895 --> 01:33:47.895
but rebounded rapidly in 21, 22.

01:33:50.167 --> 01:33:53.651
We did not get back to the
kind of pre pandemic levels

01:33:53.651 --> 01:33:56.084
but across the whole of the NHS, you know,

01:33:56.084 --> 01:33:56.917
rebounded reasonably rapidly.

01:33:56.917 --> 01:34:00.116
And I will say, you know,

01:34:00.116 --> 01:34:03.349
we've got about across
the whole of the NHS,

01:34:03.349 --> 01:34:05.310
we have about 19% more staff

01:34:05.310 --> 01:34:09.864
and we are today seeing
about 14% more patients

01:34:09.864 --> 01:34:12.103
than we were in 19, 20.

01:34:12.103 --> 01:34:16.083
When you look across all of
the services the NHS provides.

01:34:17.666 --> 01:34:20.880
Now that says we're still
not back where we were.

01:34:20.880 --> 01:34:23.276
IE as we discussed at the board in May,

01:34:23.276 --> 01:34:25.920
we are still experiencing
some of the ongoing impacts

01:34:25.920 --> 01:34:29.648
of COVID clearly last year,

01:34:29.648 --> 01:34:32.790
the massive disruption
from industrial action

01:34:32.790 --> 01:34:36.450
and in the end, you
know there will continue

01:34:36.450 --> 01:34:38.820
to be an absolute link

01:34:38.820 --> 01:34:41.820
between what we can do in the NHS

01:34:41.820 --> 01:34:43.200
and actually you know,

01:34:43.200 --> 01:34:45.813
what our partners in the local authorities

01:34:45.813 --> 01:34:48.465
and social care providers are able to do.

01:34:48.465 --> 01:34:50.050
And as we discussed, you know,

01:34:50.050 --> 01:34:53.010
to the degree there are
capacity constraints

01:34:53.010 --> 01:34:54.897
within social care,

01:34:54.897 --> 01:34:58.440
productivity will be impacted

01:34:58.440 --> 01:35:01.878
because we will have
patients longer in hospital

01:35:01.878 --> 01:35:02.760
and that's still, I think

01:35:02.760 --> 01:35:05.733
we can see in in the
data having an effect,

01:35:06.578 --> 01:35:10.247
nonetheless we've been recovering

01:35:10.247 --> 01:35:12.617
about 2% a year in the acute sector

01:35:12.617 --> 01:35:15.734
and as we said in the
earlier paper, you know,

01:35:15.734 --> 01:35:18.180
that continues to be true year to date

01:35:18.180 --> 01:35:20.627
in the acute sector here.

01:35:20.627 --> 01:35:25.627
So, you know, we see
ongoing steady recovery

01:35:31.410 --> 01:35:33.819
and we are doing, you know,

01:35:33.819 --> 01:35:37.090
a lot more work this year than
we were this time last year

01:35:37.982 --> 01:35:41.460
and that's been confirmed by the analysis

01:35:41.460 --> 01:35:44.320
by people like the
Institute of Fiscal Studies

01:35:45.155 --> 01:35:48.963
who published a paper about a
month ago actually, you know,

01:35:49.870 --> 01:35:51.690
saying that you know, they are seeing

01:35:51.690 --> 01:35:56.410
welcoming the sort of
ongoing productivity recovery

01:35:57.263 --> 01:35:59.003
that we are seeing in the acute sector.

01:36:00.600 --> 01:36:04.067
You know, we are also seeing
reductions in average length

01:36:04.067 --> 01:36:05.884
of stay for non elective admissions

01:36:05.884 --> 01:36:08.313
that's about 2.3% better than last year.

01:36:09.334 --> 01:36:11.820
Now we are not just focused
on the acute hospitals.

01:36:11.820 --> 01:36:15.060
I mean one of the sort of
weaknesses I think in the way

01:36:15.060 --> 01:36:17.114
that productivity has been understood

01:36:17.114 --> 01:36:19.586
and measures in the NHS over the years

01:36:19.586 --> 01:36:20.700
is that we have not
really had a good focus

01:36:22.243 --> 01:36:25.350
on our community services and
on mental health services.

01:36:25.350 --> 01:36:28.290
And we have done a lot of
work over the recent years

01:36:28.290 --> 01:36:30.962
to improve our understanding of the costs

01:36:30.962 --> 01:36:33.359
and improve our understanding of the data.

01:36:33.359 --> 01:36:38.020
And we have started to
produce, I mean it's very much

01:36:38.020 --> 01:36:40.565
sort of a work in progress, you know,

01:36:40.565 --> 01:36:44.098
sort of headline productivity
metrics for those sectors,

01:36:44.098 --> 01:36:45.690
you know, that we are working on

01:36:45.690 --> 01:36:49.080
with the leadership in those sectors

01:36:49.080 --> 01:36:52.852
and indeed discussing with the ONS

01:36:52.852 --> 01:36:54.891
to make sure that they can
properly incorporate it

01:36:54.891 --> 01:36:58.536
into some of the official
statistics that they produce.

01:36:58.536 --> 01:36:59.940
And again those show
that there is, you know,

01:36:59.940 --> 01:37:04.500
room for improvement in an absolute terms

01:37:04.500 --> 01:37:09.105
and indeed in terms of reducing variation.

01:37:09.105 --> 01:37:13.410
So just making sure right across the NHS

01:37:13.410 --> 01:37:15.630
we have a focus on delivering
the best for taxpayers

01:37:15.630 --> 01:37:19.145
and the best for patients.

01:37:19.145 --> 01:37:21.603
Now looking ahead to next year,

01:37:23.972 --> 01:37:26.760
as I just said, you know,
we are really going to be

01:37:26.760 --> 01:37:29.230
dependent on not more money

01:37:30.648 --> 01:37:33.670
but improving, you know, what we can do

01:37:34.595 --> 01:37:36.150
with the resources that we have.

01:37:36.150 --> 01:37:40.710
Now the key things we
are doing, as I said,

01:37:40.710 --> 01:37:42.730
we are looking to scale up

01:37:43.825 --> 01:37:45.690
like the sort of national
improvement programme

01:37:45.690 --> 01:37:50.220
that we started under NHS impact.

01:37:50.220 --> 01:37:52.353
And I distinguish that from,

01:37:53.254 --> 01:37:54.180
I'm going to say it,

01:37:54.180 --> 01:37:55.330
performance management

01:37:56.613 --> 01:37:59.474
in the kind of most challenged trust

01:37:59.474 --> 01:38:01.730
which still requires improvement.

01:38:01.730 --> 01:38:04.380
But how do we give the
skills to our clinical

01:38:04.380 --> 01:38:06.180
and operational managers

01:38:06.180 --> 01:38:08.640
across our providers

01:38:08.640 --> 01:38:11.728
and we're really focused on again,

01:38:11.728 --> 01:38:13.828
improving the flow through emergency care,

01:38:14.993 --> 01:38:16.200
which means both how we reduce

01:38:16.200 --> 01:38:19.470
avoidable admissions as well as, you know,

01:38:19.470 --> 01:38:21.744
optimising the amount of time

01:38:21.744 --> 01:38:24.300
people are in care and
improving length of stay.

01:38:24.300 --> 01:38:26.860
And if we are going to
achieve some of the goals

01:38:27.711 --> 01:38:30.036
we've set for next year, I
mean we need to sort of double

01:38:30.036 --> 01:38:34.200
or more to sort of improvement
in length of stay next year.

01:38:34.200 --> 01:38:37.747
We are looking at again at how we continue

01:38:37.747 --> 01:38:41.288
to use the assets we've got
to improve theatre utilisation

01:38:41.288 --> 01:38:46.288
and again a real focus
on outpatient services.

01:38:46.437 --> 01:38:48.813
I mean just to give sort
of a rough statistic,

01:38:50.019 --> 01:38:52.260
if we could get, you know, we now measure

01:38:52.260 --> 01:38:56.553
and are giving to
providers a statistic on,

01:38:58.173 --> 01:38:59.006
I mean effectively how many outpatients

01:38:59.006 --> 01:39:01.950
are being seen per relevant clinician.

01:39:01.950 --> 01:39:04.960
And if we could get the
bottom third up to the level

01:39:05.818 --> 01:39:08.109
of not the kind of the average

01:39:08.109 --> 01:39:11.241
but actually just the
next third, you know,

01:39:11.241 --> 01:39:13.257
that could generate two

01:39:13.257 --> 01:39:16.008
to three million extra
outpatients being seen

01:39:16.008 --> 01:39:18.358
and that's about improving,
do not attend rates

01:39:19.207 --> 01:39:20.770
as well as looking at just how well,

01:39:20.770 --> 01:39:22.740
you know, the sort of booking

01:39:22.740 --> 01:39:24.890
and administration
arrangements are working

01:39:25.892 --> 01:39:28.025
and honestly how we can use technology.

01:39:28.025 --> 01:39:29.685
I mean things like ambient voice

01:39:29.685 --> 01:39:31.880
and various other things, you know,

01:39:31.880 --> 01:39:35.387
to sort of improve actually
the ability for clinicians

01:39:35.387 --> 01:39:37.560
to do the thing they
want to do, which is see

01:39:37.560 --> 01:39:40.765
and manage people effectively.

01:39:40.765 --> 01:39:43.380
So we really do think
there are gains to be made.

01:39:43.380 --> 01:39:48.090
We are doing work with
some trusts at the moment

01:39:48.090 --> 01:39:51.739
to see, you know, just
how we can make sure

01:39:51.739 --> 01:39:55.830
we are doing job planning
as effectively as we can

01:39:55.830 --> 01:39:59.970
and can that make the difference
both boards understanding

01:39:59.970 --> 01:40:02.530
have job plans been done,
how effective are they

01:40:03.561 --> 01:40:07.083
and as importantly, are
they being fulfilled?

01:40:08.106 --> 01:40:11.100
And I think that is, you
know, it's not a criticism,

01:40:11.100 --> 01:40:12.900
it's just making sure really good

01:40:12.900 --> 01:40:15.344
management disciplines are in place.

01:40:15.344 --> 01:40:16.890
And again, we are giving
the clinicians the chance

01:40:16.890 --> 01:40:20.732
to do they actually want to do.

01:40:20.732 --> 01:40:23.520
And then clearly, I mentioned it before,

01:40:23.520 --> 01:40:25.680
how we keep improving staff retention

01:40:25.680 --> 01:40:27.720
because if we can keep
improving staff retention,

01:40:27.720 --> 01:40:31.680
we can keep reducing our
temporary staffing costs.

01:40:31.680 --> 01:40:36.680
And then next year just
continuing the focus on technology

01:40:36.930 --> 01:40:40.870
that we do the next major
step in completing this phase

01:40:41.841 --> 01:40:43.950
of the frontline digitization programme,

01:40:43.950 --> 01:40:47.890
which is giving every
major provider, making sure

01:40:48.765 --> 01:40:51.850
they have a modern electronic
patient record system

01:40:54.330 --> 01:40:58.780
that we continue to link up the NHS app

01:40:59.869 --> 01:41:03.300
to the patient portals

01:41:03.300 --> 01:41:06.279
that now most trusts have, I mean our aim

01:41:06.279 --> 01:41:09.000
would be we can make sure we've secured

01:41:09.000 --> 01:41:10.860
the funding, you know, can we get

01:41:10.860 --> 01:41:15.060
to 80% of trust basically
having 80% of their elective

01:41:15.060 --> 01:41:18.608
or you know, that sort of 70 to 80%

01:41:18.608 --> 01:41:20.070
of their elective pathways.

01:41:20.070 --> 01:41:24.140
I mean, you know, patients
basically being linked up

01:41:24.140 --> 01:41:24.973
through the app

01:41:26.426 --> 01:41:28.383
with sort of automated communication.

01:41:29.965 --> 01:41:31.650
I mean we know that if
we can make the app work

01:41:31.650 --> 01:41:33.600
and if people use it,

01:41:33.600 --> 01:41:36.861
I mean even in primary care
we think we can free up,

01:41:36.861 --> 01:41:40.400
you know, two million hours of staff time

01:41:40.400 --> 01:41:43.173
and about, you know,
900,000 hours of GP time.

01:41:44.056 --> 01:41:46.575
I mean that is just a massive gain.

01:41:46.575 --> 01:41:50.037
So those are the things we're focused on.

01:41:50.037 --> 01:41:54.390
Recently there was published some work

01:41:54.390 --> 01:41:57.423
that actually Vin's team have been doing

01:41:57.423 --> 01:41:59.856
with some clinicians
at Great Ormond Street

01:41:59.856 --> 01:42:01.697
who have actually been
running a large scale trial

01:42:01.697 --> 01:42:04.650
of the use of ambient voice technology

01:42:04.650 --> 01:42:09.247
across actually all of our services,

01:42:09.247 --> 01:42:11.883
primary care, ambulance
trusts, the acute sector.

01:42:12.824 --> 01:42:14.040
And I think that has
given us a real insight

01:42:15.141 --> 01:42:17.098
into where that has the potential

01:42:17.098 --> 01:42:18.753
to make the biggest
difference most quickly.

01:42:18.753 --> 01:42:21.540
So again, we'll work to see,
you know, what we can do

01:42:21.540 --> 01:42:24.693
to scale and roll that out.

01:42:25.677 --> 01:42:28.470
And then finally, you know,
we are doing a real focus

01:42:28.470 --> 01:42:31.593
on clearly non-pay spend as well.

01:42:32.532 --> 01:42:35.073
I mean next year in medicines
the focus has, you know,

01:42:35.073 --> 01:42:36.921
real focus on how we
just make the best use

01:42:36.921 --> 01:42:38.550
of switching to biosimilars.

01:42:38.550 --> 01:42:41.230
We think over the next four or five years

01:42:42.136 --> 01:42:43.890
that will have hundreds
of millions of pounds

01:42:43.890 --> 01:42:46.263
of potential savings.

01:42:48.360 --> 01:42:51.610
And you know, on the non-pay
side with Jacqui's team

01:42:52.478 --> 01:42:56.432
making the best use of
the national frameworks

01:42:56.432 --> 01:42:58.760
that exist where we can get,

01:42:58.760 --> 01:43:02.242
I mean basically best price at volume

01:43:02.242 --> 01:43:06.655
where with supply chain
we will agree with you

01:43:06.655 --> 01:43:08.355
their business plan for next year.

01:43:09.564 --> 01:43:12.104
- Very good, let's open it up just for two

01:43:12.104 --> 01:43:13.294
or three questions on that.

01:43:13.294 --> 01:43:15.388
So who would like to
say, Jeremy, there we go.

01:43:15.388 --> 01:43:18.007
Lots of hands, excellent, two or three.

01:43:18.007 --> 01:43:21.141
Anyways, so we're going to
go Jeremy, then Mike and Vin.

01:43:21.141 --> 01:43:22.328
- Just two quick points.

01:43:22.328 --> 01:43:24.270
So in, in a strange way, the inconsistency

01:43:24.270 --> 01:43:26.277
gives a level of optimism

01:43:26.277 --> 01:43:28.105
because it says there must
be opportunities elsewhere.

01:43:28.105 --> 01:43:30.510
And I just wonder off the
back of this great work,

01:43:30.510 --> 01:43:34.711
how much we can use this
to feed into the planning

01:43:34.711 --> 01:43:36.240
for next year and the conversations
we're having with trust

01:43:36.240 --> 01:43:40.550
to give them some options

01:43:40.550 --> 01:43:45.550
and actions some information,

01:43:46.530 --> 01:43:48.060
particularly those who are struggling

01:43:48.060 --> 01:43:51.033
to make it balanced to hit their numbers.

01:43:51.983 --> 01:43:53.346
Then at risk

01:43:53.346 --> 01:43:54.660
of repeating myself in
the previous conversation,

01:43:54.660 --> 01:43:56.530
it feels to me productivity

01:43:57.473 --> 01:43:59.998
is one of the way we
can try and get ahead of

01:43:59.998 --> 01:44:02.240
what is going to be incredibly
tight funding situation

01:44:02.240 --> 01:44:03.073
over the next five to 10 years.

01:44:03.073 --> 01:44:06.180
And again, the point on the 10 year plan,

01:44:06.180 --> 01:44:07.830
taking all those fantastic opportunities

01:44:07.830 --> 01:44:10.576
during I think feeding
those into 10 year plans

01:44:10.576 --> 01:44:13.410
so that we've got the funds
identified that require

01:44:13.410 --> 01:44:14.800
that we can capture

01:44:15.885 --> 01:44:17.986
to make the investments
we need to unlock that.

01:44:17.986 --> 01:44:20.340
I think these feels to me
needs to be intrinsically

01:44:20.340 --> 01:44:22.743
part of that overall 10
year planning process.

01:44:24.060 --> 01:44:25.256
- So Mike.

01:44:25.256 --> 01:44:26.089
- I think I'm just

01:44:26.089 --> 01:44:26.922
going to repeat what Jeremy just said.

01:44:27.913 --> 01:44:29.040
I mean, what comes through
is a now a palpable sense

01:44:29.040 --> 01:44:32.010
of leadership, in terms of the
things that need to be done,

01:44:32.010 --> 01:44:34.590
which is a testament to you and your team

01:44:34.590 --> 01:44:36.390
and a lot of the data now exists

01:44:36.390 --> 01:44:37.980
to start making the comparisons

01:44:37.980 --> 01:44:41.164
that Jeremy is describing where, you know,

01:44:41.164 --> 01:44:43.140
we know that there's quite
significant differences,

01:44:43.140 --> 01:44:44.700
variations in performance

01:44:44.700 --> 01:44:47.430
and again it feels like
there's some low hanging fruit

01:44:47.430 --> 01:44:49.680
to get after and a lot of
good things have been done

01:44:49.680 --> 01:44:54.143
around, you know, dealing
with agency retention rates

01:44:54.143 --> 01:44:56.610
and all the other things
that you've alluded to.

01:44:56.610 --> 01:45:00.690
The concern, if I have
one is, well I have lots,

01:45:00.690 --> 01:45:05.250
but the challenge of how we
carve out the right level

01:45:05.250 --> 01:45:09.254
of strategic investment to
be able to do the things

01:45:09.254 --> 01:45:10.463
that we know we need to do.

01:45:10.463 --> 01:45:11.802
And I don't if there's one consensus

01:45:11.802 --> 01:45:13.030
I think that everybody agrees with,

01:45:13.030 --> 01:45:15.240
it's that the system is under invested

01:45:15.240 --> 01:45:17.370
and that has an impact on productivity,

01:45:17.370 --> 01:45:20.193
whether it's technology or
whether it's infrastructure,

01:45:20.193 --> 01:45:22.980
whether it's construction
buildings, whatever.

01:45:22.980 --> 01:45:25.157
So a reflection on that

01:45:25.157 --> 01:45:27.959
and it kind of builds to Jeremy's point,

01:45:27.959 --> 01:45:29.760
which is you will only solve this

01:45:29.760 --> 01:45:31.080
in a way which is sustainable

01:45:31.080 --> 01:45:32.370
by continuous investment

01:45:32.370 --> 01:45:35.013
over many cycles, not just over a year.

01:45:35.872 --> 01:45:38.040
- Yeah, so Vin you had a question I think.

01:45:38.040 --> 01:45:39.911
- Yeah, it's not a question,

01:45:39.911 --> 01:45:41.918
it's just a statement chair.

01:45:41.918 --> 01:45:42.978
So firstly I think

01:45:42.978 --> 01:45:46.230
the NHS app's been tremendously exciting.

01:45:46.230 --> 01:45:48.928
There are bits of it
that don't sound exciting

01:45:48.928 --> 01:45:50.880
but actually quite simple
things like receiving reminders.

01:45:50.880 --> 01:45:53.279
Through the NHS app
you've got an appointment,

01:45:53.279 --> 01:45:56.040
you know, increases the
chance you'll attend

01:45:56.040 --> 01:45:59.880
but also into some of the
organisations have started

01:45:59.880 --> 01:46:02.741
to adopt new features in
the app, like the ability

01:46:02.741 --> 01:46:05.790
to send questionnaires to
patients pre and post appointment.

01:46:05.790 --> 01:46:07.440
So when I was a full-time consultant,

01:46:07.440 --> 01:46:10.868
I did a headache clinic,
prior to that you had to wait

01:46:10.868 --> 01:46:11.790
to come and see me before I could see you

01:46:11.790 --> 01:46:13.680
and then assess your symptoms.

01:46:13.680 --> 01:46:16.890
If questionnaires gradually
start getting adopted

01:46:16.890 --> 01:46:19.310
across England, which we would hope to do

01:46:19.310 --> 01:46:22.233
in the next year, actually
you can start collecting data

01:46:22.233 --> 01:46:23.190
and information from patients in advance

01:46:23.190 --> 01:46:25.470
and make one appointment
really worth something

01:46:25.470 --> 01:46:28.192
instead of two for follow up.

01:46:28.192 --> 01:46:29.389
So we'd hope next year very much

01:46:29.389 --> 01:46:31.290
to be able to work with
clinicians for example

01:46:31.290 --> 01:46:32.700
through getting it right first time

01:46:32.700 --> 01:46:35.070
to start standardising
some of those practises,

01:46:35.070 --> 01:46:39.480
really adopting the app's features

01:46:39.480 --> 01:46:43.020
to their maximum impact.

01:46:43.020 --> 01:46:45.736
The second bit is that, I mean
last time we had the board

01:46:45.736 --> 01:46:47.245
in Blackpool, we did talk a bit

01:46:47.245 --> 01:46:48.445
about the federated data platforms.

01:46:48.445 --> 01:46:49.645
I won't go over it again,

01:46:49.645 --> 01:46:52.170
but it is about putting data
in the hands of clinicians

01:46:52.170 --> 01:46:53.520
and operations managers

01:46:53.520 --> 01:46:55.710
and allowing them to organise their work

01:46:55.710 --> 01:46:58.611
in a way that optimises care.

01:46:58.611 --> 01:47:00.090
And we can see that not
just in the products

01:47:00.090 --> 01:47:01.955
that are also there,

01:47:01.955 --> 01:47:03.270
but also our teams are working directly

01:47:03.270 --> 01:47:05.550
with clinicians to develop new products.

01:47:05.550 --> 01:47:07.590
So you could get almost
real time information

01:47:07.590 --> 01:47:10.333
on where you can optimise scheduling

01:47:10.333 --> 01:47:11.760
of patients into clinics and theatres.

01:47:11.760 --> 01:47:14.330
And then the final bit
I just want to mention

01:47:14.330 --> 01:47:16.080
is we shouldn't forget
about surgical hubs.

01:47:16.080 --> 01:47:19.170
We're a big programme and
independent evaluation

01:47:19.170 --> 01:47:22.155
by the health foundation show
they are 20% more productive.

01:47:22.155 --> 01:47:25.920
And caring for patients in
big, busy acute hospitals

01:47:25.920 --> 01:47:27.894
where there's always competition

01:47:27.894 --> 01:47:30.360
for acute space and pushing
ahead with that programme

01:47:30.360 --> 01:47:33.180
hopefully will be a really important part

01:47:33.180 --> 01:47:35.045
of the work that we're doing

01:47:35.045 --> 01:47:35.878
to make sure patients get treated.

01:47:35.878 --> 01:47:37.539
- I'm going to just pause it

01:47:37.539 --> 01:47:38.400
'cause we're going to
be under time pressure.

01:47:38.400 --> 01:47:40.533
Julian, do you want to react to those?

01:47:41.393 --> 01:47:42.360
Those are statements really your comments

01:47:42.360 --> 01:47:44.520
by Vin but Jeremy

01:47:44.520 --> 01:47:49.520
or Mike's case, using
information into the trust.

01:47:49.663 --> 01:47:52.783
- So we are working currently
is one of the reasons

01:47:52.783 --> 01:47:53.910
why May is being productive elsewhere,

01:47:53.910 --> 01:47:58.910
on actually taking the data
we have trust by trust,

01:47:59.858 --> 01:48:02.110
which at the moment is quite acute focus

01:48:03.147 --> 01:48:04.410
though we are just
looking at what can we do

01:48:05.341 --> 01:48:06.565
on the community mental health side too,

01:48:06.565 --> 01:48:07.860
even with the data that we have,

01:48:07.860 --> 01:48:09.973
to actually tell people,

01:48:09.973 --> 01:48:12.273
well what we think the
opportunities there are

01:48:13.250 --> 01:48:17.643
commercially by point of,
you know, sort of delivery.

01:48:18.516 --> 01:48:19.966
I mean again, I think we need

01:48:20.940 --> 01:48:22.920
to have a real focus next
year on getting all boards

01:48:23.989 --> 01:48:25.190
to look at their establishment

01:48:25.190 --> 01:48:26.375
and the establishment growth they've had

01:48:26.375 --> 01:48:27.597
over the last four or five years

01:48:27.597 --> 01:48:28.430
in particularly in their
support and admin sides.

01:48:28.430 --> 01:48:30.061
I mean that has grown actually

01:48:30.061 --> 01:48:31.617
in percentage terms
faster than other areas

01:48:31.617 --> 01:48:34.833
and I think we just have to
ask people, I don't know,

01:48:35.761 --> 01:48:36.594
is it all necessary?

01:48:36.594 --> 01:48:39.253
And I think it's just, you
know, we just need people

01:48:39.253 --> 01:48:40.086
to sort of go through that stuff.

01:48:40.086 --> 01:48:43.510
But we are going to try
early in the new year

01:48:44.812 --> 01:48:46.297
that we can give organisation

01:48:46.297 --> 01:48:49.030
by organisation at least our view

01:48:50.046 --> 01:48:51.988
of the data we have on them

01:48:51.988 --> 01:48:53.503
and where we think the opportunities are

01:48:53.503 --> 01:48:55.585
and where we think it's
worth boards at least asking,

01:48:55.585 --> 01:48:57.720
you know, the serious question,

01:48:57.720 --> 01:49:01.227
and Mike, I completely agree with you,

01:49:01.227 --> 01:49:03.630
this is not just a one year thing.

01:49:03.630 --> 01:49:06.840
It is how do we set the programme up

01:49:06.840 --> 01:49:09.780
that, you know, over
a long period of time,

01:49:09.780 --> 01:49:12.630
you know, we are learning
from what's worked

01:49:12.630 --> 01:49:16.103
and you know, as well as
laying the foundations now.

01:49:16.103 --> 01:49:18.610
I mean I think one of
the issues I still think

01:49:18.610 --> 01:49:21.010
we need to get to grips with the

01:49:21.010 --> 01:49:23.510
government and the Exchequer,

01:49:23.510 --> 01:49:24.780
but I think this is true across government

01:49:24.780 --> 01:49:28.023
is in particular for
technology investment.

01:49:28.890 --> 01:49:31.590
You know, the balance
between capital and revenue

01:49:31.590 --> 01:49:35.101
and some understanding recognition

01:49:35.101 --> 01:49:37.170
that, you know, there's
a significant chunk

01:49:37.170 --> 01:49:40.470
which is revenue investment,
but it is investment.

01:49:40.470 --> 01:49:43.953
It isn't just the cost of
running things, it is investment.

01:49:44.910 --> 01:49:48.120
So, you know, no doubt those conversations

01:49:48.120 --> 01:49:50.979
will continue because
I do worry otherwise,

01:49:50.979 --> 01:49:53.050
you know, we will not be able

01:49:53.916 --> 01:49:57.090
to make the changes at the
pace that we need to make them

01:49:57.090 --> 01:50:01.141
to make the NHS sustainable for patients

01:50:01.141 --> 01:50:03.229
and taxpayers and our staff.

01:50:03.229 --> 01:50:04.200
- Julian, thank you, I
am going to move us on.

01:50:04.200 --> 01:50:07.020
I realise there'll be more
appetite for questions,

01:50:07.020 --> 01:50:09.991
but we've had a
conversation on productivity

01:50:09.991 --> 01:50:11.886
before it will come back to the board.

01:50:11.886 --> 01:50:12.923
It's a really important theme.

01:50:13.761 --> 01:50:14.820
But I do want to move
us on to the next item,

01:50:14.820 --> 01:50:17.220
which I think Julian you might introduce

01:50:17.220 --> 01:50:22.220
or just hand to Adam and
Adam, welcome to the board.

01:50:22.762 --> 01:50:24.602
I was delighted you joined.

01:50:24.602 --> 01:50:26.029
I can't remember when you joined us now,

01:50:26.029 --> 01:50:27.420
a few months ago and we
got you from treasury

01:50:27.420 --> 01:50:29.730
and I'm always delighted you do come

01:50:29.730 --> 01:50:32.190
and actually the work you're
leading on in this space

01:50:32.190 --> 01:50:35.340
is really important and incisive.

01:50:35.340 --> 01:50:36.778
So thank you.

01:50:36.778 --> 01:50:38.908
And I don't know if
Julian's going to introduce

01:50:38.908 --> 01:50:40.261
who's going to hand it straight to you,

01:50:40.261 --> 01:50:42.120
but as we do obviously
assume the paper's been read.

01:50:42.120 --> 01:50:44.130
So it's just the key points for the board

01:50:44.130 --> 01:50:46.548
to engage upon, I think.

01:50:46.548 --> 01:50:49.320
- Okay, I'll just say one thing,

01:50:49.320 --> 01:50:51.570
which is as Adam's going to explain,

01:50:51.570 --> 01:50:53.340
we have a lot of evidence

01:50:53.340 --> 01:50:56.190
and increasing evidence
about actually the link

01:50:56.190 --> 01:50:58.230
where the NHS can make a difference

01:50:58.230 --> 01:51:02.070
to economic inactivity in particular

01:51:02.070 --> 01:51:04.572
and better labour market outcomes

01:51:04.572 --> 01:51:06.445
for the working age population.

01:51:06.445 --> 01:51:08.920
I mean it's what is clearly with children

01:51:09.864 --> 01:51:12.723
how had we set them up
well yeah for the future,

01:51:14.115 --> 01:51:16.770
but where investment in health services

01:51:16.770 --> 01:51:21.300
can drive a bigger economy,
better fiscal position,

01:51:21.300 --> 01:51:24.930
and therefore a better
outcome for all concerned.

01:51:24.930 --> 01:51:29.650
And I think the work
he is doing is critical

01:51:30.897 --> 01:51:32.560
to us helping the government

01:51:33.779 --> 01:51:36.200
do better for society as a whole.

01:51:36.200 --> 01:51:37.765
- Yeah, Adam.

01:51:37.765 --> 01:51:38.960
- Thanks Julian

01:51:38.960 --> 01:51:40.110
So that the board mirror

01:51:41.383 --> 01:51:44.334
and of the summer, some
new analysis that we had

01:51:44.334 --> 01:51:46.056
that Amanda, Julian analysis to do,

01:51:46.056 --> 01:51:47.670
that analytical work continues.

01:51:47.670 --> 01:51:50.580
We are doing much more now
to show the relationship

01:51:50.580 --> 01:51:53.010
between a variety of health interventions

01:51:53.010 --> 01:51:54.123
on economic outcomes.

01:51:55.021 --> 01:51:57.241
We're doing more to understand
kind of the various clusters

01:51:57.241 --> 01:52:00.120
of conditions that are
driving economic connectivity.

01:52:00.120 --> 01:52:03.300
Kind of one big update
is to the ABRs forecast

01:52:03.300 --> 01:52:06.397
of the budget, which showed that health

01:52:06.397 --> 01:52:08.850
and disability related benefits in 23, 24

01:52:08.850 --> 01:52:13.170
going from 64.7 billion
to 100 billion pounds

01:52:13.170 --> 01:52:15.070
by the end of the forecast period

01:52:16.062 --> 01:52:17.070
and 80 billion pounds in 25, 26

01:52:17.070 --> 01:52:21.096
compared to an overall DHC
budget of 200 billion pounds.

01:52:21.096 --> 01:52:22.588
So it's a pretty enormous proportion

01:52:22.588 --> 01:52:24.060
of the total health budget.

01:52:24.060 --> 01:52:26.070
So there's a really big,

01:52:26.070 --> 01:52:28.998
significant fiscal opportunity for us

01:52:28.998 --> 01:52:31.127
to do more to keep people healthy,

01:52:31.127 --> 01:52:32.644
prevent them from getting conditions

01:52:32.644 --> 01:52:34.530
which make them sort of sick enough

01:52:34.530 --> 01:52:36.709
to drop out the labour market.

01:52:36.709 --> 01:52:38.601
So Amanda, Julian basically asked us,

01:52:38.601 --> 01:52:40.155
well what do we think deeply

01:52:40.155 --> 01:52:42.223
and creative about what do
we do with this analysis?

01:52:42.223 --> 01:52:43.560
How do we use this work

01:52:43.560 --> 01:52:45.270
to drive the three
shifts in 10 year plans?

01:52:45.270 --> 01:52:47.010
As you'll seen in the paper,

01:52:47.010 --> 01:52:51.179
we've worked with, Steve
House's team, Navina, Vin,

01:52:51.179 --> 01:52:55.180
Claire Murdoch and others
to establish these health

01:52:56.356 --> 01:52:57.571
and growth accelerators,

01:52:57.571 --> 01:52:59.331
but they're co-designed with systems.

01:52:59.331 --> 01:53:01.080
So it's very much in
this sort of new approach

01:53:01.080 --> 01:53:03.510
that we're developing, really
mature grownup approach

01:53:03.510 --> 01:53:07.080
with a variety of ICBs.

01:53:07.080 --> 01:53:09.360
So at the budget

01:53:09.360 --> 01:53:12.690
and then the sort of white
paper launched last week

01:53:12.690 --> 01:53:15.600
we announced or the government announced

01:53:15.600 --> 01:53:17.460
three health growth accelerators,

01:53:17.460 --> 01:53:19.906
the first three will
be in South Yorkshire,

01:53:19.906 --> 01:53:21.300
West Yorkshire, Northeast
and North Cumbria,

01:53:21.300 --> 01:53:23.700
that we focused on the
the top health conditions

01:53:23.700 --> 01:53:25.140
driving economic connectivity

01:53:25.140 --> 01:53:28.597
which of course is mental health illness,

01:53:28.597 --> 01:53:30.587
but also diabetes,
hypertension, CBD as well.

01:53:30.587 --> 01:53:33.450
We've got NHR who are evaluating them.

01:53:33.450 --> 01:53:35.970
But critically we're going
to look at both the health

01:53:35.970 --> 01:53:37.170
outcomes and the economic outcomes

01:53:37.170 --> 01:53:39.570
'cause the economic outcomes
can come quite quickly,

01:53:39.570 --> 01:53:42.227
but a lot of the time
the economic outcomes

01:53:42.227 --> 01:53:43.488
will take longer to appear.

01:53:43.488 --> 01:53:45.480
But if we're going to do this really well,

01:53:45.480 --> 01:53:48.180
productivity's going
to be central to them.

01:53:48.180 --> 01:53:51.060
We're going to use behavioural science,

01:53:51.060 --> 01:53:52.830
population health management, use our data

01:53:52.830 --> 01:53:55.170
much more effectively to focus on,

01:53:55.170 --> 01:53:56.230
basically to build

01:53:57.132 --> 01:53:58.906
a much more personalised
intervention system.

01:53:58.906 --> 01:54:00.436
How do you focus the right interventions

01:54:00.436 --> 01:54:03.030
at the right people, at the right time.

01:54:03.030 --> 01:54:06.750
But it gives us an opportunity
to bring together prevention,

01:54:06.750 --> 01:54:08.883
neighbourhood health, health inequalities,

01:54:10.451 --> 01:54:12.721
really improve operational
performance over time as well.

01:54:12.721 --> 01:54:13.554
And it's a big opportunity for us

01:54:13.554 --> 01:54:16.517
to improve economic growth as well.

01:54:16.517 --> 01:54:18.628
- So let's open up for questions.

01:54:18.628 --> 01:54:20.070
So Duncan, Steve, Robert.

01:54:20.070 --> 01:54:21.030
So let's get that all and then

01:54:21.030 --> 01:54:22.580
I'll come down this side, yeah.

01:54:23.915 --> 01:54:24.748
- This is great to see.

01:54:24.748 --> 01:54:26.133
And just to pick up a point, Judy,

01:54:27.068 --> 01:54:27.901
and you mentioned about children

01:54:28.788 --> 01:54:30.342
and I just, there's the element about,

01:54:30.342 --> 01:54:32.430
how do we support children
to stay in education

01:54:32.430 --> 01:54:35.661
because that then has a knock
on effect into employment.

01:54:35.661 --> 01:54:36.907
And I'm just wondering

01:54:36.907 --> 01:54:39.131
through the three growth accelerators,

01:54:39.131 --> 01:54:40.470
is there a view about,

01:54:40.470 --> 01:54:44.640
or some work in looking at
how the NHS supports people

01:54:44.640 --> 01:54:46.110
and children to stay in education

01:54:46.110 --> 01:54:48.160
and therefore the economic impact

01:54:49.061 --> 01:54:51.723
that that has then on
their ability to work?

01:54:53.520 --> 01:54:56.679
- Yeah, again, Steve Powis,
National Medical Director,

01:54:56.679 --> 01:54:58.918
again, incredibly supportive of the work.

01:54:58.918 --> 01:55:00.958
As you say, you've been
working with people in my

01:55:00.958 --> 01:55:04.980
directorate on particularly
cardiovascular interventions,

01:55:04.980 --> 01:55:08.400
but you've got in the paper
a number of conditions

01:55:08.400 --> 01:55:09.960
and a number of existing interventions,

01:55:09.960 --> 01:55:11.360
diabetes prevention programme,

01:55:12.223 --> 01:55:13.056
talking therapies you mentioned.

01:55:14.443 --> 01:55:15.450
But I just wondered if you give a broader,

01:55:15.450 --> 01:55:17.460
maybe a more granular sense of the balance

01:55:17.460 --> 01:55:22.440
between how much this
is going to be testing

01:55:22.440 --> 01:55:24.739
and evaluating existing interventions

01:55:24.739 --> 01:55:29.739
as you've just said,
testing how we can focus

01:55:29.988 --> 01:55:30.960
those interventions a bit better

01:55:30.960 --> 01:55:34.500
and the economic impact of that

01:55:34.500 --> 01:55:37.923
versus developing and testing
potentially new interventions.

01:55:39.906 --> 01:55:41.010
- And we'll do the normal trick, Adam.

01:55:41.010 --> 01:55:44.373
It's a hard look about
batching up questions, Robert.

01:55:46.163 --> 01:55:48.773
- Yeah, so I think this is
a really terrific programme.

01:55:49.890 --> 01:55:52.470
If I understand you correctly,

01:55:52.470 --> 01:55:54.503
you're going to zone in on

01:55:54.503 --> 01:55:56.886
the people who are off work and sick

01:55:56.886 --> 01:55:58.830
and attempt to get them back into work,

01:55:59.840 --> 01:56:01.045
which of course is totally laudable.

01:56:01.045 --> 01:56:04.329
The ideal of course
would be to prevent them

01:56:04.329 --> 01:56:05.809
getting sick in the first place.

01:56:05.809 --> 01:56:07.320
So this was sort of natural NHS territory

01:56:07.320 --> 01:56:09.510
because it's dealing
with people who are sick

01:56:09.510 --> 01:56:11.160
or secondary prevention.

01:56:11.160 --> 01:56:14.220
The real gain would be primary prevention

01:56:14.220 --> 01:56:16.560
and getting sick in the first place,

01:56:16.560 --> 01:56:20.010
which ICS's should have
as part of their remit

01:56:20.010 --> 01:56:22.650
'cause I'm just curious
to know the balance

01:56:22.650 --> 01:56:25.293
between those two empathies
and your programme.

01:56:26.369 --> 01:56:28.980
- So we'll come back down
the other side for questions,

01:56:28.980 --> 01:56:30.980
but do you want to take those three first?

01:56:32.256 --> 01:56:33.480
- Yes, on children

01:56:33.480 --> 01:56:37.577
and education is the use of interaction,

01:56:37.577 --> 01:56:39.870
especially when it comes
to mental ill health

01:56:39.870 --> 01:56:43.747
between educational
attainment, health outcomes

01:56:43.747 --> 01:56:45.811
and economic outcomes
is very, very strong.

01:56:45.811 --> 01:56:47.370
So there's a real focus on,

01:56:47.370 --> 01:56:48.820
I think in these accelerators

01:56:49.829 --> 01:56:51.835
on young people's mental health.

01:56:51.835 --> 01:56:53.024
There it comes within the broader context

01:56:53.024 --> 01:56:54.900
of other interventions in the white paper,

01:56:54.900 --> 01:56:57.060
these youth trailblazers which are focused

01:56:57.060 --> 01:56:59.430
on what's manifest equipment there,

01:56:59.430 --> 01:57:01.290
we won't in these accelerators

01:57:01.290 --> 01:57:04.807
focus at the very start of life

01:57:04.807 --> 01:57:07.752
because we're trying to
see an economic effect

01:57:07.752 --> 01:57:09.270
sort of within, over the
course of the department.

01:57:09.270 --> 01:57:12.079
But there's definitely kind of more work

01:57:12.079 --> 01:57:13.293
that we're doing there

01:57:13.293 --> 01:57:15.250
and definitely sort of keep talking

01:57:15.250 --> 01:57:16.500
about how we take that forward.

01:57:16.500 --> 01:57:19.710
Steve, on your point on the existing

01:57:19.710 --> 01:57:21.259
versus new interventions.

01:57:21.259 --> 01:57:23.331
So the way we're evaluating it is firstly

01:57:23.331 --> 01:57:24.330
is does the overall approach work?

01:57:24.330 --> 01:57:26.640
And that's something that
we test quite quickly.

01:57:26.640 --> 01:57:30.253
So NAHR and ONHR are partners in this.

01:57:30.253 --> 01:57:32.430
So there's never going to
be a perfectly optimised

01:57:32.430 --> 01:57:34.760
policy package 'cause
the problems are dynamic.

01:57:34.760 --> 01:57:39.450
But we want to test as does
a systems based approach

01:57:39.450 --> 01:57:41.220
and with these sorts of interventions

01:57:41.220 --> 01:57:43.533
make a difference health income outcomes?

01:57:44.501 --> 01:57:46.530
And that will be kind of largely

01:57:46.530 --> 01:57:48.190
things that we are already doing

01:57:49.066 --> 01:57:51.010
where we have good confidence

01:57:51.010 --> 01:57:53.885
that these are going to have
a big health interventions

01:57:53.885 --> 01:57:56.707
and then to test whether
what in terms would be,

01:57:56.707 --> 01:57:59.494
where there is novelty I think is a way

01:57:59.494 --> 01:58:02.351
in which we join up
those services initially.

01:58:02.351 --> 01:58:05.061
So how do you bring
together your mental health

01:58:05.061 --> 01:58:05.894
and your missed case services,

01:58:05.894 --> 01:58:09.456
but also we know from
data, you know, Dutch data

01:58:09.456 --> 01:58:11.670
and we're testing it apart
here that depression, diabetes

01:58:11.670 --> 01:58:15.030
and depression and COPD for
example are two big clusters

01:58:15.030 --> 01:58:18.195
that are particularly sharply increasing

01:58:18.195 --> 01:58:19.389
your probative exit.

01:58:19.389 --> 01:58:22.104
So how do we tackle that
multi-morbidity challenge as well.

01:58:22.104 --> 01:58:24.903
Robert, on on your point, actually,

01:58:25.982 --> 01:58:28.000
the accelerators really focused on those

01:58:28.000 --> 01:58:29.437
who are on the edges of the labour market.

01:58:29.437 --> 01:58:30.720
So those who are already
in the labour market

01:58:30.720 --> 01:58:31.952
as well as those

01:58:31.952 --> 01:58:33.900
who've only really recently
left the labour market,

01:58:33.900 --> 01:58:36.450
try to prevent them from getting so unwell

01:58:36.450 --> 01:58:38.973
that they end up leaving labour market.

01:58:39.967 --> 01:58:41.205
Probably quite a different set

01:58:41.205 --> 01:58:42.399
of interventions you would need

01:58:42.399 --> 01:58:43.920
people who've been out
to work for a long time.

01:58:43.920 --> 01:58:46.794
- Thank you, so there could
be three more questions

01:58:46.794 --> 01:58:48.007
coming down this side.

01:58:48.007 --> 01:58:49.438
One for me, which I don't
mind looking stupid.

01:58:49.438 --> 01:58:54.438
Can you explain to me the correlation

01:58:54.510 --> 01:58:58.140
with the waiting list
and these statistics?

01:58:58.140 --> 01:59:00.141
So how many of these statistics

01:59:00.141 --> 01:59:00.974
are actually on the waiting list

01:59:00.974 --> 01:59:02.223
as an order of magnitude?

01:59:03.405 --> 01:59:04.530
'cause I don't think I know that answer,

01:59:04.530 --> 01:59:07.413
but I don't think it's as
many as is sometimes assumed

01:59:07.413 --> 01:59:08.850
just in external commentary.

01:59:08.850 --> 01:59:11.351
It'd be very helpful to know that.

01:59:11.351 --> 01:59:12.777
And then Jeremy, you were going to.

01:59:12.777 --> 01:59:14.078
- I was going to ask a similar question.

01:59:14.078 --> 01:59:16.184
So when we did the workforce,
10 year workforce planning,

01:59:16.184 --> 01:59:17.383
we did a lot of demand planning

01:59:17.383 --> 01:59:18.589
and one kind of stuff we'd need

01:59:18.589 --> 01:59:20.940
and which areas we'd need the expertise.

01:59:20.940 --> 01:59:23.309
It strikes me it is great looking out

01:59:23.309 --> 01:59:24.943
and looking at what this
might do for the economy,

01:59:24.943 --> 01:59:25.800
but it feels to me
there's a parallel piece

01:59:25.800 --> 01:59:29.910
which is looking in, where are
we spending the 180 billion

01:59:29.910 --> 01:59:32.447
and if we were able to
address these issues,

01:59:32.447 --> 01:59:35.220
it's not productivity,
but it is in a sense,

01:59:35.220 --> 01:59:37.477
how could we have a go at
the 118 billion save money,

01:59:37.477 --> 01:59:40.740
and what that might mean
for our hospital programme.

01:59:40.740 --> 01:59:43.350
What might it mean for
our workforce programme

01:59:43.350 --> 01:59:45.810
and to some extent where
would you prioritise

01:59:45.810 --> 01:59:48.760
to try and manage the cost
internally into the NHS as well?

01:59:49.604 --> 01:59:50.792
So you may have done some of that work,

01:59:50.792 --> 01:59:52.749
but it wasn't so obvious
in the discussion.

01:59:52.749 --> 01:59:53.926
So I think looking out is great,

01:59:53.926 --> 01:59:56.100
but also looking back in as
part of the 10 year programme

01:59:56.100 --> 01:59:58.903
seems to be a way of managing supply

01:59:58.903 --> 02:00:01.200
and demand in what is a
challenging situation.

02:00:01.200 --> 02:00:02.700
- And then Vin think.

02:00:02.700 --> 02:00:04.653
- Yeah I did, firstly Adam,

02:00:06.031 --> 02:00:07.529
thank you for your leadership on this.

02:00:07.529 --> 02:00:08.362
I genuinely think this is
one of the most exciting

02:00:08.362 --> 02:00:10.647
and galvanising things that
we're doing in NHS at the moment

02:00:10.647 --> 02:00:12.473
and I was up in northeast
and north Cumbria

02:00:12.473 --> 02:00:15.737
and there is genuine excitement there.

02:00:15.737 --> 02:00:18.207
Not just in the NHS but
actually in local government

02:00:18.207 --> 02:00:19.230
and in the voluntary sector as well.

02:00:19.230 --> 02:00:20.283
So that's fantastic.

02:00:21.270 --> 02:00:22.380
So when I kind of look back,

02:00:22.380 --> 02:00:25.650
I'm a paediatrician so when
I look back on my career,

02:00:25.650 --> 02:00:28.650
the kind of policy areas
littered with things like this

02:00:28.650 --> 02:00:31.560
where they're evaluated too
soon and then they're abandoned

02:00:31.560 --> 02:00:33.750
and actually when you
look back 10 years later,

02:00:33.750 --> 02:00:36.570
you think maybe we abandoned
that a bit too soon.

02:00:36.570 --> 02:00:38.610
So I just wondered what's
the kind of reasonable

02:00:38.610 --> 02:00:39.990
set of expectations

02:00:39.990 --> 02:00:43.822
because some of these
complex sort of physical

02:00:43.822 --> 02:00:47.220
and social support programmes,
they do need time to bed in

02:00:47.220 --> 02:00:49.230
and I just wonder how
we set our expectations

02:00:49.230 --> 02:00:51.433
so that they're reasonable and make sure

02:00:51.433 --> 02:00:52.649
we keep backing this

02:00:52.649 --> 02:00:54.450
and don't kind of evaluate too soon.

02:00:54.450 --> 02:00:55.740
- So, yes.

02:00:55.740 --> 02:00:57.660
- Those are the last questions on this.

02:00:57.660 --> 02:01:01.350
- So, on the waiting list,
there's a very real impact

02:01:01.350 --> 02:01:04.650
of long waiting on economic activities.

02:01:04.650 --> 02:01:09.650
So that is established
by the ABR last year

02:01:09.800 --> 02:01:11.700
and they published work

02:01:11.700 --> 02:01:16.700
in the report in summer 23,
which had an estimate for those

02:01:18.413 --> 02:01:21.843
who are inactive because of
long, long waiting times.

02:01:22.983 --> 02:01:26.333
There is definitely some
debate over the relative impact

02:01:27.840 --> 02:01:29.523
of waiting times on.

02:01:30.454 --> 02:01:31.744
- [Chair] Number of the
people on the waiting list.

02:01:31.744 --> 02:01:32.946
- Yes.

02:01:32.946 --> 02:01:34.320
- And are they actually
economically because of that.

02:01:34.320 --> 02:01:36.810
- Yes, so we are now
doing more work with ONS

02:01:36.810 --> 02:01:40.597
to match our waiting list
with labour force data

02:01:40.597 --> 02:01:44.870
to get a really much richer
understanding of that as well.

02:01:44.870 --> 02:01:47.013
So we'll have new findings on that,

02:01:47.940 --> 02:01:50.433
on Jeremy, on the impact of demand.

02:01:50.433 --> 02:01:52.893
I think it's really, really important

02:01:52.893 --> 02:01:54.783
and very much on my mind as well.

02:01:54.783 --> 02:01:57.016
The other side of my
role and in particular

02:01:57.016 --> 02:01:59.104
if we're thinking over a
five year time horizon,

02:01:59.104 --> 02:02:01.254
there are some things
that you could do now

02:02:02.250 --> 02:02:03.742
or in the next sort of year or two,

02:02:03.742 --> 02:02:04.973
especially in northeast North Cumbria,

02:02:04.973 --> 02:02:06.990
some of the work they're
doing on the people,

02:02:06.990 --> 02:02:08.400
identify people who are on waiting list,

02:02:08.400 --> 02:02:10.110
what can you do to
prevent them getting worse

02:02:10.110 --> 02:02:13.170
so they don't need more,
don't crash into services

02:02:13.170 --> 02:02:15.360
in future years.

02:02:15.360 --> 02:02:17.250
So that impact and we're sort of thinking

02:02:17.250 --> 02:02:20.470
about how would we assess the impact

02:02:21.351 --> 02:02:22.320
on core performance over time as well.

02:02:22.320 --> 02:02:24.132
So that's definitely.

02:02:24.132 --> 02:02:26.093
- Again it back, sorry,
coming back to investment

02:02:26.093 --> 02:02:28.500
that you might want to
invest there rather than have

02:02:28.500 --> 02:02:31.767
that investment grow
in multiple five times

02:02:31.767 --> 02:02:34.333
and have a much more
expensive intervention

02:02:34.333 --> 02:02:35.525
five years down the line.

02:02:35.525 --> 02:02:36.358
- Yeah, yeah, definitely.

02:02:36.358 --> 02:02:40.075
And Vin, on your point
on evaluating too soon,

02:02:40.075 --> 02:02:45.075
it's really important and we can think

02:02:45.545 --> 02:02:47.130
of any except plenty programmes like this,

02:02:47.130 --> 02:02:49.891
but what we've done is we've done,

02:02:49.891 --> 02:02:51.562
we've built that into
how we've evaluating.

02:02:51.562 --> 02:02:53.040
So we're trying to do

02:02:53.040 --> 02:02:56.970
to show it in 25, 26 as close to real time

02:02:56.970 --> 02:03:01.970
kind of based upon sort of
the NIHR sort of evaluation

02:03:03.333 --> 02:03:05.869
of is the programme as a whole working

02:03:05.869 --> 02:03:07.290
using kind of a series of process metrics.

02:03:07.290 --> 02:03:09.996
But we've also asked them to do with three

02:03:09.996 --> 02:03:11.790
to five year follow up
so we can test in five,

02:03:11.790 --> 02:03:14.138
we'll know the three to five year time,

02:03:14.138 --> 02:03:15.663
how has it worked.

02:03:16.912 --> 02:03:18.150
So we've built multiple
time horizons into it.

02:03:18.150 --> 02:03:20.901
I think the key thing, one
really interesting thing

02:03:20.901 --> 02:03:23.494
that's come out is that
from some of the early work

02:03:23.494 --> 02:03:24.699
on things like bariatric surgery

02:03:24.699 --> 02:03:27.977
which published or talking
therapies and elsewhere,

02:03:27.977 --> 02:03:29.580
is that the effects don't just disappear

02:03:29.580 --> 02:03:34.580
after one year and they really
do endure after some time.

02:03:34.749 --> 02:03:35.790
So that thus the health

02:03:35.790 --> 02:03:38.661
and economic impact is
much more longstanding,

02:03:38.661 --> 02:03:40.050
is often not internalised.

02:03:40.050 --> 02:03:41.913
So that's an important part for us.

02:03:43.583 --> 02:03:44.807
- Adam, thank you.

02:03:44.807 --> 02:03:46.026
Very good discussion.

02:03:46.026 --> 02:03:48.245
I mean it's really important
piece of work actually.

02:03:48.245 --> 02:03:49.563
So thank you.

02:03:50.540 --> 02:03:53.681
Now we've got two more items to go.

02:03:53.681 --> 02:03:55.709
I know we've been going since
nine o'clock this morning,

02:03:55.709 --> 02:03:56.959
but two more items to go.

02:03:57.868 --> 02:04:00.510
So let's move on to the quality strategy

02:04:00.510 --> 02:04:02.940
and I think Steve and Duncan,

02:04:02.940 --> 02:04:04.860
Duncan you're going to lead in.

02:04:04.860 --> 02:04:05.910
- Yeah, so thank you.

02:04:05.910 --> 02:04:07.196
I think this is again still Vin's words

02:04:07.196 --> 02:04:10.620
about galvanising and exciting.

02:04:10.620 --> 02:04:12.785
I think this is another galvanising

02:04:12.785 --> 02:04:14.730
and exciting piece of work
actually as we develop

02:04:14.730 --> 02:04:17.490
the quality strategy for the NHS

02:04:17.490 --> 02:04:19.320
and reflecting quality

02:04:19.320 --> 02:04:23.160
as the organising principle for the work.

02:04:23.160 --> 02:04:25.230
It's our intention to do this alongside

02:04:25.230 --> 02:04:28.076
and parallel with the 10 year plan

02:04:28.076 --> 02:04:30.574
and also the work that
Penny Dash is doing.

02:04:30.574 --> 02:04:32.493
And just to remind the
board at the moment,

02:04:32.493 --> 02:04:34.500
Penny is looking at six organisations

02:04:34.500 --> 02:04:35.940
and how they're working together,

02:04:35.940 --> 02:04:37.860
particularly focused
around quality and safety.

02:04:37.860 --> 02:04:41.723
So Health Watch, the HSSIB,

02:04:42.723 --> 02:04:44.310
CQC, et cetera.

02:04:44.310 --> 02:04:46.470
And so really making sure
that this work aligns

02:04:46.470 --> 02:04:48.060
with any findings out of that

02:04:48.060 --> 02:04:50.520
I think is going to be really important.

02:04:50.520 --> 02:04:54.480
You'll see in the paper
we've set out our intention

02:04:54.480 --> 02:04:55.800
to use the Darzi definition of quality.

02:04:55.800 --> 02:04:58.002
So safe, effective

02:04:58.002 --> 02:05:00.720
and making sure there's
a positive experience.

02:05:00.720 --> 02:05:02.820
And I think reflection

02:05:02.820 --> 02:05:04.350
in some of the discussions we've had today

02:05:04.350 --> 02:05:07.230
about how do we make sure
we give them equal weighting

02:05:07.230 --> 02:05:11.141
in our approach and ensure

02:05:11.141 --> 02:05:12.450
that we focus on all of those areas.

02:05:12.450 --> 02:05:16.050
And also that there is a current

02:05:16.050 --> 02:05:18.120
throughout this around inequalities

02:05:18.120 --> 02:05:19.410
and making sure inequalities

02:05:19.410 --> 02:05:23.460
is a strong theme in the work that we do.

02:05:23.460 --> 02:05:26.070
We've also, in picking
up on the conversation

02:05:26.070 --> 02:05:30.510
we've just had, making
sure value and productivity

02:05:30.510 --> 02:05:35.510
and recognising that
that is part of quality

02:05:35.580 --> 02:05:37.690
and making sure that the effective use

02:05:38.909 --> 02:05:41.430
of every penny is important to quality

02:05:41.430 --> 02:05:44.040
and also access,

02:05:44.040 --> 02:05:48.270
which we've obviously touched
on access today as well.

02:05:48.270 --> 02:05:51.269
So we've set out in the
paper how we'll develop

02:05:51.269 --> 02:05:53.951
the strategy in some core areas,

02:05:53.951 --> 02:05:56.070
particularly we've reflected

02:05:56.070 --> 02:05:58.020
or we will reflect on some of the areas

02:05:58.868 --> 02:06:00.800
that have built up over the years

02:06:00.800 --> 02:06:03.480
in terms of recognition
and reward for quality.

02:06:03.480 --> 02:06:05.790
So using this as an
opportunity to review areas

02:06:05.790 --> 02:06:08.154
such as CQUINs

02:06:08.154 --> 02:06:11.403
or quality counts, how we are using them

02:06:11.403 --> 02:06:13.050
really to drive improvement

02:06:13.050 --> 02:06:15.750
and making sure that they are effective

02:06:15.750 --> 02:06:17.220
still in our approach.

02:06:17.220 --> 02:06:19.130
And getting the balance

02:06:19.130 --> 02:06:20.526
between quality improvement,

02:06:20.526 --> 02:06:22.560
planning, controls and assurance.

02:06:22.560 --> 02:06:25.180
We will work with the
National Quality Board

02:06:25.180 --> 02:06:29.160
on the development of this piece of work

02:06:29.160 --> 02:06:31.170
and also the quality committee.

02:06:31.170 --> 02:06:35.006
I'm sure Robert will come
in at some point about this,

02:06:35.006 --> 02:06:36.450
but we've got this as a standing item

02:06:36.450 --> 02:06:38.800
on the board quality committee as well

02:06:39.646 --> 02:06:41.945
to make sure the board has sight

02:06:41.945 --> 02:06:45.063
and tracking of progress
and ongoing involvement.

02:06:46.088 --> 02:06:47.488
But perhaps I'll hand over to Steve

02:06:47.488 --> 02:06:48.776
if there's anything else
to add to that, Steve?

02:06:48.776 --> 02:06:49.609
- Nothing more to add.

02:06:49.609 --> 02:06:51.603
Very happy to take
questions and discussion.

02:06:52.740 --> 02:06:55.294
- I'm going to throw
it to you Robert first

02:06:55.294 --> 02:06:56.897
to comment if you'd like to comment

02:06:56.897 --> 02:06:58.749
and then you may have
other questions later on

02:06:58.749 --> 02:06:59.957
as we loop around,

02:06:59.957 --> 02:07:00.790
but as you chair the
quality committee first.

02:07:00.790 --> 02:07:03.130
- Sure, no, I mean I think
this is terrific work

02:07:04.272 --> 02:07:08.460
and I think the product
would be really important.

02:07:08.460 --> 02:07:13.460
I guess I'd particularly
emphasise the effectiveness point.

02:07:13.566 --> 02:07:15.257
I think there's great work going on

02:07:15.257 --> 02:07:17.917
about clinical effectiveness in NHSE

02:07:17.917 --> 02:07:21.677
and getting better outcome measures

02:07:21.677 --> 02:07:22.510
and focusing on inequality population

02:07:22.510 --> 02:07:24.723
not from primary care
all that will come out.

02:07:25.616 --> 02:07:27.720
And so I think very supportive.

02:07:27.720 --> 02:07:30.302
- Actually just two points I would add

02:07:30.302 --> 02:07:32.100
and Duncan's hinted at this,

02:07:32.100 --> 02:07:34.770
so clearly there are a number
of building blocks already.

02:07:34.770 --> 02:07:36.816
We have a patient safety strategy

02:07:36.816 --> 02:07:40.018
that is now a number of years old.

02:07:40.018 --> 02:07:43.820
It's developed and there
may be a bit of a refresh

02:07:43.820 --> 02:07:45.026
as part of this.

02:07:45.026 --> 02:07:47.490
We are undertaking a
strategic piece of work,

02:07:47.490 --> 02:07:49.570
a strategic review of our clinical audits

02:07:50.536 --> 02:07:51.369
and registries programme.

02:07:51.369 --> 02:07:53.370
So again, that is another key component,

02:07:53.370 --> 02:07:55.680
particularly on clinical effectiveness.

02:07:55.680 --> 02:07:59.371
So we're not seeking to
duplicate those bits of work.

02:07:59.371 --> 02:08:03.210
There will be a fair amount
of integration of existing

02:08:03.210 --> 02:08:05.650
or ongoing bits of work
but there are some areas

02:08:06.488 --> 02:08:07.680
and patient experience
I think is one of those,

02:08:07.680 --> 02:08:10.920
where we feel that we do
need to do some more work.

02:08:10.920 --> 02:08:13.440
- Okay, so I know Emily had a question.

02:08:13.440 --> 02:08:15.793
Andrew I think has a question, so Emily?

02:08:15.793 --> 02:08:18.357
- So two things.

02:08:18.357 --> 02:08:20.010
The first is it is in the annex,

02:08:20.010 --> 02:08:23.100
but I just wanted to mention

02:08:23.100 --> 02:08:27.380
that the 10 year plan work
stream that I'm leading,

02:08:27.380 --> 02:08:29.718
one of the vision work streams
has as its mission statement,

02:08:29.718 --> 02:08:30.900
I have access to high quality

02:08:30.900 --> 02:08:33.210
and effective care when
and where I need it.

02:08:33.210 --> 02:08:34.043
So we've been working really closely

02:08:34.043 --> 02:08:36.060
with the team to make
sure all of the stuff

02:08:36.060 --> 02:08:37.800
you're doing here is going to be reflected

02:08:37.800 --> 02:08:40.890
as well as obviously
hearing all of the inputs

02:08:40.890 --> 02:08:42.590
from systems and condition groups.

02:08:43.500 --> 02:08:48.032
Second thing I wanted to
pick up was on the table

02:08:48.032 --> 02:08:51.405
where we talk about measuring publishing

02:08:51.405 --> 02:08:53.520
and publishing quality,
which Duncan, you and I

02:08:53.520 --> 02:08:55.760
and Aidan and others have spoken about,

02:08:55.760 --> 02:08:58.270
I'm not saying anything new

02:08:59.349 --> 02:09:01.740
but this is really critical for me

02:09:01.740 --> 02:09:04.620
because the work we've
done to try and get,

02:09:04.620 --> 02:09:07.424
you know, sometimes sponsored by me

02:09:07.424 --> 02:09:09.792
but but done by lots of people
across the organisation,

02:09:09.792 --> 02:09:12.391
particularly Vin's teams, to
make sure we've got actual,

02:09:12.391 --> 02:09:13.612
not just lots of data but
insight into the data.

02:09:13.612 --> 02:09:16.380
And I think we really
struggle with quality

02:09:16.380 --> 02:09:19.950
to know how do we look at this
in a way with the financial

02:09:19.950 --> 02:09:22.470
and the operation data as Jan
was talking about earlier,

02:09:22.470 --> 02:09:25.170
to really, as you said earlier
Duncan, spot the places

02:09:26.121 --> 02:09:27.930
where it's a worry before
it's a big problem.

02:09:27.930 --> 02:09:30.956
And obviously building
that into the work that Jan

02:09:30.956 --> 02:09:32.280
and I spoke about earlier
is really important.

02:09:32.280 --> 02:09:35.310
So I'm really keen to make
sure we're an active part

02:09:35.310 --> 02:09:36.573
of the answer on that.

02:09:37.568 --> 02:09:39.123
- Andrew.

02:09:40.420 --> 02:09:43.290
- So just taking a kind of
productivity lens on this,

02:09:43.290 --> 02:09:47.250
I think may be worth just refreshing

02:09:47.250 --> 02:09:49.533
where we are with clinical pathways.

02:09:50.504 --> 02:09:53.730
So if you look at the top 10
diagnoses in general medicine

02:09:53.730 --> 02:09:58.730
for a typical DGH, we haven't
got a standardised approach

02:09:58.950 --> 02:10:03.150
and I think there's lots of
potential there to develop that.

02:10:03.150 --> 02:10:07.170
So it'd be good if we could
put some focus on that,

02:10:07.170 --> 02:10:09.300
'cause you know, we're trying to create

02:10:09.300 --> 02:10:12.540
a better flow through our system

02:10:12.540 --> 02:10:15.300
and I think trying to get
a standardised approach

02:10:15.300 --> 02:10:17.040
is you know, a way forward.

02:10:17.040 --> 02:10:20.100
It may meet with lots of
resistance from clinicians,

02:10:20.100 --> 02:10:23.542
but, you know, heart attack, stroke,

02:10:23.542 --> 02:10:26.190
we've done some good work on that front,

02:10:26.190 --> 02:10:28.980
but we've got the whole panoply

02:10:28.980 --> 02:10:31.830
of stuff that every A&amp;E sees

02:10:31.830 --> 02:10:33.690
that I think, you know,

02:10:33.690 --> 02:10:34.952
a fresh look at it.

02:10:34.952 --> 02:10:36.202
- Vin and then.

02:10:38.156 --> 02:10:39.978
- Yeah, it just to make a point really,

02:10:39.978 --> 02:10:42.980
so firstly I think the comments

02:10:42.980 --> 02:10:44.390
that Emily's just made about data,

02:10:44.390 --> 02:10:46.710
that Adam's made about data

02:10:46.710 --> 02:10:48.930
and that we've made
throughout this meeting

02:10:48.930 --> 02:10:50.010
show the real importance

02:10:50.010 --> 02:10:52.650
of linking up data sets across the NHS.

02:10:52.650 --> 02:10:55.540
So a few weeks ago a report commissioned

02:10:56.480 --> 02:10:57.657
by Chris Witty, chief medical officer,

02:10:57.657 --> 02:11:00.427
the chief statistician Neil Diamond

02:11:00.427 --> 02:11:02.430
and by Tim Ferris, my predecessor,

02:11:02.430 --> 02:11:04.757
reported on the importance of this

02:11:04.757 --> 02:11:06.420
and called for a national
strategy on data linkage.

02:11:06.420 --> 02:11:09.122
And that will be something
that we'll work up

02:11:09.122 --> 02:11:11.460
in the 10 year pine
group that I'm chairing

02:11:11.460 --> 02:11:14.562
because that is such a
critical part of our future.

02:11:14.562 --> 02:11:15.900
If we take Andrew's example of pathways,

02:11:15.900 --> 02:11:17.250
patients don't just come in

02:11:18.150 --> 02:11:18.983
and out of acute hospitals,

02:11:18.983 --> 02:11:21.210
patients get looked after
in primary secondary care,

02:11:21.210 --> 02:11:23.103
increasingly in social care as well.

02:11:24.070 --> 02:11:25.380
And so I know that's something
that this board supports

02:11:25.380 --> 02:11:27.990
and something we are very
hopeful that our proposals

02:11:27.990 --> 02:11:30.084
will be considered by government.

02:11:30.084 --> 02:11:32.527
- Duncan or Steve, you have a go

02:11:32.527 --> 02:11:35.030
at responding to those questions

02:11:35.030 --> 02:11:36.300
or points, I mean some
statements of support

02:11:36.300 --> 02:11:38.322
really as much questions.

02:11:38.322 --> 02:11:39.536
Do you want to react to any of those?

02:11:39.536 --> 02:11:41.400
- Well, to Andrew's
point, I think I agree,

02:11:41.400 --> 02:11:43.653
we do have, again, components of that.

02:11:44.576 --> 02:11:47.100
So we have the national
optimal stroke imaging pathway,

02:11:47.100 --> 02:11:49.626
in the stroke pathways,

02:11:49.626 --> 02:11:51.333
which is one bit of the stroke pathway.

02:11:51.333 --> 02:11:53.610
But nevertheless it's a
nationally agreed pathway

02:11:53.610 --> 02:11:56.850
and diagnostics imaging
is such a core component

02:11:56.850 --> 02:12:01.850
of getting individuals with
suspected stroke diagnosed

02:12:02.826 --> 02:12:05.247
and then to the right place
for the right treatment.

02:12:05.247 --> 02:12:07.673
So I'm sure that's
something we can take away

02:12:07.673 --> 02:12:09.505
and build on.

02:12:09.505 --> 02:12:10.920
And again, it does link
into productivity discussion

02:12:10.920 --> 02:12:13.860
because I think, I didn't
say it at the time,

02:12:13.860 --> 02:12:17.807
but productivity is often,
you know, thought of,

02:12:17.807 --> 02:12:19.720
often I hear comments

02:12:20.623 --> 02:12:22.635
that clinicians don't like
the word productivity,

02:12:22.635 --> 02:12:23.468
but I think when you get them working

02:12:23.468 --> 02:12:26.340
on best clinical practise, then I think

02:12:26.340 --> 02:12:28.803
that's where productivity
actually comes alive.

02:12:30.366 --> 02:12:35.366
- So if I was to give one
reflection, which is probably,

02:12:36.453 --> 02:12:37.910
and you can just shoot it down,

02:12:37.910 --> 02:12:42.700
it's partly what Penny Dash
is currently looking at

02:12:44.226 --> 02:12:45.960
and part of the response, what Emily said

02:12:45.960 --> 02:12:47.227
about the amount of data we have

02:12:47.227 --> 02:12:50.400
and actually data's fine, but
insights are better I think.

02:12:50.400 --> 02:12:52.011
- Yeah.

02:12:52.011 --> 02:12:53.919
- So I had this trade for a while

02:12:53.919 --> 02:12:58.650
about what we tend to see
when we have safety issues

02:12:58.650 --> 02:13:00.600
and we have investigators,

02:13:00.600 --> 02:13:04.048
we have this longitudinal
approach, don't we,

02:13:04.048 --> 02:13:06.570
to safety regulation,
and safety is clearly one

02:13:06.570 --> 02:13:08.524
of the three components

02:13:08.524 --> 02:13:10.241
of the Darzi definition
quality with which we agree.

02:13:10.241 --> 02:13:12.720
So actually we've got this world

02:13:12.720 --> 02:13:16.320
where we've just added
each time there's review,

02:13:16.320 --> 02:13:18.731
in comes someone very able,

02:13:18.731 --> 02:13:22.170
but they add a whole load
new more requirements,

02:13:22.170 --> 02:13:24.647
definitions, regulations.

02:13:24.647 --> 02:13:29.647
And I take that as a
probably rather glib opinion

02:13:32.940 --> 02:13:35.010
but actually would say
as you look at this,

02:13:35.010 --> 02:13:39.810
it's a very big complex space

02:13:39.810 --> 02:13:44.403
and do you, in the private
sector, you'd sunset a lot,

02:13:45.805 --> 02:13:48.055
you would actually take
out a lot of activity

02:13:49.021 --> 02:13:52.410
that actually in the end is
noise rather than insightful.

02:13:52.410 --> 02:13:54.920
And the question here,
I think when you look

02:13:54.920 --> 02:13:56.130
at the definitions

02:13:56.130 --> 02:13:57.510
that we're going to apply
and the data we use,

02:13:57.510 --> 02:13:59.496
and I don't just mean the safety

02:13:59.496 --> 02:14:01.490
can be in the effective treatment,

02:14:01.490 --> 02:14:03.210
the effectiveness of the interventions,

02:14:03.210 --> 02:14:08.210
are we rigorous enough about
continuously decluttering?

02:14:08.430 --> 02:14:12.050
So actually we can still
manage most effectively

02:14:14.430 --> 02:14:18.179
the data that we're seeing
about how we assess quality.

02:14:18.179 --> 02:14:19.260
'Cause actually, you know, the number

02:14:19.260 --> 02:14:21.540
of different safety regulators

02:14:21.540 --> 02:14:24.536
or recommendations we have,

02:14:24.536 --> 02:14:26.067
just what you had to reduce
in maternity the MSSP

02:14:26.067 --> 02:14:29.232
or you know, that's a real attempt

02:14:29.232 --> 02:14:31.080
to put an umbrella set of requirements

02:14:31.080 --> 02:14:35.340
over multiple investigations
necessarily carried out.

02:14:35.340 --> 02:14:39.300
But trying to bring some sort
of, I'll call it coherence.

02:14:39.300 --> 02:14:41.640
And we do know, don't we,
that when we have incidents,

02:14:41.640 --> 02:14:44.160
which is why I think Penny
is doing this review,

02:14:44.160 --> 02:14:45.930
partly why doing this review,

02:14:45.930 --> 02:14:50.190
one of the pleas we get from
the executives in managerial

02:14:50.190 --> 02:14:51.610
or in clinical position

02:14:52.751 --> 02:14:53.880
actually is if something goes wrong,

02:14:53.880 --> 02:14:56.640
I've suddenly got 30 different people

02:14:56.640 --> 02:15:01.640
asking me for 160 different
pieces of information.

02:15:01.711 --> 02:15:03.660
And so there's something for
me about when we look at this,

02:15:03.660 --> 02:15:05.673
let's not be longitudinal as well,

02:15:06.559 --> 02:15:08.148
let's actually do something

02:15:08.148 --> 02:15:10.200
about sunsetting or simplification.

02:15:10.200 --> 02:15:14.478
So we get at the information
that really matters most.

02:15:14.478 --> 02:15:17.171
That goes to the definition of quality.

02:15:17.171 --> 02:15:18.683
But I dunno if that's.

02:15:18.683 --> 02:15:20.010
- Well yes, I think the
data issue is really complex

02:15:20.010 --> 02:15:22.189
for a whole host of reasons

02:15:22.189 --> 02:15:23.688
and we actually measure a lot of data.

02:15:23.688 --> 02:15:24.521
- Yeah.

02:15:25.789 --> 02:15:27.002
- You could argue too much.

02:15:27.002 --> 02:15:27.957
So the core question is,
which is the core data,

02:15:27.957 --> 02:15:30.481
and we debate this in
the quality committee,

02:15:30.481 --> 02:15:32.160
we have done for years.

02:15:32.160 --> 02:15:34.140
On effectiveness for instance,

02:15:34.140 --> 02:15:36.590
what we are really interested
in is outcome data.

02:15:37.543 --> 02:15:39.779
So in cancer, it's cancer survival data,

02:15:39.779 --> 02:15:40.974
that clearly has a lag on it.

02:15:40.974 --> 02:15:42.540
You're always looking in the rear mirror

02:15:42.540 --> 02:15:45.060
but you can have metrics

02:15:45.060 --> 02:15:47.343
and the face diagnose standard

02:15:47.343 --> 02:15:51.900
and early detection rates are one example

02:15:51.900 --> 02:15:53.959
of where you might be looking

02:15:53.959 --> 02:15:55.824
at some more processing indicators
that are lead indicators

02:15:55.824 --> 02:16:00.480
and you could go through that
for just about everything.

02:16:00.480 --> 02:16:03.916
Of course others have
interest in this area,

02:16:03.916 --> 02:16:05.428
particularly when it comes
to early warning signs

02:16:05.428 --> 02:16:07.770
where things might be going off.

02:16:07.770 --> 02:16:10.669
The CQC have have done this in the past

02:16:10.669 --> 02:16:12.570
around their monitoring systems.

02:16:12.570 --> 02:16:13.800
But it is complex

02:16:13.800 --> 02:16:16.213
and I wouldn't want to give the impression

02:16:16.213 --> 02:16:17.760
that it's an easy thing to do,

02:16:17.760 --> 02:16:19.590
but it is one of the things
that we should look at

02:16:19.590 --> 02:16:21.540
and certainly sunsetting.

02:16:21.540 --> 02:16:23.220
So for instance, the quality accounts,

02:16:23.220 --> 02:16:26.310
are we asking the right things
in the quality accounts?

02:16:26.310 --> 02:16:28.680
Some of that is within statute,

02:16:28.680 --> 02:16:30.450
but I think as Duncan said,

02:16:30.450 --> 02:16:32.836
we should take the opportunity

02:16:32.836 --> 02:16:33.669
to look at all of these things.

02:16:33.669 --> 02:16:36.353
- Very good, okay, are we, Mary?

02:16:38.118 --> 02:16:39.325
- Sorry, I just wanted to say,

02:16:39.325 --> 02:16:42.131
I guess we were asked to comment as well.

02:16:42.131 --> 02:16:44.979
My comment is I think
this will work really well

02:16:44.979 --> 02:16:46.350
'cause it's in such clear English,

02:16:46.350 --> 02:16:48.835
but I didn't really understand how long

02:16:48.835 --> 02:16:51.240
we're going to take to
develop the strategy.

02:16:51.240 --> 02:16:53.070
And my other point is that somewhere,

02:16:53.070 --> 02:16:58.070
I think you talked about
quite a lot of things in it,

02:16:59.551 --> 02:17:02.910
but continuity of care is so central

02:17:02.910 --> 02:17:04.780
and I just would like that

02:17:06.119 --> 02:17:07.488
to come out perhaps a bit more strongly

02:17:07.488 --> 02:17:08.693
in what we've finally developed.

02:17:09.661 --> 02:17:13.996
- I mean completely agree,

02:17:13.996 --> 02:17:16.550
you champion that remind us
all the time of the continuity

02:17:16.550 --> 02:17:18.903
of care and think it's
really important, so.

02:17:19.740 --> 02:17:22.170
- Well I firstly on the
continuity care, absolutely.

02:17:22.170 --> 02:17:25.863
I think that's from the best part of this.

02:17:26.773 --> 02:17:27.606
And actually if you look at,

02:17:27.606 --> 02:17:30.537
and we need to lift
the experience element,

02:17:30.537 --> 02:17:31.440
I think that would talk to that as well.

02:17:31.440 --> 02:17:32.850
In terms of the timeline,

02:17:32.850 --> 02:17:37.560
obviously we are working
parallel with the 10 year plan

02:17:37.560 --> 02:17:39.570
and then some of the
timelines around that as well.

02:17:39.570 --> 02:17:42.573
So there will be a significant amount

02:17:42.573 --> 02:17:45.072
of work done in the
early part of next year

02:17:45.072 --> 02:17:46.560
to progress this.

02:17:46.560 --> 02:17:50.590
And then obviously the
alignment with the work

02:17:51.431 --> 02:17:53.260
that we're also doing on with Dash

02:17:54.178 --> 02:17:56.243
and then obviously the
operating framework as well.

02:17:57.505 --> 02:18:00.466
- Yes, I think there's always a temptation

02:18:00.466 --> 02:18:01.380
with quality because it covers everything

02:18:01.380 --> 02:18:02.913
to try and put everything in.

02:18:03.938 --> 02:18:05.514
So I think one of the things we will need

02:18:05.514 --> 02:18:07.213
to be is quite rigorous

02:18:07.213 --> 02:18:09.690
and try and make sure
it's a focus strategy.

02:18:09.690 --> 02:18:11.550
The second thing I was
just going to mention

02:18:11.550 --> 02:18:14.550
because it's in the
paper, was we will do this

02:18:14.550 --> 02:18:16.650
with the National Quality Board.

02:18:16.650 --> 02:18:18.719
It's worth saying something

02:18:18.719 --> 02:18:19.946
about the National Quality Board.

02:18:19.946 --> 02:18:24.180
The NQB has existed since
pre NHS England time.

02:18:24.180 --> 02:18:26.650
It originated in the Department of Health

02:18:27.484 --> 02:18:29.437
and it is still in essence

02:18:29.437 --> 02:18:30.660
sponsored by the Department of Health.

02:18:30.660 --> 02:18:34.056
It now broadly acts as a forum

02:18:34.056 --> 02:18:35.775
to bring together arms length bodies

02:18:35.775 --> 02:18:37.299
and regulators, mostly clinical leaders

02:18:37.299 --> 02:18:39.900
but not exclusively to
discuss issues around quality.

02:18:39.900 --> 02:18:42.580
To bring quality issues
that they're dealing with

02:18:43.537 --> 02:18:45.690
in their own organisation
for information and comment.

02:18:45.690 --> 02:18:50.232
And it does occasionally from
time to time publish guidance.

02:18:50.232 --> 02:18:52.935
It published some guidance
yesterday on risk management

02:18:52.935 --> 02:18:54.690
in integrated care boards for example.

02:18:54.690 --> 02:18:56.880
It is not hugely resourced

02:18:56.880 --> 02:18:59.730
and it doesn't have a huge amount

02:18:59.730 --> 02:19:03.930
of authority within the system,
but it is a useful board.

02:19:03.930 --> 02:19:05.100
I know it is one of the things

02:19:05.100 --> 02:19:07.691
that Penny will be
looking at in her review.

02:19:07.691 --> 02:19:10.620
And she came to the National Quality Board

02:19:10.620 --> 02:19:12.837
just a couple of weeks
ago at the last meeting.

02:19:12.837 --> 02:19:17.398
So whether we see any
evolution of the NQB,

02:19:17.398 --> 02:19:19.113
we will have to wait and see.

02:19:19.113 --> 02:19:22.380
But it is important I think
that we do the work with NQB.

02:19:22.380 --> 02:19:24.864
- Amanda, do you want to make a comment?

02:19:24.864 --> 02:19:26.422
- Actually I was going
to say exactly what Steve

02:19:26.422 --> 02:19:27.805
has just said

02:19:27.805 --> 02:19:29.343
'cause I just thought
it was really important

02:19:29.343 --> 02:19:31.028
to pull out the fact that this is not

02:19:31.028 --> 02:19:33.096
a unilateral NHS England piece of work.

02:19:33.096 --> 02:19:34.910
This is very much a, I mean,

02:19:34.910 --> 02:19:36.120
and again thank you to Steve and Duncan

02:19:36.120 --> 02:19:38.100
and others for the leadership on this,

02:19:38.100 --> 02:19:40.834
but it's really important that it does

02:19:40.834 --> 02:19:45.270
go through the NQB so that it does reflect

02:19:45.270 --> 02:19:49.560
the combined views of all of
the really important partners

02:19:49.560 --> 02:19:51.030
that work in this space.

02:19:51.030 --> 02:19:55.290
So I think that that, you
brought that to life Steve,

02:19:55.290 --> 02:19:58.671
but it is really important forum

02:19:58.671 --> 02:19:59.610
because actually the authority

02:19:59.610 --> 02:20:01.740
of the individual organisations

02:20:01.740 --> 02:20:03.480
represented there is immense.

02:20:03.480 --> 02:20:05.844
And if we're talking about something

02:20:05.844 --> 02:20:07.897
that is going to bind together

02:20:07.897 --> 02:20:11.160
the whole of the landscape around quality,

02:20:11.160 --> 02:20:14.263
then we can't do that without the NQBs

02:20:14.263 --> 02:20:17.130
explicit involvement and endorsement.

02:20:17.130 --> 02:20:20.080
Exactly the same point
goes for the 10 year plan

02:20:21.135 --> 02:20:22.341
and the importance of making sure

02:20:22.341 --> 02:20:23.303
it's just knitted through that.

02:20:24.193 --> 02:20:26.055
And again, you've rightly
referenced the importance

02:20:26.055 --> 02:20:27.480
of alignment with Penny Dash's work

02:20:27.480 --> 02:20:30.040
and I think it's a really timely thing

02:20:30.881 --> 02:20:31.860
given that those two things are happening.

02:20:31.860 --> 02:20:35.466
Penny's work and the 10 year
plan for you to be leading this

02:20:35.466 --> 02:20:38.490
so that it's there as a
proactive input, thank you.

02:20:38.490 --> 02:20:41.993
- Okay, let's go, thank
you Duncan, thanks Steve.

02:20:41.993 --> 02:20:45.313
Thank you Robert for chairing
the quality committee.

02:20:45.313 --> 02:20:47.850
John, should we go to the last?

02:20:48.719 --> 02:20:51.836
- Yes and actually, and thank
you for Robert's leadership.

02:20:51.836 --> 02:20:52.830
We should say that 'cause I
think that has really injected

02:20:52.830 --> 02:20:54.520
some pace it has and some energy

02:20:55.359 --> 02:20:56.730
into our internal governance,

02:20:56.730 --> 02:20:58.710
which again is hugely important.

02:20:58.710 --> 02:21:03.393
- Very important, so John going to,

02:21:04.558 --> 02:21:05.970
it's really important
commissioning integration.

02:21:05.970 --> 02:21:09.813
As a topic delegation of
specialised commissioning to ICBs.

02:21:10.982 --> 02:21:12.193
- [John] Great, thank you.

02:21:12.193 --> 02:21:13.387
- Over to you.

02:21:13.387 --> 02:21:14.582
- Thank you chair.

02:21:14.582 --> 02:21:15.783
So John Stewart, national director

02:21:15.783 --> 02:21:17.006
for specialised commissioning.

02:21:17.006 --> 02:21:18.217
So the purpose of the paper really

02:21:18.217 --> 02:21:20.220
is to seek board approval to
proceed with the delegation

02:21:20.220 --> 02:21:24.360
of commissioning responsibility
for 70 specialised services

02:21:24.360 --> 02:21:27.993
to all ICBs across the
country from April 25.

02:21:28.836 --> 02:21:30.180
And I think it's probably worth
saying right from the outset

02:21:30.180 --> 02:21:32.130
that there are around 100 services

02:21:32.130 --> 02:21:33.990
within the portfolio,

02:21:33.990 --> 02:21:36.480
including all highly specialised services

02:21:36.480 --> 02:21:39.697
that are not considered
suitable for delegation

02:21:39.697 --> 02:21:41.700
and will be retained by NHS England

02:21:41.700 --> 02:21:46.110
and directly commissioned by
NHS England going forward.

02:21:46.110 --> 02:21:48.660
Just as a reminder, the
board has considered this

02:21:48.660 --> 02:21:52.210
commissioning reform agenda
on two occasions actually

02:21:53.115 --> 02:21:53.948
in the last 12 months.

02:21:53.948 --> 02:21:57.330
So last December the board
approved the full delegation

02:21:57.330 --> 02:22:00.288
of commissioning
responsibility at that point

02:22:00.288 --> 02:22:04.110
for 59 services to all
ICBs across the Midlands,

02:22:04.110 --> 02:22:07.700
east of England and the northwest regions

02:22:07.700 --> 02:22:08.820
from April of this year.

02:22:08.820 --> 02:22:11.398
ICBs and all those other remaining regions

02:22:11.398 --> 02:22:14.110
continued with statutory joint

02:22:15.643 --> 02:22:16.830
commissioning responsibilities
with NHS England

02:22:16.830 --> 02:22:21.028
in preparation for full
delegation from April 25.

02:22:21.028 --> 02:22:22.320
So this coming April.

02:22:22.320 --> 02:22:25.680
And then again in March, the
board considered this topic

02:22:25.680 --> 02:22:28.560
and approved a final list
of services for delegation.

02:22:28.560 --> 02:22:30.390
And that slightly grew from the 59

02:22:30.390 --> 02:22:32.520
that have been delegated this year

02:22:32.520 --> 02:22:35.854
to a final list of 70 services.

02:22:35.854 --> 02:22:38.280
So I think we're clear
now on which services

02:22:38.280 --> 02:22:40.942
are being delegated and which services

02:22:40.942 --> 02:22:43.110
are going to be retained by NHS England.

02:22:43.110 --> 02:22:45.480
I think since then it's
probably just worth highlighting

02:22:45.480 --> 02:22:47.430
a number of developments
that I think further

02:22:47.430 --> 02:22:50.220
kind of reinforce the
importance of these changes.

02:22:50.220 --> 02:22:53.640
And the first of those I
think is for new government

02:22:53.640 --> 02:22:55.620
setting a clear reform agenda

02:22:55.620 --> 02:22:58.560
focused on achieving three big shifts.

02:22:58.560 --> 02:23:00.750
The shift from sickness to prevention,

02:23:00.750 --> 02:23:01.800
hospital to community,

02:23:01.800 --> 02:23:04.530
and of course shift from
analogue to digital.

02:23:04.530 --> 02:23:06.270
And of course a key driver

02:23:06.270 --> 02:23:09.180
of delegating specialised services

02:23:09.180 --> 02:23:12.570
has always been about
enabling this left shift

02:23:12.570 --> 02:23:14.640
through really aligning incentives

02:23:14.640 --> 02:23:17.730
to deliver more upstream interventions.

02:23:17.730 --> 02:23:20.250
Secondly, we then had the publication

02:23:20.250 --> 02:23:23.355
of Lord Darzi's investigation of the NHS.

02:23:23.355 --> 02:23:26.550
And I think his report
really just again underscores

02:23:26.550 --> 02:23:29.580
the importance of delivering
that lift left shift.

02:23:29.580 --> 02:23:31.410
And I highlighted it in the paper,

02:23:31.410 --> 02:23:34.356
but you know, Lord Darzi does note

02:23:34.356 --> 02:23:36.660
how specialised services are accounting

02:23:36.660 --> 02:23:40.770
for a growing proportion
of the overall NHS budget.

02:23:40.770 --> 02:23:44.406
And we absolutely understand
why that is the case.

02:23:44.406 --> 02:23:47.076
And it is largely driven by our ability

02:23:47.076 --> 02:23:51.030
to, you know, provide new
treatments for conditions

02:23:51.030 --> 02:23:53.610
and other diseases that we
previously couldn't treat.

02:23:53.610 --> 02:23:55.440
And that is very welcome.

02:23:55.440 --> 02:23:58.629
But it's also, he describes creating

02:23:58.629 --> 02:24:00.025
a kind of inexorable pressure on costs,

02:24:00.025 --> 02:24:02.520
hence again, the need to focus

02:24:02.520 --> 02:24:06.630
on prevention upstream
interventions going forward.

02:24:06.630 --> 02:24:08.340
I think it's also worth just reflecting

02:24:08.340 --> 02:24:11.070
on the previous discussion we had as well

02:24:11.070 --> 02:24:13.950
and Lord Darzi's investigation,
kind of emphasising

02:24:13.950 --> 02:24:16.200
the importance of making sure that we have

02:24:16.200 --> 02:24:18.870
that clear framework for quality

02:24:18.870 --> 02:24:22.920
as we, you know, develop all of these,

02:24:22.920 --> 02:24:24.630
you know, reform proposals.

02:24:24.630 --> 02:24:26.850
And I think that's precisely why

02:24:26.850 --> 02:24:29.670
delegation is actually
taking place in the context

02:24:29.670 --> 02:24:33.762
of an ongoing framework of
nationally set standards,

02:24:33.762 --> 02:24:37.830
service specifications and
clinical access policies.

02:24:37.830 --> 02:24:40.650
So even for those
services that we delegate,

02:24:40.650 --> 02:24:43.200
NHS England remains the
accountable commissioner.

02:24:43.200 --> 02:24:46.520
And so we will continue to
provide kind of that clarity

02:24:46.520 --> 02:24:49.817
around what good high
quality care looks like

02:24:49.817 --> 02:24:51.180
that Lord Darzi called for.

02:24:51.180 --> 02:24:53.820
Similarly, we will continue
to support the development

02:24:53.820 --> 02:24:57.520
of nationally defined
metrics, quality dashboards

02:24:58.821 --> 02:25:01.680
to support improvement,
oversight and assurance.

02:25:01.680 --> 02:25:04.770
We will provide ongoing
national clinical leadership

02:25:04.770 --> 02:25:06.853
across the entire portfolio

02:25:06.853 --> 02:25:10.764
through our clinical reference
group infrastructure.

02:25:10.764 --> 02:25:15.060
And you know, finally I think, you know,

02:25:15.060 --> 02:25:17.271
we within NHS England will continue

02:25:17.271 --> 02:25:18.660
to have a really important
role in this space

02:25:18.660 --> 02:25:20.700
in supporting kind of
the latest innovations

02:25:20.700 --> 02:25:23.080
working with NICE, with partners

02:25:23.953 --> 02:25:25.329
through organisations

02:25:25.329 --> 02:25:27.427
like the Accelerated Access Collaborative

02:25:27.427 --> 02:25:29.010
and others to really
drive kind of innovation

02:25:29.010 --> 02:25:30.663
across the whole portfolio.

02:25:31.500 --> 02:25:34.320
In terms of delegation this
year, I think it'd be fair

02:25:34.320 --> 02:25:36.931
to say that you know,

02:25:36.931 --> 02:25:39.360
in those regions we've
delegated responsibility,

02:25:39.360 --> 02:25:41.850
it has gone very smoothly,

02:25:41.850 --> 02:25:45.027
including all the kind of
complex technical aspects

02:25:45.027 --> 02:25:47.160
like making sure that cash is flowing

02:25:47.160 --> 02:25:49.410
in the right way, really important.

02:25:49.410 --> 02:25:52.020
But I think you'll see in
the paper at paragraph 10

02:25:52.020 --> 02:25:55.590
that we've also highlighted
just some of the early areas

02:25:55.590 --> 02:25:58.909
of focus and improvement
that those are ICBs

02:25:58.909 --> 02:26:01.195
and those regions are
really coming together

02:26:01.195 --> 02:26:03.241
to really target and try
and drive improvement.

02:26:03.241 --> 02:26:07.369
Those ICBs in the remaining four regions

02:26:07.369 --> 02:26:09.760
who are yet to go with full delegation

02:26:09.760 --> 02:26:11.190
have obviously been working
very closely together

02:26:11.190 --> 02:26:14.358
and with our regional teams to make sure

02:26:14.358 --> 02:26:15.560
that they are ready

02:26:15.560 --> 02:26:16.890
to take on full delegated responsibility.

02:26:16.890 --> 02:26:20.753
We held a national moderation
panel back in October

02:26:20.753 --> 02:26:23.310
to review those plans,
which we were assured

02:26:23.310 --> 02:26:27.709
were in kind of a good state
to proceed with these plans

02:26:27.709 --> 02:26:30.963
to delegate the entire
suitable portfolio from April.

02:26:30.963 --> 02:26:32.710
They were further considered

02:26:33.648 --> 02:26:36.690
by the NHS England executive
group at the end of October.

02:26:36.690 --> 02:26:38.760
And I think the only
kind of recommendation

02:26:38.760 --> 02:26:42.510
coming from that discussion
was the importance

02:26:42.510 --> 02:26:45.790
of probably applying
a consistent condition

02:26:46.668 --> 02:26:49.950
around delegation for those
ICBs that are in segments three

02:26:49.950 --> 02:26:52.800
or four of the NHS oversight framework

02:26:52.800 --> 02:26:57.150
and that, you know, requires
regional NHS England approval

02:26:57.150 --> 02:26:59.974
before kind of any significant changes

02:26:59.974 --> 02:27:02.580
in kind of a specialised
allocation are made.

02:27:02.580 --> 02:27:06.624
So in summary, we're
looking for board support

02:27:06.624 --> 02:27:09.240
to proceed with delegation
for those services

02:27:09.240 --> 02:27:11.610
to all ICBs from April,

02:27:11.610 --> 02:27:16.610
including giving delegated
authority to regional directors

02:27:16.950 --> 02:27:19.560
to sign the actual delegation agreement

02:27:19.560 --> 02:27:22.770
and approve that condition
that I just referred to

02:27:22.770 --> 02:27:27.183
around those ICBs in
segment three and four.

02:27:28.502 --> 02:27:31.320
- So comments or questions, Jeremy?

02:27:31.320 --> 02:27:34.969
- Just a conversation we had at yesterday.

02:27:34.969 --> 02:27:36.741
So I was just going to flag Steve,

02:27:36.741 --> 02:27:38.245
the conversation we're having

02:27:38.245 --> 02:27:39.180
around a slightly philosophical

02:27:39.180 --> 02:27:41.607
or existential conversation
around how do we,

02:27:42.505 --> 02:27:43.710
in NHS England check this is going okay

02:27:43.710 --> 02:27:47.270
from a governance point of
view, first, second, third line

02:27:47.270 --> 02:27:49.220
of defence and how we
make, get the link up

02:27:49.220 --> 02:27:51.390
or the assurance that it's working through

02:27:51.390 --> 02:27:53.635
without being overly bureaucratic.

02:27:53.635 --> 02:27:55.369
So I just flagged that that
conversation is going ahead

02:27:55.369 --> 02:27:56.568
so we get that in place

02:27:56.568 --> 02:27:57.473
before the next financial year.

02:27:58.590 --> 02:27:59.848
- Mary.

02:27:59.848 --> 02:28:01.593
- I actually think you might
need to keep an eye on the fact

02:28:01.593 --> 02:28:03.843
that the cost of
commissioning doesn't go up.

02:28:08.202 --> 02:28:10.110
- Who wants to come in?

02:28:10.110 --> 02:28:12.480
- So these budgets historically

02:28:12.480 --> 02:28:14.283
have been overspent from memory.

02:28:15.118 --> 02:28:17.737
- Are you in charge of
specialised commission?

02:28:17.737 --> 02:28:20.057
- I am and there has been no overspend

02:28:20.057 --> 02:28:21.640
in the specialised budget

02:28:22.598 --> 02:28:25.293
for I think at least nine, 10 years.

02:28:26.494 --> 02:28:28.452
- Oh wow, oh wow.

02:28:28.452 --> 02:28:32.796
All right, wow, that's surprising.

02:28:32.796 --> 02:28:34.546
Well done, well done.

02:28:37.150 --> 02:28:38.345
- Was that your point?

02:28:38.345 --> 02:28:39.338
Have you been disempowered by that?

02:28:39.338 --> 02:28:40.171
- Absolutely.

02:28:41.801 --> 02:28:43.634
- Good response, John.

02:28:44.593 --> 02:28:46.589
- Can I, oh sorry.

02:28:46.589 --> 02:28:50.748
- I just want to reinforce,
make three points.

02:28:50.748 --> 02:28:53.301
One is, I think it's
really important this,

02:28:53.301 --> 02:28:54.929
it's about devolution

02:28:54.929 --> 02:28:56.137
and delegation to the ICBs,

02:28:56.137 --> 02:28:58.560
and it's really, really important

02:28:58.560 --> 02:29:00.760
that they are given as much authority

02:29:01.705 --> 02:29:02.843
close to their patients as they can.

02:29:04.053 --> 02:29:05.520
And clearly then to Jeremy's
point, it's important though

02:29:05.520 --> 02:29:07.794
that there is some tracking

02:29:07.794 --> 02:29:11.699
because particularly in the
world of financial pressure,

02:29:11.699 --> 02:29:15.280
actually we just need to
keep sight of the fact

02:29:16.235 --> 02:29:17.980
that this is equitable decisioning

02:29:18.977 --> 02:29:20.580
or whatever one we'll call it.

02:29:20.580 --> 02:29:22.090
- The tracking performance.

02:29:22.090 --> 02:29:24.480
But also is it being governed properly?

02:29:24.480 --> 02:29:26.370
That's a bit debate and probably
the tracking performance

02:29:26.370 --> 02:29:29.761
probably sits here and then
the governance around it

02:29:29.761 --> 02:29:31.320
probably sits at but both of those.

02:29:31.320 --> 02:29:33.470
- Yeah because actually
there is this phase

02:29:34.671 --> 02:29:35.640
that which the ICB becomes responsible,

02:29:35.640 --> 02:29:39.840
but NHS signal remains
accountable, which is interesting.

02:29:39.840 --> 02:29:42.000
So that governance and
tracking is really important.

02:29:42.000 --> 02:29:44.830
I would then make one sort of odd point,

02:29:44.830 --> 02:29:48.833
which is I've rarely
seen a contract like it

02:29:48.833 --> 02:29:53.060
and so I've seen lots

02:29:53.060 --> 02:29:56.013
of contracts in my time and
I can't, in all honesty,

02:29:57.057 --> 02:29:58.417
and it's for the minutes if you like,

02:29:58.417 --> 02:29:59.333
I haven't read every word of it,

02:30:00.409 --> 02:30:03.540
but my goodness, is this really
what we need as a contract

02:30:03.540 --> 02:30:07.345
to actually do specialised
commissioning services?

02:30:07.345 --> 02:30:11.604
I mean the lawyers haven't gone mad,

02:30:11.604 --> 02:30:14.580
but they've been allowed
to run wild, are they not?

02:30:17.965 --> 02:30:22.965
- The delegation agreement
has been developed

02:30:23.136 --> 02:30:24.510
in partnership with
regional colleagues ICBs.

02:30:24.510 --> 02:30:27.505
So I think people are
comfortable with that.

02:30:27.505 --> 02:30:29.641
And it was for the agreement that was used

02:30:29.641 --> 02:30:31.681
and maybe slightly
updated when we delegated

02:30:31.681 --> 02:30:36.681
to those ICBs this year, I
think it follows the same kind

02:30:37.352 --> 02:30:39.000
of delegation agreement that was used,

02:30:39.000 --> 02:30:43.620
and Steve will clarify
this, but for primary care

02:30:43.620 --> 02:30:46.750
and other direct commissioning functions

02:30:47.596 --> 02:30:48.950
with some modifications.

02:30:48.950 --> 02:30:50.158
But I take your point,

02:30:50.158 --> 02:30:51.780
it's quite a long agreement.

02:30:51.780 --> 02:30:52.967
- We know the primary care contract

02:30:52.967 --> 02:30:55.440
is about 1000 pages long, you know.

02:30:56.809 --> 02:30:57.999
- So it's short.

02:30:57.999 --> 02:31:01.350
- It's short, I dunno
if I make your point.

02:31:01.350 --> 02:31:03.596
I don't think it's a useful point.

02:31:03.596 --> 02:31:04.429
It's just an observation.

02:31:04.429 --> 02:31:06.180
But I would do wonder whether actually

02:31:06.180 --> 02:31:08.470
there's a better and simpler approach

02:31:09.528 --> 02:31:11.820
rather than every
possible legal definition

02:31:11.820 --> 02:31:15.483
being thrown into a
commissioning contract actually.

02:31:16.598 --> 02:31:17.799
But anyway.

02:31:17.799 --> 02:31:18.632
- I've got a question if I may.

02:31:18.632 --> 02:31:20.883
I just want to build on the question

02:31:20.883 --> 02:31:24.180
that Jeremy asked about
governance and oversight

02:31:24.180 --> 02:31:26.956
and it's from just a
slightly different angle.

02:31:26.956 --> 02:31:28.980
So we know that a lot of the partners

02:31:28.980 --> 02:31:32.243
we work with who represent the interests

02:31:32.243 --> 02:31:34.521
of particular groups are anxious

02:31:34.521 --> 02:31:35.850
about the devolution of
specialised commissioning

02:31:35.850 --> 02:31:38.280
because at the moment, some of them feel

02:31:38.280 --> 02:31:42.530
like they have a central person
they can come and talk to

02:31:42.530 --> 02:31:45.486
and if they're worried
about services, the quality

02:31:45.486 --> 02:31:47.520
of services, access to
services, provision of services,

02:31:47.520 --> 02:31:50.610
there's a way in to the NHS
to have that conversation

02:31:50.610 --> 02:31:54.486
and that once it's
devolved out to 42 ICBs,

02:31:54.486 --> 02:31:55.699
that will be harder.

02:31:55.699 --> 02:31:57.171
- They can always read the contract.

02:31:57.171 --> 02:31:59.053
- Well, thank you Richard.

02:31:59.053 --> 02:32:01.881
I know you have made provision for that

02:32:01.881 --> 02:32:04.953
in your point here on point 17B,

02:32:05.865 --> 02:32:07.136
where you've talked about the fact

02:32:07.136 --> 02:32:08.160
that there are very clear policies

02:32:08.160 --> 02:32:11.979
and standards that will be
expected to be followed.

02:32:11.979 --> 02:32:12.812
But I just wonder if I could ask you

02:32:12.812 --> 02:32:15.710
or invite you to say what you would say

02:32:15.710 --> 02:32:19.560
to give confidence to those organisations

02:32:19.560 --> 02:32:22.729
that we know are anxious
on behalf of the patients

02:32:22.729 --> 02:32:24.840
that they represent, that
this isn't going to lead

02:32:24.840 --> 02:32:27.960
to a lack of opportunity to influence

02:32:27.960 --> 02:32:30.066
or a lack of opportunity to see

02:32:30.066 --> 02:32:33.933
consistent standards
actually applied in practise.

02:32:35.839 --> 02:32:37.627
- Yeah, no a really good point.

02:32:37.627 --> 02:32:39.962
And I think there are
mixed views out there

02:32:39.962 --> 02:32:40.795
amongst both kind of clinicians

02:32:40.795 --> 02:32:43.320
and patient groups around, you know,

02:32:43.320 --> 02:32:46.450
the benefits of delegation, you know,

02:32:46.450 --> 02:32:48.660
and I think that that is recognised.

02:32:48.660 --> 02:32:50.641
I think the most important thing

02:32:50.641 --> 02:32:52.140
to say though is this
point about NHS England

02:32:52.140 --> 02:32:53.790
remaining the accountable commissioner,

02:32:53.790 --> 02:32:55.230
what we're not doing here

02:32:55.230 --> 02:32:58.281
is we are not de-listing these services.

02:32:58.281 --> 02:32:59.640
So these services remain specialised.

02:32:59.640 --> 02:33:01.110
In fact it is actually the Secretary

02:33:01.110 --> 02:33:03.000
of State rather than NHS England

02:33:03.000 --> 02:33:06.374
who specifies which
services are specialised.

02:33:06.374 --> 02:33:09.336
And so there are no
change in the portfolio

02:33:09.336 --> 02:33:11.940
but what it means being the
accountable commissioner

02:33:11.940 --> 02:33:13.980
is we do have that ability to continue

02:33:13.980 --> 02:33:15.690
to set the national standards.

02:33:15.690 --> 02:33:17.040
It means that there will continue

02:33:17.040 --> 02:33:18.630
to be people sitting nationally

02:33:18.630 --> 02:33:22.470
where patient groups can
come to and raise concerns

02:33:22.470 --> 02:33:24.480
or talk about, you know, opportunities

02:33:24.480 --> 02:33:26.520
for improving very much as they do now

02:33:26.520 --> 02:33:29.558
on a kind of daily basis with my team.

02:33:29.558 --> 02:33:31.885
So I think that is really important

02:33:31.885 --> 02:33:36.360
and I think the oversight
and insurance bit is critical

02:33:36.360 --> 02:33:39.900
and you know, we are working
with our regional colleagues

02:33:39.900 --> 02:33:41.520
and Emily's directorate to make sure

02:33:41.520 --> 02:33:45.390
that specialised services is
not kind of looked at in a silo

02:33:45.390 --> 02:33:46.860
but are fully integrated

02:33:46.860 --> 02:33:50.067
into the kind of overall
oversight and assurance framework.

02:33:50.067 --> 02:33:53.580
And I think we've got a
kind of good starting point

02:33:53.580 --> 02:33:56.670
for that because of the, you
know, standards, metrics,

02:33:56.670 --> 02:33:58.560
dashboards that we already have.

02:33:58.560 --> 02:34:00.900
What we've tried to do
within specialised services

02:34:00.900 --> 02:34:02.760
is almost mirror that kind of framework

02:34:02.760 --> 02:34:04.800
that Lord Darzi set out

02:34:04.800 --> 02:34:07.233
and we don't intend to
lose any aspect of that.

02:34:07.233 --> 02:34:10.140
- Very good, any other
comments or questions?

02:34:10.140 --> 02:34:12.476
Happy to support, very good.

02:34:12.476 --> 02:34:14.673
Very happy to support,
thank you very much.

02:34:15.960 --> 02:34:18.630
So I think that brings to the end

02:34:18.630 --> 02:34:21.169
of the, no it doesn't.

02:34:21.169 --> 02:34:24.150
We have one item, stared
item for information,

02:34:24.150 --> 02:34:27.060
which is a direct directions
of mandatory requests

02:34:27.060 --> 02:34:30.180
summary of the national
major trauma registry

02:34:30.180 --> 02:34:31.743
NMTR for NHS Wales.

02:34:32.580 --> 02:34:35.645
Requests 2024, which issued to NHS England

02:34:35.645 --> 02:34:38.623
by the Digital Healthcare
Wales in October, 2024.

02:34:38.623 --> 02:34:39.456
It's for your information

02:34:39.456 --> 02:34:41.850
and the board sent me to
note the mandatory request

02:34:41.850 --> 02:34:44.150
issued to NHS England
in October of this year.

02:34:45.494 --> 02:34:46.735
Noted, very good.

02:34:46.735 --> 02:34:48.842
Thank you very much.

02:34:48.842 --> 02:34:51.900
So can I just then see if
there's any other business

02:34:51.900 --> 02:34:53.220
and it has been a long day,

02:34:53.220 --> 02:34:55.418
but that isn't to put you off.

02:34:55.418 --> 02:34:57.007
If there is any other business

02:34:57.007 --> 02:34:59.170
just going around the table,

02:34:59.170 --> 02:35:02.290
just looking around the
table, any other business?

02:35:02.290 --> 02:35:04.424
In which case can I
thank all of you actually

02:35:04.424 --> 02:35:07.020
for your diligence and commitment

02:35:07.020 --> 02:35:10.094
through what's been a
very long but good day.

02:35:10.094 --> 02:35:12.270
I mean, I think the discussions
we had in the morning,

02:35:12.270 --> 02:35:15.000
in addition to the topics
we've covered this afternoon

02:35:15.000 --> 02:35:17.792
